             "name": "Andhra Pradesh- Handicrafts- Bidri/Inlay on Metal",
             "desc": "The craft of Bidri or inlay on metal originated in Persia. It has motifs of leaves and floral designs of Persian and Mughal art. It was Sultan Ahmad Shah Wali who introduced this craft to India and he and the Nizam of Hyderabad made this craft most popular. Bidriware is a silver inlay work where designs in pure silver are inlaid in an alloy of zinc and copper. Though the original home of Bidri is Bidar in Karnataka, it is perhaps one of the most popular crafts of Andhra. The technique of bidri came to India from Iran (Persia) in the 14th century during the mughal reign when swords and other weapons were decorated with Bidriwork. The designs are drawn free hand using sharp chisels. The base metal (black) is a combination of zinc with copper which are gently heated together. The complex process of Bidriware includes casting, engraving, inlaying and polishing. The items are first cast in moulds made from clay, sand, resin and oil. A simple chisel, of varied shapes and sizes, is then used to engrave the design. Silver metallic wire is then laid in the engraved area. The item is then cleaned and polished to be finally put in copper sulphate solution which gives it the dark colour",
