@@ -177,7 +177,7 @@
           "title": "Golconda fort",
           "name": "Golconda fort",
           "desc": "is one of the famous forts of India. The name originates from the Telugu words Golla Konda meaning Shepherds Hill. The origins of the fort can be traced back to the Yadava dynasty of Deogiri and the Kakatiyas of Warangal. Originally a mud fort, it was passed on to the Bahmani dynasty and later to the Qutub Shahis, who held it from 1518 to 1687 CE. The first three Qutb Shahi kings rebuilt Golconda, over a span of 62 years. The fort also has the cannons used in the last siege of Golconda by Aurangzeb, to whom the fort ultimately fell. The huge gates of the fort are decorated with large pointed iron spikes. These spikes prevented elephants from damaging the fort. The fort of Golconda is encircled by a 11-km long outer wall. This was build in order to fortify the fort.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Andhra_Pradesh/golconda-fort.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Andhra_Pradesh/golconda-fort.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Ancient 750-1200",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -186,7 +186,7 @@
           "title": "Andhra Pradesh- Monument- Qutub Shahi Tombs",
           "name": "Andhra Pradesh- Monument- Qutub Shahi Tombs",
           "desc": "The tombs of the legendary Qutub Shahi kings lie to the north of Golconda, about a kilometre away from Banjara Darwaza of the Golconda Fort. Planned and built by the Qutb Shahis themselves, these tombs are said to be the oldest historical monuments in Hyderabad. They form a large group of tombs and stand on a raised platform. The tombs are built in Persian and Hindu architectural styles using grey granite, with stucco ornamentation, the only one of its kind in the world where an entire dynasty has been buried at one place.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Andhra_Pradesh/qutb-shahi-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Andhra_Pradesh/qutb-shahi-1.webp",
