@@ -197,7 +197,7 @@
           "title": "Andhra Pradesh- Fairs and Festivals- Ugadi",
           "name": "Andhra Pradesh- Fairs and Festivals- Ugadi",
           "desc": "Ugadi is the Telugu new year day celebrated on Chaitra Suddha Padyami, the first day of the bright fortnight in the month of Chaitra ( April) There is a strong belief that this day's happenings fore shadow the course of events in the entire year ahead. Everybody in the house gets up early in the morning takes an oil bath and puts on new clothes. Torana, are tied to all the doors of the house. A special preparation called ugadi pachadi (chutney) is a must in very house. Fresh tamarind with fresh water, jaggery, fresh mangoes and fresh margosa flowers form the ingredients of Ugadi Pachadi. The significance of this Pachadi is, the mixture of bitter margosa flowers and sweet jaggery is that life also is a mixture of sorrow and joy. In the evening people gather in temples or in common places. The village purohit worships the new panchangam or almanac. Then he announces the influence of various planets during the new year over the wind, rains and crops, cattle, wealth and human beings.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Andhra_Pradesh/ugadi-pachadi.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Andhra_Pradesh/ugadi-pachadi.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         }
@@ -208,7 +208,7 @@
           "title": "Salar Jung Museum",
           "name": "Salar Jung Museum",
           "desc": "The Salar Jung Museum is one of the oldest museum that has the largest personal collection of artifacts of Nawab Mir Yousuf Ali Khan Salar Jung III. The museum houses more than 43,000 art objects and 50,000 books in the 38 rooms spread over three floors of the building.It also has a prized collection belonging to different civilizations dating back to the first century. Salar Jung III, the former Prime Minister of the seventh Nizam of Hyderabad, spent a substantial amount of his income for over thirty five years to make this priceless collection. The collections include Indian art, middle eastern, far eastern and european art, along with a rare manuscript section. Indian art includes stone sculptures, a Bharhut rail slab, standing Buddha statue, Ananthasayi Vishnu, red stone sculptures of Kushana period etc;, bronze images dating back to later Pallava and Chola periods, jade carvings, painted textiles, wood carvings, miniature paintings, modern art, ivory carvings, textiles, metal-ware, manuscript, arms and armour etc. middle eastern art contains the collection of carpets, paper (manuscripts), glass, metal-ware, furniture, lacquer etc. from Persia, Arabia, Syria, and Egypt. Collection of far eastern art exhibit porcelain, bronze, enamel, lacquerware, embroidery, painting, wood and inlay work from China, Japan, Tibet, Nepal and Thailand etc. Oil and watercolor paintings form an important part of the European collection. Founders gallery displays personal artifacts, momentos received and portraits of the Salar Jungs and the Nizams. Royal clothes, porcelain and silver bowls, books furniture like the ceremonial throne used by Salar Jung III etc. The museum also has a fine selection of printed and embroidered fabrics in cotton, silk and wool belonging to India. Brocades woven with silver and gold thread, silk saris of south India, Kashmiri shawls, muslin waistcloth, famous phulkari embroidery work from Punjab, appliqué temple hangings from Rajasthan, Zari coats, chikan work from Lucknow etc. are displayed here. There is a marvelous childrens gallery showcasing assemblage of toys from all over the world. It displays puppets, model trains and objects acquired by Salar Jung III during his childhood. European artifacts include the world famous statue of Veiled Rebecca, her face only visible through a marble veil. Another equally famous sculpture is a 19th century double-figure sycamore wood carving done by G.H Benzoni, an Italian sculptor. The painting gallery possesses the works of famous Raja Ravi Varma, Tagores, Chughtai and contemporary artists of Andhra Pradesh and other parts of India. There is a gallery exclusively for display of guns, daggers, shields and swords including that of Mughal emperor Aurangazeb, Tipu Sultan, Mohammad Shah and from the Qutb Shahi period. Selection of Indian miniatures like Mughal, Rajasthani, Pahari and Deccani paintings are also displayed here. An amazing room is the clock room which is one of the best galleries displaying more than 300 clocks in various sizes ranging from Sandiers to modern day huge pieces. There are tiny pieces set in magnifying glasses to huge and stately clocks from France, England, Germany, Italy and Switzerland. An interesting exhibit here is a musical clock from Cook and Kelvy of England with a toy figure of a watchman, who pushes open the door every hour in a great hurry, to beat the melodious gong to indicate time.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Andhra_Pradesh/salar-jung-museum.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Andhra_Pradesh/salar-jung-museum.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Modern 1947-1980",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -217,7 +217,7 @@
           "title": "Museum's collection",
           "name": "Museum's collection",
           "desc": "This museum in Hyderabad owes its birth, as its name denotes, to the well known collection assembled over forty years by the artist couple, Jagdish Mittal and his wife, Kamla. The Museum's collection specializes in traditional arts and crafts of the Indian subcontinent up to 1900 CE. and consists of around 1580 objects which include: miniature paintings , drawings, manuscripts, calligraphy , bronzes , terracottas and ivory figures ,jade objects, metalware , textiles , arms and armour and artistic handicrafts There are also several select examples of Nepali and Tibetan thangkas . The Museum is particularly rich in miniatures with representative examples, spanning a period from about 1200 CE to the end of the 19th century, including all the important schools like the Western Indian or Gujarati, Rajasthani, Sultanate, Mughal, Pahari or Punjab Hills, Central Indian, and Deccani. All art objects were assembled by the Mittals chiefly for their aesthetic excellence. Some are crucial for scholars since they are the only examples from a particular school. This was because Mr. Mittal is also a serious art historian. It was the dedication of the couple and their almost religious fervour for art collection that such an achievement could have been possible. A large number of art objects of the Museum have been lent for display at the prestigious Festival of India exhibitions held in London ,U.S.A. and France as well as exhibitions held in the National Museum, New Delhi, and Salar Jung Museum, Hyderabad. Medevial 1550-1600 The Charminar constructed in 1591 by Mohammed Quli Qutab Shah was built to mark the end of a plague in Hyderabad city. The city has almost become synonymous with the monument. It is a massive and impressive structure with four minarets around which one finds a fine blend of modernity and tradition. The Charminar is square in shape, each side measuring 100 feet, with a central pointed high arch at the center. It has four imposing arches with a row of small vaulted niches, which face the four cardinal directions. The Charminar is a two-storied building and balconies on the first floor provide a great view of the surrounding areas. A small mosque on the west facing Mecca adorns the top floor of the Charminar. This mosque is said to be the oldest surviving mosque in Hyderabad. The four minarets of the Charminar and their domed finials rising from their lotus-leaves cushion, rise to 180 feet from the ground. The whole structure contains various small and ornamental arches arranged in vertical and horizontal fashion. The projected canopy, decorative brackets and decoration in stucco plaster add graceful elegance to the Charminar. On the upper courtyard, a screen of arches topped by a row of square jalis or water screens provides a delicate look to the Charminar.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Andhra_Pradesh/jagdish-kamla.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Andhra_Pradesh/jagdish-kamla.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Modern 1947-1080",
           "materials": ""
         }
