@@ -289,7 +289,7 @@
             "title": "Arunachal Pradesh- Handicrafts- Handlooms",
             "name": "Arunachal Pradesh- Handicrafts- Handlooms",
             "desc": "Weaving is an important household industry involving the women of Arunachal Pradesh. The actual weaving is done with a bamboo tube and using cotton and wool. Bark fibres extracted from trees like udal, pudu and grasscloth is also used. The designs are essentially geometric varying from formal arrangement to lines and bands. The colors that are generally used in the weaves are black, yellow, dark blue, green and scarlet - all put together in the most fascinating combinations. There is an abundant use of natural dyes in the weaving though a lot of synthetic dyes are now used. The Tibetan and Bhutanese influence is evident in the Monpa loom on which the Monpa women weave with cotton, wool and bark fibre. They are well known for their fine and colourful coats, shawls, sashes, carpets, bags and tapes (for tieing around hats). The Apatanis are traditionally the most advanced weavers of the state producing excellent ceremonial coats and shawls, and embroidered cotton cloth.Many tribes such as the Nishis, Hill Miris, Akas, Bugums and Mijis depend on the Apatanis for their supply of cloth. Singpho weaving is famous for its artistic patterns. Similarly, the Tangas are reputed for their spun-dyed clothes out of which they make handbags,",
-            "img": "/state-images/Arunachal_Pradesh/weaving.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Arunachal_Pradesh/weaving.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -298,7 +298,7 @@
             "title": "Arunachal Pradesh- Handicrafts- Woodcarving",
             "name": "Arunachal Pradesh- Handicrafts- Woodcarving",
             "desc": "Woodcarving has a long tradition among the Monpas, Khamtis, Wanchos, Phom and Konyak tribes. The main center for woodcarving and woodcraft in the state is the Wancho area of Tirap district. The Wancho woodcarvings have three functions. The first is the one connected with headhunting; the second with the decoration of the Morungs or mens communal houses; and the third with the funerary images of different animals. The Wancho wood carver has a deep sense of proportion. In carving of the human figure, special attention is paid to the head. The features are carved in low relief and are fairly realistic. The tribes like Monpas, Sherdukpens, and Bugums create a variety of coloured masks. The masks are made of a single block of wood hollowed inside and holes are usually but not always made for eyes and mouths. Masks are only worn by boys and men. Women never wear",
-            "img": "/state-images/Arunachal_Pradesh/woodcarving.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Arunachal_Pradesh/woodcarving.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -319,7 +319,7 @@
           "title": "Arunachal Pradesh- Monument- Malinithan and Bismaknagar",
           "name": "Arunachal Pradesh- Monument- Malinithan and Bismaknagar",
           "desc": "Malinithan and Bismaknagar are important archeological sites in Arunachal Pradesh. Malinithan has many granite images of Hindu gods and goddesses. Bismaknagar is situated at the foot of Mishmi Hills in Lohit district. It has a ruined fort that dates back to the period of the Mahabharata. There are also the",
