           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -421,7 +421,7 @@
           "title": "Madan Kamdev Temple",
           "name": "Madan Kamdev Temple",
           "desc": "Famous for its impressive archaeological ruins and stone sculptures, Madan Kamdev has a sculpture of Lord Shiva and the goddess Uma dating back to the Pallava dynasty (eleventh-twelfth centuries). Archaeologists also found evidence of tanks for water storage and fortifications at the site. All this shows that Madan Kamdev must have been an important town in ancient times. The wall, pillars, lintels and door frames of the temple are decorated with floral motifs, images and icons of griffins, nymphs, dancing apsaras, six headed Bhairava, four headed Shiva, women and animals in various postures.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Assam/madan-kamdev-temple.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Assam/madan-kamdev-temple.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -430,7 +430,7 @@
           "title": "Assam  Monument- Sibsagar",
           "name": "Assam  Monument- Sibsagar",
           "desc": "Sibsagar, a historical city was the capital of the mighty Ahoms, who ruled Assam for more than six hundred years before the advent of the British. The most remarkable landmark of the town is the 200 year old Sibsagar tank. On its banks are three significant temples - Shivadol, Vishnudol and Devidol which were all built by Queen Madambika, wife of Siva Singha. The Shivadol is believed to be the highest Siva Temple in India, having a height of 104 feet and the perimeter of 195 feet. The temple belongs to the dvaikuta class in Indian temple architecture and consists of two cells  a garbhagriha (sanctum sanctorum) and a mandapa (pillared hall). The majestic shikhara (dome) with fluted vertical lines rises over the garbhagriha. Vishnudol dedicated to Lord Vishnu, has a Shiva lingam in the sanctum sanctorum, which is quite unique.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Assam/sibsagar.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Assam/sibsagar.webp",
           "historical_significance": "1200-1750",
           "materials": ""
         }
@@ -441,7 +441,7 @@
           "title": "Assam - Fairs and Festivals - Bihu",
           "name": "Assam - Fairs and Festivals - Bihu",
           "desc": "Every Assamese celebrates the festival of Bihu - there are three other main festivals which are part of Bihu namely Rangali Bihu, Bhogali Bihu and Kangali Bihu. Of all the festivals that are celebrated in Assam, the Bihu festivals are the most important one and is celebrated by all the Assamese people no matter what their caste, creed, religion and faith might be. Spring festival or Rangali celebrated in the middle of March marks the beginning of agricultural season. The Bhogali or the harvesting festival which is celebrated during January is characterized by a community feast, buffalo fight and other such entertainment. Lastly, the Kangali is another version of the festival celebrated in the middle of October marking the completion of sowing and transplanting of paddies. The festivals are characterized by songs and dance by groups of performers and observed with joy and gaiety. The performers have traditional dresses like the Dhoti, Gamccha, and Mekhala Chadar. Hips swayings, brisk steps, flinging and flipping of hands are used.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Assam/bihu-dance.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Assam/bihu-dance.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -450,7 +450,7 @@
