@@ -804,7 +804,7 @@
             "title": "Tortoise shellcraft",
             "name": "Tortoise shellcraft",
             "desc": "Diu is famous for the tortoise shell craft where chiefly trinket boxes are made in combination with bone. The shell surface of the box is covered with intricate designs of bones. The designs include fine geometrical patterns, epic figures and animals fringed by floral motifs. A combination of horns, bone and tortoise shells are alsocombined to make products. Bone is also combined with sandalwood, ebony and tortoise shells to create intricate patterns of fretwork that are mounted or inlaid to make ornate octagonal jewel boxes.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Daman_Diu/tortoise-shell.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Daman_Diu/tortoise-shell.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           }
@@ -816,7 +816,7 @@
           "title": "Diu Fort",
           "name": "Diu Fort",
           "desc": "Of greatest interest in Diu, is the fort of Diu which occupies a very prominent position. It is an expansive and imposing structure situated on the extreme coast of the island. Towards the west of the fort lies the town of Diu. A number of old cannons and collection of iron shells can be seen here. Near the Diu Fort, is Panikotha fortress in the sea which can be reached by bo",
-          "img": "/state-images/Daman_Diu/diufort.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Daman_Diu/diufort.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1400-1450",
           "materials": ""
         }
@@ -852,7 +852,7 @@
             "title": "Visual of Company Painting",
             "name": "Visual of Company Painting",
             "desc": "The city was planned systematically, combining twentieth century architecture with that of twentieth centuries older. Sir Edward Lutyens was responsible for the overall plan of New Delhi, and his forte is Rajpath, approached by a 3.2km long road flanked by the imposing buildings of the two Secretariats, built by Herbert Baker. Once known as The Viceroy Palace the Rashtrapati Bhawan, built of brown stone, is truly a befitting home for the President of India and remains Lutyens most significant achievement. It is unquestionably a masterpiece of symmetry, discipline, silhouette, colour and harmony flanked by the two Secretariats and the three buildings together, opens into a huge square called the Viceroys Court where the Jaipur Column stands tall. The Viceroys Court, which frames the main entrance to the house, has lateral entrances on the axis of the Jaipur Column. The main entrance is approached by a broad flight of steps which lead to a 12-column portico. The most outstanding feature of the building is the huge copper dome that rises over a vast colonnaded facade. Beneath the dome is the circular Durbar Hall. The coloured marbles used in the hall come from all parts of India. The columns at the front entrance have bells carved into their capitals. To the west the Bhawan overlooks an enormous Mughal garden designed by Lutyens. Here the principles of hierarchy, order, symmetry and unity are extended from the house into the landscape. A series of ornamental fountains, walls, gazebos and screens are combined with scores of trees, flowers and shrubs. The seat of Indian Parliament, the Sansad Bhawan is a marvellous piece of architecture designed by Lutyens and constructed under the direct supervision of Sir Herbert Baker. Designed as a circular structure, the two semicircular house chambers flank the Central Hall with its impressive dome. The building has a continuous open corridor on the first floor fringed with a colonnade of 144 creamy sandstone pillars. The exterior walls of red sandstone are carved in geometric patterns that echo Mughal jaalis. But it wasnt Lutyens or Bakers who built the rest of New Delhi as it is commonly believed; most if its structures were designed by an unknown Englishman called Robert Tor Tussell, who built Connaught Place, Eastern and Western Courts, Flagstaff House (where Jawaharlal Nehru lived later) and the thousands of public buildings, post offices, officers bungalows and public buildings. India Gate, erected as a memorial in the memory of 90,00 soldiers who laid down their lives during world war I is located at Rajpath, the gateway is 42 m high. Also known as the All India War Memorial, India Gate was designed and constructed by Lutyens. Another additional 13,516 names engraved on the arch and foundations form a separate memorial to the British and Indian soldiers killed on the North-West Frontier in the Afghan War of 1919. Another memorial, Amar Jawan Jyoti is in the form of a flame that burns day and night under the arch to remind the nation of soldiers who perished in the Indo-Pakistan War of December 1971. The entire arch stands on a low base of red Bharatpur stone and rises in stages to a huge cornice.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Delhi/rashtrapati-bhavan.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Delhi/rashtrapati-bhavan.webp",
             "historical_significance": "1900-1947 Modern",
             "materials": ""
           }
@@ -864,7 +864,7 @@
             "title": "Craft Museum",
             "name": "Craft Museum",
             "desc": "The National Handicrafts and Handlooms Museum, popularly known as Crafts Museum displays Indias rich tradition of handicrafts. An attraction of the museum is the presence of craftsmen, who come from different parts of the country to demonstrate their skills. The museum posesses the most exceptional and superb pieces of art and craft, specially the textiles selectively collected from all over the country. The Museum was established in 1956, as a project to show the works of indigenous artisans in one place. By the early 1980s, over 20,000 objects had been collected. Constructed with mud the numerous huts are beautifully decorated with folk art and exhibits include",
