@@ -885,7 +885,7 @@
           "title": "Delhi-Monument-Qutab Complex",
           "name": "Delhi-Monument-Qutab Complex",
           "desc": "1100-1200 Early Medieval The Qutub Minar, probably a tower of victory started by Qutub-ud-din Aibak during the twelfth century, and completed by Altamash is an interesting mix in Hindu and Islamic styles and each of the original three storeys has different designs. The base storey has alternate angular and circular extensions while those of the second one are round and the third one has only angular flutings. The projecting balconies have a very interesting pattern with an intricate design in which triangular pieces of vaulting spring from the corners. The attractiveness of the balconies is heightened by the bands of deep inscriptions. The complex also houses the Quwwat-ul-Islam Masjid which was erected on the site of a Hindu temple. It has an intricately carved ceiling followed by the spectacular courtyard of the mosque which is lined by the rows of the profusely adorned pillars on both sides. Hindu motifs, like tasseled ropes, bells, tendrils, cows and leaves, are visible all over the mosque. The very first indication of the Islamic character of the building come from the elegant pointed arches with curvaceous and serpentine calligraphy of texts from Quran in Arabic crowning them, towards the west of the mosque. A massive stone screen erected in front of the prayer hall, with a central arch and two similar, though smaller, arches on either side; are profusely carved, crowded with exquisite decorations and is one of the earliest and finest examples of the fusion of Hindu and Islamic art. Alai Darwaza, is the first manifestation of Islamic, architecture and ornamentation has a huge dome with a central knob, pointed arches shaped like horseshoes, and the lotus-bud fringes of the arches. The Darwaza is built of red sandstone with arched openings on all sides. The arch frames are embellished with lotus-bud designs, which are also seen in the perforated screens on the sides of the gate.The gateway is very skillfully decorated withcomplex carvings of the geometric patterns and white marble bands of calligraphy. Apart from Qutab minar, the area also hosts many tombs such as Jamali Kamali Tomb, Sultan Garhi Tomb, Balban Tomb, Madhi Masjid, Palam Mosque and many more.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/d_007.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/d_007.webp",
           "historical_significance": "1100-1200 Early Medieval",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -894,7 +894,7 @@
           "title": "Delhi - Monument - Ferozeshah Kotla",
           "name": "Delhi - Monument - Ferozeshah Kotla",
           "desc": "Feroze Shah Kotla, the palace of Feroze Shah Tughlaq has nothing much that survives, except the Ashoka Pillar, which Feroze Shah Tughlaq had brought from Topra, in Ambala and the ruins of the Jama Masjid and a round step well (baoli), which is two floors deep and has antechambers at each level.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/d_010.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/d_010.webp",
