@@ -903,7 +903,7 @@
           "title": "Delhi  Monument - Red Fort",
           "name": "Delhi  Monument - Red Fort",
           "desc": "1700-1750 Medieval Shahjahan laid out a new walled city called Shahjahanabad (the present old Delhi) along the Yamuna. It is there he erected a Red Fort similar to Agra, its name attributed to the red sandstone of its walls and adorned it with white marble pavilions including halls of public and private audience (Diwan-i-am where the Emperor would sit on a marbled paneled alcove, studded with gems, and hear complaints of the common people and Diwan-i-khas, where the Emperor held private meetings. This hall is made of marble, and its centre-piece used to be the Peacock Throne, which was studded with rubies and gems and was carried away to Iran by Nadir Shah in 1739. Domed and arched marble palaces, plush private apartments like Rang Mahal, the residence of the Emperors wives and mistresses are also part of this magnificent complex. The Rang Mahal was crowned with gild turrets, skillfully painted and ornamented with a complex assortment of decorated with excellent paintings, gold bordered projections, mosaics of mirrors and the ceiling was made with gold and silverwhich wonderfully reflected in a central pool in the marble floor, a mosque (Moti Masjid) and richly designed gardens. Decorated with gold, precious stones and inlays of pietra dura work, the Red Fort still remains an impressive testimony to the Mughal splendor and might.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/red-fort-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/red-fort-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "1700-1750 Medieval",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -912,7 +912,7 @@
           "title": "elhi  Monument -Jama Masjid",
           "name": "elhi  Monument -Jama Masjid",
           "desc": "Jama masjid, the biggest mosque in India started by Shah Jahan, is built of colossal proportions and has a courtyard, which can accommodate 25,000 devotees at a time. Constructed in sandstone and white marble, it combines the best of Hindu and Muslim styles of architecture, standing on 260 pillars supporting 15 domes at varying elevations. The vast courtyard is flanked by pillared corridors which run all along the courtyard and have domed pavilions in the middle on either side. The prayer hall has a magnificent facade of eleven arches, the central one being higher than the others and serving as the entrance. It is topped off by three magnificent domes which are richly ornamented and have black and white marble stripes.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/jama-masjid-delhi.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/jama-masjid-delhi.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -921,7 +921,7 @@
           "title": "Delhi- Monument- Humayuns Tomb",
           "name": "Delhi- Monument- Humayuns Tomb",
           "desc": "1550-1600 Medieval Humayuns Tomb, built by his widow Haji Begum in 1564 is one of the most spectacular monument belonging to the Mughal period. A dazzling landmark in the evolution of Mughal architecture in India it is the first Indian building to use the Persian double dome and harmonious proportions. The red sandstone and marble structure is believed to be the inspiration for the designs of Taj Mahal. Standing on a stone platform, surrounded by a garden divided into quadrants by water channels. the plan of the building is simply brilliant and very mathematical. Enclosed by a high wall is a square garden divided initially into four large squares separated by causeways and channels, each square divided again into smaller squares by pathways (Charbagh) which is a typical Mughal garden. The intricate and delicately beautiful lattice work on the tomb remained the trademark of Mughal architecture. On the second storey, a high double dome of marble surmounts the roof with pillared chhatris placed around it.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/humayun-tomb.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/humayun-tomb.webp",
           "historical_significance": "1550-1600 Medieval",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -930,7 +930,7 @@
           "title": "Delhi- Monument- Purana Qila",
           "name": "Delhi- Monument- Purana Qila",
           "desc": "1500-1550 Proto-historic The Purana Qila (Old Fort) stands on the site where the ancient city of Indraprashtha is believed to have existed. Archaeological excavations have yielded Painted Grey Ware pottery which has been dated to 1000BCE. Similar pottery has been noticed in other sites associated with the epic Mahabharata as well, which seems to prove that this indeed was the place where Indraprastha once flourished. The Old Fort was the citadel of the city of Dinapanah which Humayun started building in 1533 and completed five years later.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/d_032.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/d_032.webp",
           "historical_significance": "1500-1550 Proto-historic",
           "materials": ""
