@@ -1004,7 +1004,7 @@
           "title": "Delhi-Fairs & Festivals- Phoolwalon Ki Sair",
           "name": "Delhi-Fairs & Festivals- Phoolwalon Ki Sair",
           "desc": "Phoolwalon Ki Sair (walk of the florists) is a festival that has continued from the time of the Mughals, who encouraged the celebrations as a means of bonding among people. This annual festival of flowersellers, is a festival in which both Hindus and Muslims participate. Phoolwalon-ki-Sair or the Sair-e-Gulafroshan, a three-day festival celebrated simultaneously at the dargah of Khwaja Bakhtiyar Kaki and at the ancient shrine of Devi Jog Maya in Mehrauli. Kathak dances, qawwalis, a blaze of lights, huge pankhas (fans) made of palm leaves, decorated with tinsel and flowers and acrobats delight a vast audience. A huge procession, led by fire dancers, takes the flower pankhas through the streets of Mehrauli. Audio/video of Qawali",
-          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/phoolwalon-ki-sair.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/phoolwalon-ki-sair.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -1013,7 +1013,7 @@
           "title": "Delhi- Fairs & Festivals-Dilli Haat",
           "name": "Delhi- Fairs & Festivals-Dilli Haat",
           "desc": "Situated in the heart of the city, the Dilli Haat is a version of the traditional weekly rural market, offering a delightful amalgam of craft, food and other cultural activities. However, while the village haat is a mobile, flexible arrangement at Dilli Haat, a permanent haat, it is the craftsmen who are mobile and ever-changing thereby offering a kaleidoscope of the richness and diversity of Indian handicrafts and artifacts. A wide variety of skilfully crafted handicrafts, intrinsic to each part of the country are seen in this exotic bazaar. These range from Delhi has seen the rise and fall of many empires which have left behind a plethora of monuments that tell tales of the grandeur and glory of bygone ages. A city which traces its history to Mahabharata, the great epic tale of wars fought between the Kauravas and the Pandavas for the city of Indraprastha. If some monuments remind the rule of the mighty Mughals and Lodhis, others speak of the rich legacy that the British had left behind. In the late tenth century, Lalkot was the provincial Rajput centre ruled by Prithviraj Chauhan who was defeated by the Ghurids of Ghazni under the command of Qutab-ub-din Aibak. Mehrauli on the outskirts of the southern part of Delhi is known for the Qutab complex of monuments.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/dillihaat.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/dillihaat.webp",
           "historical_significance": "1980-2000 Contemporary",
           "materials": ""
         }
@@ -1024,7 +1024,7 @@
           "title": "Delhi- Museum-Teen Murti Bhawan",
           "name": "Delhi- Museum-Teen Murti Bhawan",
           "desc": "Teen Murti Bhawan was the home of Jawaharlal Nehru, Indias first Prime Minister. Formerly called Flagstaff House, it used to be the house of the British Commander-in-Chief. This was the residence of Nehru from 1948 till his death in 1964. Having a façade of stone and stucco in an austere classical style, it is preserved as a museum and a library of modern Indian history in memory of Jawaharlal Nehru.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/d_085a.jpg",
