           "materials": ""
         }
@@ -1066,7 +1066,7 @@
             "title": "Mussoll Dance",
             "name": "Mussoll Dance",
             "desc": "Mussoll is a folk-play-cum-dance played by the Kshatriyas among the Christians from the two villages of Chandor-Kott and Kouddi in Chandor village. Mussoll used by women folk for pounding rice. The dance is a war dance - a dance of a martial race or caste. From the lyrics of the song, the Mussoll dance commemorates the victory over the Cholas by King Harihara II,son of Bukka I of the Vijayanagarempire at the ancient fort of Chandrapur around 1310CE. Mussoll dance has a constituent symbol, i.e. a bear which is the symbol of Cholas. The Mussoll or pounding pestle used in the dance is made of solid bamboo of about 6-7 feet in length with inserted hawk's bells and is carried only by those who will actually dance.",
