@@ -1125,7 +1125,7 @@
           "title": "Goa- Monument- Church-Church of Our Lady of Rosary",
           "name": "Goa- Monument- Church-Church of Our Lady of Rosary",
           "desc": "The oldest surviving church in Goa is the Church of Our Lady of Rosary in old Goa. A combination of Gothic and Renaissance style specific to Portugal, this church is built on the site of Alfonso de Albuquerque's hill of Victory soon after he conquered Goa in 1510. Dedicated to Our Lady of Immaculate Conception or the Virgin Mary, the huge Convent dating from 1606, was the first Nunnery in Asia. It is believed that St Francis would preach here in the evenings. The almost austere simplicity of the exterior stands in contrast to the rich Gothic decorations of the interiors. The roof of the church is supported by wooden beams. The porch has two stories and the facade has round shaped towers on either side. The decorative motifs of this church include cables and anchors with seashells etc. The interiors of the church reflect the influences of other religions in the decorations. The main altar for instance has cashew, flowers and mango designs. The Baroque Period coincides with the Renaissance period in Europe and also coincides with the arrival of missionaries to Goa including St Francis Xavier. Church building during this time reached a peak with styles and plans that were totally European. The churches of Old Goa including the Basilica of Bom Jesus and the Se Cathedral, and the Church of St Cajetan and the largest of them all, the Augustine Church of Our Lady of Grace, now in ruins, belong to this period and style. The architecture of this period is a mixture of Tuscan, Doric, Ionic and Corinthian styles.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Goa/church-of-our-lady-of-rosar.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Goa/church-of-our-lady-of-rosar.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1500-1550",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -1134,7 +1134,7 @@
           "title": "Goa- Monument- Church- Se Cathedral",
           "name": "Goa- Monument- Church- Se Cathedral",
           "desc": "Admired as one of the largest churches in Asia, the Se Cathedral church is an expression of the magnificence of the Portuguese era in Goa. It is dedicated to St. Catherine of Alexandria as Alfonso de Albuquerque conquered Goa on the St. Catherine's day (November 25) in 1510. Even though the original mud and stone structure was built in that year itself, the present Se Cathedral church came up in 1652, after years of laborious craftsmanship. The exterior of the church is a blend of Tuscan and Doric architecture styles of Europe, while the interior is a blend of Mosaic-Corinthian style. The interiors are regal with an imposing principal chapel. There are four chapels on either side of the main altar. Although the main façade of the Se Cathedral was flanked by two bell towers, the one towards the north collapsed in 1776. The remaining tower houses five bells including the Golden bell, named so because bits of gold were mixed with other metals for making the bell.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Goa/se-cathedral-oldgoa.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Goa/se-cathedral-oldgoa.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1500-1550",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -1143,7 +1143,7 @@
           "title": "Goa- Monument- Church- Basilica of Bom Jesus",
           "name": "Goa- Monument- Church- Basilica of Bom Jesus",
           "desc": "The Basilica of Bom Jesus, or Infant Jesus, is a Catholic basilica. Declared a world heritage site, Basilica of Bom Jesus stands as a fine example of Jesuit architecture. Famous for housing the mortal remains of St. Francis Xavier, the Jesuit missionary to India and Japan, it is one of the richest churches in Goa. This church is covered with marble and inlaid with precious stones and paintings depicting the life of St. Francis Xavier. The facade of the Basilica is a visual delight showing a combination of Ionic, Doric and Corinthian styles in the columns. The ground floor has three portals, above them are three corresponding long windows and on the second floor are three circular windows. The Church is styled with curves and embellishments and the facade is adorned by pillars and carvings.The roof of the church was originally tiled. St. Francis's body was brought to Goa almost 150 years after his death. It was a gift from Medici, Cosimo III, the Grand Duke of Tuscany. It now lies in an airtight glass coffin, placed inside a silver casket fashioned by a seventeenth century Florentine jeweller. The churches of the Indian Baroque period represent the local contribution to church building in terms of style and design especially designs of the outer facade and the ceiling with inclusion of flowers, tropical motifs etc. The prominent churches of this period include the Church of St Francis of Assisi at old Goa, The Church of Holy Spirit at Margao and the Church of St Ana at Talaulim and The Church of Our Lady of Compassion at Divar.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Goa/Basilica-of-Bom-Jesus-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Goa/Basilica-of-Bom-Jesus-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Medieval 1650-1700",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -1152,7 +1152,7 @@
           "title": "Goa  Monument- Church- Church of St Francis of Assisi",
           "name": "Goa  Monument- Church- Church of St Francis of Assisi",
           "desc": "The Church of the St Francis of Assisi was built by eight Franciscan Friars in 1517. It has carved woodwork, murals with scenes from the life of St Francis and the floor is made mostly of gravestones that date from the sixteenth century. The convent behind this church is now an archaeological museum with portraits of Portuguese viceroys, and sculptures from Hindu temples, amongst other things, on display. The churches of the Rococo period are characterized by their rather small size but with an accent on exquisite and ornate finishing on the inside with local motifs and paintings. Another element was the use of Stucco on the exterior facade. The style reflected to a large extent the relaxation of the religious fervor of the Portuguese. This was also the period of the New Conquests. One of the classical examples of this style is the Church of St. Stephen's at San Estevan near Panaji. Others include the Church of Our Lady of Immaculate conception at Moira, the Church of St Alex at Calangute and the Church of Our Lady of Rosary at Margao.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Goa/st-francis.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Goa/st-francis.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1500-1550",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -1172,7 +1172,7 @@
           "title": "Goa- Fairs and Festivals- Carnival",
           "name": "Goa- Fairs and Festivals- Carnival",
           "desc": "Among the many and various colorful feasts and festivals that are celebrated in Goa, the Goa Carnival is the most eagerly awaited event. The Carnival is exclusive and unique to Goa, and was introduced by the Portuguese who ruled over Goa for over five hundred years. Although, the three-day festival is primarily celebrated by Christians, it has also adapted Hindu tradition and festivities, western dance forms, and turned into a spectacular event. The carnival is held in February for three days and nights,when the legendary king Momo takes over the state and the streets come alive with music and color.It is a weeklong event of festivity and merry-making, which has been celebrated since 18th Century. The carnival is meant to be for feasting and merrymaking. Huge parades march through the streets with bands, dances and floats in the evenings. The origins of the carnival can be traced to carivals started in the Spanish and Portugese colonies, which were known for their singing and dancing.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Goa/Goa-carnival-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Goa/Goa-carnival-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -1181,7 +1181,7 @@
           "title": "Goa- Fairs and Festivals- Feast of St. Francis Xavier",
           "name": "Goa- Fairs and Festivals- Feast of St. Francis Xavier",
           "desc": "The feast of Saint Francis Xavier is held on 3rd December every year at Velha Goa or Old Goa which is about 10kms east of Panaji. The mummified body of St. Francis Xavier is enshrined in a silver casket in one of the chapels of Basilica of Bom Jesus built in 1605 by the Jesuits. The body of the Saint is exposed to public once every ten years and pilgrims from all over the world come to pay homage. The Relics of the Saint placed in a crystal urn show him to be clad in rich vestments with the coat of arms of the queen of Portugal. By the side is a gold medal and a staff covered in gold and 160 emeralds. On the day of the Feast of St. Francis Xavier, the saint's finger is displayed to the public. The exposition of the body of St. Francis Xavier is held once every ten years.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Goa/G_029.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Goa/G_029.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -1190,7 +1190,7 @@
           "title": "Shigmo Festival",
           "name": "Shigmo Festival",
           "desc": "Shigmo is a social festival in Goa which is like Holi, where a puja of the stems of the betel nut, struck on the ground before the temple is performed and a little grass put at their feet is then burnt. There are a number of dances which are performed.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Goa/shigmo.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Goa/shigmo.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
           "materials": ""
         }
@@ -1226,7 +1226,7 @@
             "title": "The basic and foremost feature of this unique form of painting is an exhaustive detailing of each and every component relating to the scene.",
             "name": "The basic and foremost feature of this unique form of painting is an exhaustive detailing of each and every component relating to the scene.",
             "desc": "There are four primary schools of Indian Miniature Paintings - the Rajput, the Mughal, Pahari and the Deccan schools. Miniature Painters used varied substances for coloring their drawings, namely- minerals, vegetables, precious stones, indigo, conch shells, pure gold and silver. The illustrated manuscripts of Jains and Buddhists, and the flowering of the Mughal, Rajput, Pahari and Deccan Miniatures are noted for their meticulous execution and artistic skills. The fine stroke of brushes conveyed the themes from the Ramayana, Mahabharata, Bhagvata Purana, Rasikpriya, Rasamanjiri, etc. The themes have also emerged from the compositions of the great poets like Sur, Keshav, Bihari, Matiram, Dev and Puhukar who wrote great poems like Sursagar, Bihari Satsai, Rasraj, Ashtayam and Rasbeli. There are several themes of different nature. They centered on the love of Radha and Krishna. definite narrative content. These brilliantly illustrated paintings glow as if illuminated from inside. The use of bold contrasts such as red, green, blue yellow is definitely a native influence. Artists belonging to Pahari school of painting used bright colors and depicted themes of love. These Paintings from the Hills, were developed at the Hindu royal courts of Kulu, Guler, Mankot, Nurpur, Mandi, Kangra and Basoholi regions of the North Indian mountain valleys.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/miniature-painting-1.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/miniature-painting-1.webp",
             "historical_significance": "",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -1235,7 +1235,7 @@
             "title": "Raja Ravi Verma & his paintings",
             "name": "Raja Ravi Verma & his paintings",
             "desc": "Raja Ravi Varma was born on April 29, 1848 at Kilimanoor, a small town in Kerala. His uncle was a well known artist known as Raja Raja Varma who gave him elementary art lessons. He was taken to Thiruvananthapuram in his fourteenth year to stay in the royal palace and learn oil painting. Ravi Varma had many opportunities to discover and learn new techniques and media in the field of painting. His later years spent in Mysore, Baroda and other parts of the country enabled him to sharpen and expand his skills and blossom into a mature and complete painter. He was the first artist to cast the Indian Gods and mythological characters in natural earthy surroundings using European realism. His dazzling oil paintings of Indias ancient glory and his mass reproductions through oleography reached out to the Indian populace in an extraordinary scale at the turn of the twentieth Century. Varma had already made a name for himself as a highly talented painter who had mastered the European painter, the Odore Jensen at the Travancore court. His technique of oil painting observing a visiting Dutch paintings had won prizes at Madras and had been highly appreciated by the British governor too. But Baroda, definitely, was a major turning point in his career. Varma was invited to paint a portrait of the 18-year-old Sayajirao Gaekwad in 1881. The young prince was so happy with the result that he not only commissioned Varma to paint portraits of many of his family members thereafter, but also got him to execute more artistically ambitious works on themes drawn from Puranic legends, Sanskrit classics and mythological epics to decorate the newly built Lakshmi Vilas palace. Varma, was one of the first Indian artist to adopt the style of French and British naturalist painters by adopting devices like the chiaroscuro and dramatic lighting effects in the oil-on-canvas medium. But Varma employed these western techniques to paint purely Indian themes. Episodes from literary and mythological sources like those of Dushyant-Shakuntala, Nala-Damayanti, Radha-Krishna, Harishchandra-Taramati, the Mahabharata and the Ramayana were his favourite subjects.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/rajaravivarma.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/rajaravivarma.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Modern 1800-1850",
             "materials": ""
           }
@@ -1246,7 +1246,7 @@
             "title": "Garba Dance",
             "name": "Garba Dance",
             "desc": "Navaratri or nine nights before Dussera is the most colourful festival of Gujarat when worship of the mother goddess and her numerous forms takes place in October. During the celebration which starts in the evening and continues till late at night, people assemble together in squares and temple compounds and sing and dance. Garba dance is performed in a circle by men and women around an earthenware pot called a garbo, filled with water. As the dancers whirl around the pot, a singer and a drummer provide the musical accompaniment. The participants clap in a steady rhythm. At times they dance with small and colourful sticks on rhythm and instead of clapping use sticks held in both hands. This is known as dandia. As they whirl to the rhythm of the dance, men and women strike the dandias together, adding to the joyous atmosphere. Proto historic 2500-600 BCE The 4,500-year-old archaeological site of Lothal belonging to the Indus Valley Civilisation was discovered in 1954. Like the cities of Mohenjodaro and Harappa in Pakistan, Lothal too had excellent architecture and town planning. Lothal is prominent as the walled city and as the port from where overseas trade was conducted. The dwellings were constructed with baked bricks and had underground drains as in Mohenjodaro. Lothal, literally means 'the mound of the dead, and the most architecturallysophisticated part is its dockyard, which provided berthing facilities for the ships. Constructed from kiln-baked bricks, the dock comprised a basin. A wide channel was constructed to facilitate entry of ships. Near the dock were warehouses for storage of cargo brought in by the ships. This was probably one of the most important ports on the subcontinent. Lothal was an active business center and had business links with Mesopotamia, Egypt and Persia as the seals discovered at the site suggest. The entire city had a drainage system, well laid-out, paved roads and a bath for every house, some of which were double storied and built on mud platforms. Beads made in the city were quite famous and there is evidence of a bead factory in Lothal. Pottery was another flourishing industry. The ruins of a fire altar suggest that the people of Lothal worshipped the fire god along with the sea god. The unique Harappan system of weights and measures was used in Lothal too. Bricks were in a perfect ratio and the decimal system was used for measuring width.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/garba.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/garba.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           }
@@ -1257,7 +1257,7 @@
             "title": "Gujarat- Handicrafts- Sankheda Laquerware Furniture",
             "name": "Gujarat- Handicrafts- Sankheda Laquerware Furniture",
             "desc": "Sankheda in Gujarat is famous for artistic furniture of low seats, tables, stools and Jhoola. The lacquerware is found designed into chair-backs and chair-legs, hand-rests, and specially carved shapes to make the chairs more attractive. The colours used for lacquering are gold, silver, red, blue, yellow, orange and black. The lacquerware itself has designs using the above colour combinations as dots, squares, and other geometric or floral motifs.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/sankheda-furniture.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/sankheda-furniture.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -1266,7 +1266,7 @@
             "title": "Pottery",
             "name": "Pottery",
             "desc": "Kutch and Saurashtra in Gujarat are noted for their beautiful earthenware. They produce natural white wares in attractive shapes and designs. Banaskantha produces water pots that are very artistically and skilfully decorated. Vidi, a small village in Kutch is abundant in white clay for manufacturing this",
-            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/pots-white-clay.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/pots-white-clay.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -1275,7 +1275,7 @@
             "title": "Gujarat- Handicrafts -Wood Carvings",
             "name": "Gujarat- Handicrafts -Wood Carvings",
             "desc": "Gujarat is one of the richest regions known for wood carvings. Even the ordinary houses of this state are beautifully decorated with carvings. A fascinating feature of Gujarat carvings is the large variations found in a single motif. Currently, both old Indian style carved furniture with low seats and tables, as well as modern furnitures are produced. In the traditional style, well known items are: nav khania and tran khania, cupboards with nine and three compartments for keeping personal belongings, the paniara stands for water pots which need special mention because of their very lovely carvings, mostly floral and geometrical and the jhula (swing). Pethapur in Mehsana district is famous for engraving of blocks for textile printing for which teakwood is mostly used.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/Pethapur-block-wood.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/Pethapur-block-wood.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -1284,7 +1284,7 @@
             "title": "Gujarat- Handicrafts- Silks",
             "name": "Gujarat- Handicrafts- Silks",
             "desc": "Gujarat has developed its own style of kinkhabs (brocades) woven with extra weft patterns. At one time these were exported to as far as Russian royalty from the port of Surat. Kanbis or patidars, khatris and mommin (particularly the women) are expert craftspersons in this field. There are dozens of designs in vogue like tara mandal- a rather complicated constellation, animals, fruits, stylised dance figures, peacocks, women waving fans and lotus flowers in many shapes. Rudrol in Mehsana district, Jamnagar and Dokla produce them. Several varieties of silk like gajji and mashru are made. A famous item is the Nathdwara pichwai in brochade style. Tanchoi fabric iswoven in Surat. In tanchoi sarees the designs are always floral with interspersing of birds. The usual ground is bright blue, purple, green or red with areas patterned in tabby weave. Figures of flying birds, paired cocks amidst floral sprays are worked on them. Sometimes the pallu is done more solidly with peacocks, baskets or bunches of flowers or hunting scenes. Patola is the most colourful and elaborate weave with its figured body and the subtle merging of one shade into another. The most renowned amongst patolas is the one made in Patan in Mehsana district. These patolas comprise mostly of the wedding saree with dark green, white or yellow against a ruby red background, and leaf and flower patterns while the pallu features dancing girls and parrots or swans.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/pichwai.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/pichwai.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -1293,7 +1293,7 @@
             "title": "Gujarat- Handicrafts- Bandhani Craft",
             "name": "Gujarat- Handicrafts- Bandhani Craft",
             "desc": "Bandhani, the craft of tie and dye of fabrics is an ancient art practiced in many places in Gujarat and Rajasthan. Jamnagar, Anjar and Bhuj are famous centres of this form of craft. Bandhanis of each of these regions are distinctive in design, technique, and ornamentation. The cloth is folded and dyed and designs are created with the combination of small dots and circles. The borders are broad and are worked both in matching and contrasting colours. Amongst the numerous designs the two important ones are barah bagh or bavan bagh which means twelve gardens or fifty two gardens. It is said that the entire bandhani tradition of this region is manifested in the gharchola, a form of a saree. The making of the gharchola is a complicated and time-consuming process. A starched silk odhni or sari is fixed to a large wooden frame using nails. The sari/odhini, coloured red or green, is divided into a network of squares by rows of white tie-dyed spots or woven bands of zari. Motifs within each compartment range from elephants, parrots, and flowers to dancing girls. Jamnagar has a satiny, silk type of glazed silk on which bandhani is done for blouses, vests and lamp shades.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/gharchola.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/gharchola.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -1302,7 +1302,7 @@
             "title": "Gujarat- Handicrafts -Crochet Lacework",
             "name": "Gujarat- Handicrafts -Crochet Lacework",
             "desc": "The oldest centre of crochet lace work in Gujarat is Jamnagar where the women of the Vohra community have been engaged in this art since Aurangzeb's time. They specialize in a particular type of conical cap that is shaped using a mould of conical shape while crocheting. The colours are dark, black and brown with white and look as effective as ikat work.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/lace-jamnagar.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/lace-jamnagar.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           }
@@ -1314,7 +1314,7 @@
           "title": "Gujarat- Monument- Dholavira",
           "name": "Gujarat- Monument- Dholavira",
           "desc": "Proto Historic 3000-2500 BCE Dholavira located on the North West corner of the Khadir Island in Kutch was discovered in the year 1967 and the ruins of the settlement found here are believed to be more than 5000 years old. These excavation sites are one of the largest Harappansites in India. The ruins of the ancient town serve as a proof of the fact that there is a connection with Indus valley because a planned city has been excavated, which is built according to a geometric plan. The city is divided into the citadel, the middle town and the lower town. The excavation brought to light the sophisticated urban planning and architecture, and unearthed large numbers of antiquities. It is estimated that Dholavira is even older than the port city of Lothal as the excavations conducted here came up with a number of ancient artifacts, like seals, beads, animal bones, gold, silver, terracotta ornaments and vessels linked to Mesopotamia. There is even evidence that the town was an important trade center between other settlements in Sind, Punjab and Western Asia. The most fascinating discovery of Dholavira is the large inscription, comprising of ten letters in the Indus script. Each letter has been engraved on slices of crystalline material.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/dholavira.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/dholavira.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -1323,7 +1323,7 @@
           "title": "Gujarat- Monument- Step Well- Mata Bhavanis Vav",
           "name": "Gujarat- Monument- Step Well- Mata Bhavanis Vav",
           "desc": "One of the oldest step-wells is the Mata Bhavani's Vav at Ahmedabad, built in the eleventh century. The water is approached by a long flight of steps above which rises a sequence of two, three and four storey open pavilions. The elaborate ornamentation of the columns, brackets and beams and the",
-          "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/mata-bhawani-vav.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/mata-bhawani-vav.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 750-1200",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -1332,7 +1332,7 @@
           "title": "Gujarat- Monument- Step Well- Ruda Baoli- Adalaj",
           "name": "Gujarat- Monument- Step Well- Ruda Baoli- Adalaj",
           "desc": "One of the well preserved and intricately sculpted step well is the Ruda baoli at Adalaj, located 12 miles north of Ahmedabad. Octagonal in shape, it has stairways from three sides meeting on a wide landing from where it descends down to the water level. The long flight of steps descend, columns and connecting beams create open structures of increasing complexity; the receding perspectives of columns and cross-beams are particularly striking. Wall niches incorporate miniature pilasters, eaves and roof-like pediments. The decoration of the columns, curved brackets and lintels is highly elaborate with repeated friezes and ornamentation of animal motifs and deities. Ahmedabad in Gujarat, has a rich historical heritage that can be traced back to the ancient times. The city was built in 1411 by Sultan Ahmad Shah. Humayun took over Gujarat in 1534 then lost it and it was reclaimed by Akbar in 1572 and therefore has a legacy of Indo- Islamic architecture. The city was surrounded by walls that still exist.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/adlaj-baoli.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/adlaj-baoli.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -1359,7 +1359,7 @@
           "title": "Gujarat- Monument- Step Well- Dada Harirs Vav- Ahmedabad",
           "name": "Gujarat- Monument- Step Well- Dada Harirs Vav- Ahmedabad",
           "desc": "The Dada Harir's vav at Ahmedabad, is one of the finest example of the Mughal period. The Dada Harir's vav is modelled on the earlier Mata Bhavani's vav, though it has an additional domed pavilion at the entrance. One striking feature of this vav is the complete absence of figural themes. The motifs in stylized scrollwork that adorn the wall niches are examples of Islamic architecture.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/dada-hari-vav.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/dada-hari-vav.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1400-1450",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -1368,7 +1368,7 @@
           "title": "Surya Temple,Modhera",
           "name": "Surya Temple,Modhera",
           "desc": "A masterpiece of the Solanki dynasty, the Sun Temple at Modhera is one of the few shrines that are dedicated to the Sun God apart from the one at Konark and Martand. Situated on the banks of Pushpavati river, it was built by King Bhimdev (believed to be the descendant of the lineage of Sun God). The temple has a shrine that is designed in a manner, so that the first rays of the Sun shine on the image of the Lord Surya in the garbhagriha. Though the Temple was plundered by Mahmud Ghazni; the architectural grandeur has still not vanished. Erected on a high platform, the Temple appears majestic with its grand structure and the Toranas leading to the main halls. The exterior walls are engraved with intricate carvings, and every single inch of the structure is covered with the sculptural patterns of Gods, Goddesses, birds, beasts and flowers. The Sun Temple is divided into three parts namely Surya Kund, Sabha Mandap and Guda Mandap. Surya Kund is a deep stepped tank in the front of the temple that was used to store pure water. The devotees used to take a halt here for ceremonial purposes before moving towards the temple. More than 108 shrines mark the steps of this tank including the shrines dedicated to Lord Ganesha, Lord Shiva, Sheetala Mata and many others. In front of the tank, a huge torana (archway) leads to the Sabha Mandap. Sabha Mandapwas the assembly hall where religious gatherings took place. This hall is open from all the four sides and has 52 delicately carved pillars. The intricate carvings depict scenes from the Ramayana, Mahabharata and scenes from the life of Lord Krishna. In order to get into the garbhagriha, one has to cross the passage with pillars and arches. Guda Mandap is the garbhagriha that is supported by a lotus-base plinth. Once, this hall used to house the idol of the Sun God. The designing of the hall was done in a way, so that the idol gets the first glimpse of the Sun at equinoxes. The walls represent the Sun God in his 12 different facets of each month. The carved walls also depict the aspects of human life like the cycle of birth and death.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/Modhera_SunTemple1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/Modhera_SunTemple1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1500-1550 Early Medieval 750-1200",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -1377,7 +1377,7 @@
           "title": "Gujarat- Monument- Jain Temples  Palitana",
           "name": "Gujarat- Monument- Jain Temples  Palitana",
           "desc": "Palitana temples are considered to be the most sacred amongst all the Jain temples and perhaps the largest cluster of Jain temples anywhere. From the base to the peak of the Shatrunjaya hill, where the temples are located, there are in all 863 temples. The interesting fact is that no one is allowed to sleep overnight including the priest, because the temple city has been built as an abode for the Gods. The temples dedicated to the Jain Tirthankaras were built in two phases-the eleventh and twelfth centuries in the first phase and in the sixteenth century as the second phase. Some of the earliest temples built in the eleventh century were destroyed by Muslim invaders in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries. The current temples date back to sixteenth century onwards. The hilltops are bounded by sturdy walls and the temples are grouped into nine enclosures each having several minor temples clustered around a central temple. Like the Jains who prefer the purity of white, their temples too are usually white, a combination of plaster and marble. The temples are elaborately ornamental. Throughout the cluster one can see detailed carvings, beautiful idols and images, jeweled statues and intricate toranas. Among the most important temples here, is the Chaumukh temple with its four-faced deity of Adinath enshrined on a marble pedestal in a shrine open on all four sides. Built in the early seventeenth century, it is planned as a large square subdivided into smaller squares, each structure topped by a dome. The innermost five domes topped squares form a cross and represent the five hills sacred to the Jains. The Adishwar temple is probably the most profusely decorated in Palitana. Its ornate pillars and roofs are decorated with intricately cut marble in the shape of dragons. Near the Adishwar temple is the Muslim shrine of Angar Pir. Childless couples make offerings of miniature cradles at this shrine in the hope of being blessed with children. Other temples worth mentioning are the Sampriti Raj, Kumarpal, and Rampal temples.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/palitana-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/palitana-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 750-1200",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -1386,7 +1386,7 @@
           "title": "Cave Temples",
           "name": "Cave Temples",
           "desc": "There are three main caves which were probably Jain and Buddhist monasteries. Babapyara cave is Jain while the cave in Uparkot Fort and the Kapurkodiya caves are Buddhist. Since they were used for residential purposes of the monks they are very plain and have simple and few carvings. Only the Uparkot cave has ancient carvings on its walls and pillars.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/Uparkot-Fort.jpg",
