@@ -1790,7 +1790,7 @@
             "title": "Jammu & Kashmir- Handicrafts- Wood",
             "name": "Jammu & Kashmir- Handicrafts- Wood",
             "desc": "Today, Kashmir is also known throughout the world for its exquisite crafts which are as diverse as woolen textiles, hand-woven carpets, to the intricately designed papier-mâché, wood work, silverware, etc. Beautifully crafted objects out of walnut including superbly carved furniture items such as wall hangings, jewellery boxes, screens, room dividers, fruit bowls, trays, etc. are made by expert craftsmen. It is a wonderful combination of chiselling and carving. The exquisite lattice work on screens resembles marble lattice work done on marble.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Jammu_Kashmir/craft-wood-screen.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Jammu_Kashmir/craft-wood-screen.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -1799,7 +1799,7 @@
             "title": "Jammu & Kashmir- Handicrafts- Wool",
             "name": "Jammu & Kashmir- Handicrafts- Wool",
             "desc": "Pashmina, Shahtoosh and Jamawar are the best known among Kashmirs superbly woven woollen shawls. The weaving and dyeing of these shawls has a lengthy process, which require great precision. Pattern drawing is transferred to a graph done by highly skilled craftsmen. Many shawls have weaving on both sides called do rukha. The colour combinations are fantastic and the woven designs are brilliantly complemented by superb embroidery.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Jammu_Kashmir/shawl.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Jammu_Kashmir/shawl.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -1808,7 +1808,7 @@
             "title": "Papier Mâché Artist",
             "name": "Papier Mâché Artist",
             "desc": "The craft of Papier Mache involves the use of paper pulp for creating beautiful painted objects by craftsmen in lifelike images inspired from nature. Known locally as Kari Kamandari, Kashmirs outstanding papier mâché work has a glossy surface on which floral motifs such as the chinar leaf, iris, rose, tulips, hyacinth and arabesques are painted with great finesse and delicacy in sparkling tones. The creation of a papier-mache object has two processes - the sakhtsazi (making the object) and the naqashi (painting the surface). Papier Mache Photo Gallery",
-            "img": "/state-images/Jammu_Kashmir/papier-mache-1.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Jammu_Kashmir/papier-mache-1.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Medieval",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -1817,7 +1817,7 @@
             "title": "Jammu & Kashmir- Handicrafts- textiles",
             "name": "Jammu & Kashmir- Handicrafts- textiles",
             "desc": "Traditional hand knotted silk carpets in India can be found in Kashmir. The origins of these traditional silk carpets can be traced to the Mughal period. Do you know that to complete a large silk carpet takes about one year of intensive labour. The process of creating a hand knotted silk carpet is lengthy and a lot of preparation has to be made by the craftsman before weaving the carpet. The yarn used for weaving determines the type of the carpet to be created. Woolen carpets are always created on a cotton base with woolen yarn. Silk yarn is sometimes used in woolen carpets as highlights on certain designs and motifs. The most recurring motif that is found on these carpets is the tree of life. The natural beauty of Kashmir and its picturesque views have inspired the carpet weavers. Other popular themes are: hunting scenes, bird and animal motifs and historical themes. These carpets come in a variety of colors and designs. Carpets from Kashmir are unique because they are made with yarn dyed with mineral colors. Hues of red, blue and yellow color are mainly used. Two very different and interesting floor coverings are namdas and gabbas of Kashmir. They are both very attractive and distinctive in its own way. The base of namda is a locally prepared felt. Highly decorative designs are embroidered and created with appliqué work, sometimes by laying dyed wool and pressing it on the surface. The designs are mostly floral and geometrical. Gabba is a rug made from old woollens on which different coloured cut out forms are secured with chain stitch. The edges and the central portion are covered with large embroidery. They usually have bright flashes of design against a sombre background.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Jammu_Kashmir/Namdaa.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Jammu_Kashmir/Namdaa.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -1847,7 +1847,7 @@
           "title": "Ruins of Sun Temple, Martand",
           "name": "Ruins of Sun Temple, Martand",
           "desc": "Martand temple is dedicated to the Sun God. This eight century example of stone architecture reflects the influence of Gandhara style and local wooden prototypes conforming to the wooden model. The temple has steep sloping roofs.",
