           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -1883,7 +1883,7 @@
           "title": "Jammu & Kashmir- Monument- Alchi",
           "name": "Jammu & Kashmir- Monument- Alchi",
           "desc": "Ladakh has many historic monasteries called Gompas, where Buddhist Bhikkus (Bhikshus) and Bhikshunies have been living, studying and practicing their religion. Located in scenic locations the monasteries of Ladakh are, on hills and mountains and have rich collections of Buddhist Thangka paintings, art and artifacts. Alchi, a village in the Leh district is famous for one of the oldest monasteries in Ladakh, mainly known for its wall paintings of the eleventh century reflecting artistic and spiritual details of both Buddhism and Hindu kings of that time in Kashmir. The monastery was built by Lotsawa Rinchen Zangpo. At Alchi there are three shrines; the main one called Dukhang or Assembly Hall,the three-storeyed Sumtseg, and the Manjushri. There are also the votive stupas called the great Chörten and the small Chörten. Dukhang is the oldest monument preserved at Alchi. The entrance to the temple is through a courtyard and an elaborately carved doorway with panels of tiny Bodhisattvas and guardian figures. The hall has a niche at the back with a plaster image of Vairocana. The hall is painted with different mandalas. The entrance is flanked by an only partly preserved Life of the Buddha and a large depiction dedicated to donors and their the sides of the Alchi Photo Gallery niche is a depiction of attendant figures, feasts. Below the thousand Buddhas painted on animals and sacred symbols. Sumtseg, is dedicated to a triad of Bodhisattvas and their secondary deities located in three niches. The facade is carved in wood consisting of pillars, carved brackets, and capitals. Light penetrates through open shafts on each floor. The largest Bodhisattva on the back wall, Maitreya, is flanked by Avalokiteshvara and Manjushri. Besides these each niche has four secondary deities and two flying goddesses .Each of the Bodhisattvas are dressed in beautifully decorated garments. The Manjushri Temple is square with a large central platform. It supports the four pillars holding the diagonally arranged cross-beams of the ceiling. The four images of Manjushri are seated back to back in an elaborate common frame crowded various images of animals, gods and symbols and topped by the decorative scrolls deriving from the tails of the makaras. The Hemis Gompa is the biggest monastery in Ladakh.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Jammu_Kashmir/alchi-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Jammu_Kashmir/alchi-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "1000-1100",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -1892,7 +1892,7 @@
           "title": "Jammu & Kashmir- Monument- Thicksey Monastery",
           "name": "Jammu & Kashmir- Monument- Thicksey Monastery",
           "desc": "One of the finest examples of architecture from Ladakh, Thicksey Monastery is one of the most important monasteries. It houses one of the largest Buddhist statues, the 15 meter tall Champa, the Buddha of Future. About a hundred monks live here who follow traditional ways of running the monastery and teach monks and villagers. This Gompa is situated on the top of the hill and the 12-storey monastery complex contains numerous stupas, statues, thangkas, wall paintings, swords and a large pillar",
-          "img": "/state-images/Jammu_Kashmir/thiksey-monastery.jpg",
