@@ -1945,7 +1945,7 @@
             "title": "Chhou dance",
             "name": "Chhou dance",
             "desc": "is performed in the Jharkhand. This dance has evolved during the course of interactions with other neighboring cultures of West Bengal and Bihar. It is a dance mostly done in open field and in the night. The dance arena is generally surrounded by fire poles .The artists go for a bath and puja before performing this dance as the characters they play are of Gods. The Chhou dancers use colourful decorative masks of big size. These masks are made of paper mache', light wood and paper. Chhou naach is a tribal form of nritya natika (dance- drama) where various characters with Chhou masks play mythological characters. Most of these plays are based on epics of Mahabharat and Ramayan which makes it very spiritual and respectful. The characters of Chhou dance wear traditions ornaments and dance with weapons. Their clothes are very colourful. This dance involves lots of gymnastic actions in the dance sequences and most of the characters are very aggressive in nature. This dance drama is supported by traditions nagada (drums) and flute as background sound and rhythm of dance action varies with the modulations of drum beats.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Jharkhand/dance21.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Jharkhand/dance21.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           }
@@ -2002,7 +2002,7 @@
             "title": "Karnataka- Performing Art- Yakshagaana",
             "name": "Karnataka- Performing Art- Yakshagaana",
             "desc": "Yakshagaana is believed to have a history of one thousand years and is believed to represent a specific type of music of the yakhas. Yaksha are the demi-gods attending on 'Kubera'- the god of wealth in the Hindu mythology. Gaana means song. By combining the meaning, the term Yakshagaana might mean the 'song of Yakshas'. Of the twenty districts in Karnataka, Yakshagana has established its firm roots in the districts of Dakshina Kannada, Uttara Kannada, Dharwad, Mysore and Hassan. The epics of Ramayana and Mahabharata offer extensive material for Yakshagana prasangas (or themes). The literature is presented both in verse and prose and the former is interpreted in simple prose and dialogues. The show begins with an announcement of Yakshagana by beating the drum, in important places of the village. Yakshagana is performed in a rectangular stage built with four wooden poles installed on four corners and covered by mats of palm leaves on the top. Away from the backstage is a small place called 'chowki', the greenroom for the artistes. The audience sits on all the three sides of the platform and a curtain covers only the back stage. In the Yakshagana , the Bhagavata is the chief narrator of the story, who recites it in verse form. Bhagavata also conducts light hearted conversation with the Vidushaka- the jester. The dialogues between these two characters is very amusing and the Bhagavata ably supports the Vidushaka in carrying out extempore dialogues. Music is the essence of Yakshagana and the narration is in verse form. It is the songs and poems which transmit the story and the songs are invariably sung by the Bhagavata. The music is excellent and synchronizes well with the costume and make up of the characters. Prose in Yakshagana is in the form of dialogues. The songs sung by the Bhagavata are at a very high pitch and hence is considered as a distinct style of music. The rhythm that dictates the body movements, footwork and the performance actually flows from the musical instruments.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/yakshagaana1.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/yakshagaana1.webp",
             "historical_significance": "",
             "materials": ""
           }
@@ -2013,7 +2013,7 @@
             "title": "Karnataka-Handicrafts- Chennapatna",
             "name": "Karnataka-Handicrafts- Chennapatna",
             "desc": "Chennapatna is well known for its unusual wooden lacquered toys and products. These craftsmen called chitragars are involved in this traditional craft since ages and make beautiful toys like a grinding stone, mortar, pestle and rolling pin, as well as a well to hold water. Other creations include Wood turning requires extremely close grained and moderately hard wood and Hale wood is the most suited wood found in this region. The required size and shape of wood is cut, seasoned, attached on to a lathe and turned in order to smoothen it. Chisels are used to shape the revolving piece of wood and flaws are sandpapered away with. The lacquering of the surface is done by rubbing lac sticks into the revolving wood and imprinting it with lacs in reds, yellows, greens, blacks and mauve. Plain lacquering is favoured though abri or cloud work, zig-zags, stripes, and dana or the spotted technique are also used.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/Chennapatna-toys-copy.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/Chennapatna-toys-copy.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -2022,7 +2022,7 @@
             "title": "Karnataka- Handicrafts- Sandalwood Carving",
             "name": "Karnataka- Handicrafts- Sandalwood Carving",
             "desc": "Karnataka has a huge forest-belt and sandalwood carvers are found in Bangalore, Mysore, Shimoga, Honavar and Kumta on the coast. Sandalwood is of great religious importance and is an extremely expensive material for carving artifacts. Sandalwood is of two types: srigandha which is close grained and yellowish-brown in colour and used for carving; and nagagandha which is darkish-brown in colour and from which oil is extracted. Sandalwood is a rich, golden brown wood that gives off a fragrance. The wood is cultured and used to make a plethora of products that range from idols of deities and finely twisted chariots to decorative pieces such as paper cutters, walking sticks, trays, boxes and elephant figures. Jali or fret work with patterns in high and low relief are generally seen on them.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/mysore-sandalwood-karnataka.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/mysore-sandalwood-karnataka.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -2040,7 +2040,7 @@
             "title": "Karnataka- Handicrafts- Rosewood/ Wood Carving Inlay work",
             "name": "Karnataka- Handicrafts- Rosewood/ Wood Carving Inlay work",
             "desc": "Mysore and Bangalore are the main centers for rosewood carving and artists make beautiful decoration and utility objects from this. There are temples and palaces which have ancestral art of wood carving on the bracketed pillars, door frames and arches. Rosewood furniture and elephants fitures are the products which are always in high demand. Wood inlay is the process of decorating the surface of wood by setting in pieces of material such as ivory, bone, plastic, or wood of different colours. This craft is also concentrated in Mysore and Bangalore where its roots can be traced back to a family by the name of Mirza Yusuf Ali who was pioneer in the field. The artisan smoothens the base of the rosewood and the design is traced and etched into the surface. The several components of the inlay are painstakingly assembled to match and fit exactly into the grooves and are then glued in. The design is finished by obtaining the required shades with several coats of polish. Examples of some famous inlay work in Karnataka include the ivory inlay in rosewood and ebony in the Srirangapatnam mausoleum; the doors of the Amba Vilas palace in Mysore are also fine examples of inlay. Products with inlay include plates, boxes, bowls, cigarette cases, and figures of animals, especially elephants, which continue to be popular. The designs include floral and geometric patterns, landscapes, pastoral scenes, processions, and scenes from the epics.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/woodinlay.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/woodinlay.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           }
@@ -2052,7 +2052,7 @@
           "title": "Bidar fort",
           "name": "Bidar fort",
           "desc": "is a fifteenth century fort which was the capital of the Bahamani dynasty. It is surrounded by a triple-moated wall, with intricate battlements and an imposing gateway. Though the ruins are crumbling the bastions and gates, royal baths and kitchens, audience halls, and pleasure pavilions stand as witnesses to Bidar's past glory. The Rang Mahal has elaborately carved wooden pillars and Persian couplets engraved in tiles and exquisite mother-of-pearl inlay work. The Solah Khambha Mosque was originally designed as a Jami masjid.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/bidarfort1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/bidarfort1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1400-1450",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2061,7 +2061,7 @@
           "title": "Karnataka- Monument- Pattadakal",
           "name": "Karnataka- Monument- Pattadakal",
           "desc": "With its beautifully chiselled temples, this World Heritage Site on the banks of the Malaprabha is reminiscent of the richness of Chalukyan architecture. Pattadakal reached its glory under the Chalukya kings and was once used as a ceremonial centre where kings were crowned and commemorated. It has a cluster of ten major temples, which reflect the maturity of architectural style in the Badami and Aihole regions. At the entrance of the site, are the eight century temples of Jambulinga, Kadasiddeshvara, and Galaganatha with their curvilinear shikaras or spires. The biggest temple, dedicated to Shiva, Virupaksha, has a huge gateway, several inscriptions, and a profusion of friezes from the epics. Facing the temple is a pavilion containing a massive Nandi. The style of the large Virupaksha temple is a forerunner of the Chalukya style.This is the only temple that is still used for worship. Parts of the temple predate the Vijayanagar empire. The temple, with nine storied gopuram towers above the other structure at Hampi. The ceiling of the rangamandapa is beautifully painted with scenes from the Puranas. The Mallikarjuna Temple is similar to Virupaksha Temple, in design but smaller in size.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/pattadakal1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/pattadakal1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Ancient 600-750",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2079,7 +2079,7 @@
           "title": "Karnataka- Monument- Halebid",
           "name": "Karnataka- Monument- Halebid",
           "desc": "Halebid, the ancient capital of the Hoysalas was earlier known as Dvarsamudra. The Hoysaleswara temple, perched on a star-shaped base amidst lawns, is a sculptural extravaganza. It reflects the Hoysala style in all its grandeur. Its walls are richly carved with an endless variety of Hindu deities, sages, stylised animals, birds, and friezes depicting the life of the Hoysala kings. The temple platform is carved in horizontal bands. Musicians, animals, mythical beasts and humans, have all been sculpted in a row above another band of sculptures in the same way. Even the eaves are densely carved. The tower over the vimana is broken. The temple has one Shiva shrine and another dedicated to Santaleswara, two mandapas and two Nandi shrines.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/halebid1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/halebid1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Ancient 001-600 CE 600-750 CE Early Medieval 1200-1300",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2088,7 +2088,7 @@
           "title": "Karnataka- Monument- Badami",
           "name": "Karnataka- Monument- Badami",
           "desc": "Badami is situated at the mouth of a ravine between two rocky hills. The exquisite sculptures and the red sandstone cliffs of Badami boast of the four ancient rock-cut caves on top of a hill. They are filled with carved pillars and bracket figures. The largest of them is the third cave, dedicated to Lord Vishnu. The sculptural wonder here is the eighteen-armed Nataraja striking eighty one dance poses in the first cave overlooking the caves is the Agastya Teertha Tank. Its banks are dotted with a cluster of Bhoothanatha temples.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/badami1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/badami1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Ancient 001-600 CE 600-750 CE",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2097,7 +2097,7 @@
           "title": "Karnataka- Monument- Aihole",
           "name": "Karnataka- Monument- Aihole",
           "desc": "Aihole is considered as the cradle of Hindu temple architecture. There are hundreds of temples in the villages and fields nearby. Durga temple is the most impressive one with its semicircular apse, elevated plinth, and the gallery encircling the sanctum. The Ladkhan Temple, which is one of the earliest temples, was originally a royal assembly hall and marriage mandapa chosen as the abode of a Muslim prince, Lad Khan. The Huchimalli temple with a sculpture of Vishnu sitting atop a large cobra, the Ravanphadi cave temple celebrating the many forms of Shiva, the Konti temple complex, the Umamaheswari temple, the Jain Meguti Temple, and the two-storied Buddhist Temple are the other noteworthy monuments.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/aihole1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/aihole1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Ancient 001-600 CE 600-750 CE Early Medieval 750-1200",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2106,7 +2106,7 @@
           "title": "Karnataka- Monument- Lakkundi",
           "name": "Karnataka- Monument- Lakkundi",
           "desc": "Lakkundi has fifty stunning temples and twenty nine inscriptions dating back to the Chalukya period. The most ornate and spectacular of these is the Kashi Vishwanatha temple. The Jain temple dedicated to Mahavira is one of the largest and oldest shrines here. Lakkundi is also noted for its step wells, artistically built with small, canopied niches inside the walls of several wells that enshrine lingas.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/lakkundi1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/lakkundi1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Ancient 750-1200 CE",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2115,7 +2115,7 @@
           "title": "Karnataka- Monument- Bijapur-Gol Gumbaz",
           "name": "Karnataka- Monument- Bijapur-Gol Gumbaz",
           "desc": "The one-time capital of the Adil Shahi kings (1489-1686) is filled with mosques, mausoleums, palaces, forts, watchtowers, and strong gateways, with the massive Gol Gumbaz dominating the landscape. One of the most impressive architectural achievements of the Deccan, is the mausoleum of Muhammad Adil Shah. The tomb is set in a large walled garden, in the Persian style. This path bisects the garden. On either side are ruins of buildings made of dark bricks. This magnificent mausoleum of Mohammed Adil houses the world's second largest dome. Unsupported by pillars it is an architectural wonder. Built in 1659, its most striking features are the seven storied octagonal pillars at the four corners and the heavy bracketed cornice bellow the parapet. The Gol Gumbaz is an enormous cube of stone and masonry. The amazing whispering gallery distinctly echos the faintiest whisper eleven times.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/golgumbaz.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/golgumbaz.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1650-1700",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2124,7 +2124,7 @@
           "title": "Karnataka- Monument- Bijapur-Tomb of Ibrahim Rauza",
           "name": "Karnataka- Monument- Bijapur-Tomb of Ibrahim Rauza",
           "desc": "Said to have inspired the Taj Mahal in Agra the palatial Mosque and tomb of Ibrahim Rauza is very impressive. The mausoleum has a mosque and four graceful minarets within a rectangular enclosure and can be accessed by a lofty entrance tower. The mausoleum designed by Malik Sandal was surrounded by a royal garden during the Shahi rule.The facade of the mosque has five arches with elaborate plasterwork on the spandrels. The mosque has graceful arches, aisle halls, intricate designs and large crowning onion dome. This spherical dome is raised on a high neck decorated with long petals. What makes it even more special is the priceless Koran written in letters of gold that is carefully preserved here.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/bijapur.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/bijapur.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Medieval 1600-1650",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2133,7 +2133,7 @@
           "title": "Gulbarga Fort",
           "name": "Gulbarga Fort",
           "desc": "The Gulbarga Fort was originally built by Raja Gulchand and later fortified by Ala-ud-din Bahman. The fort contains large buildings, mosques, temples, stables, ammunition dumps, carriages, fifteen towers, twenty six guns, and several beautiful courtyards. The most beautiful of the sprawling fort is the thirty eight thousand square feet Jumma Masjid with its elegant domes and arched columns. The crumbling ruins of Gulbarga Fort, the Sultan Hasan's tomb, the tomb of Ghias-ud-din, the fine mausoleum of Feroze Shah and family, the Haft-Gumbaz reminds one of Karnataka's glorious past, when kingdoms fell and others rose from their ashes.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/gulbarga-fort.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/gulbarga-fort.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1200-1300",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2142,7 +2142,7 @@
           "title": "Mysore Palace",
           "name": "Mysore Palace",
           "desc": "The original Mysore palace built of wood, got burnt down in 1897 and was rebuilt for the twenty fourth Wodeyar king in 1912. Designed by the well-known British architect, Henry Irwin, the palace is a treasure house of exquisite carvings. Built in Indo-Saracenic style with domes, turrets, arches, and colonnades, the Mysore palace is a treasure house of works of art from all over the world. Intricately carved doors open into luxuriously furnished rooms. The majestic Durbar hall has an ornate ceiling and many sculpted pillars. The marriage pavilion is adorned with glazed tile flooring, stained glass windows and domed ceilings. The walled palace complex houses the residential museum, temples, and shrines, including the Shwetha Varahaswamy temple. The magnificently jewel-studded golden throne, the",
-          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/mysorepalace1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/mysorepalace1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Modern 1900-1947",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2151,7 +2151,7 @@
           "title": "Karnataka- Monument- Somnathpur",
           "name": "Karnataka- Monument- Somnathpur",
           "desc": "Situated in the village of Somnathpur, the exquisitely carved, star-shaped temple with triple towers is a perfect example of Hoysala architecture. This place boasts of one of the last and the grandest of Hoysala monuments - the Kesava temple. By the year 1268 CE, the year in which the Kesava temple at Somnathpur was built. The riches and splendour of the Hoysala court were already evident in their grand temples at Belur and Dvarasamudra (present day, Halebid). The Hoysala temple at Somnathpur, is truly unique in design, perfect in proportion and the stone carvings are striking marvels in stone. The shrine stands in the centre of a walled compound, around which runs an open verandah with sixty four cells. The temple itself, stellar in shape, has three elaborately carved pinnacles with a common Navranga and stands on a raised platform. The three sanctums once housed beautifully carved idols of Kesava, Janardhana and Venugopala. Today the idol of Lord Kesava is missing, but the other two still adorn the sanctums in their original form. The friezes on its outer walls with their intricately carved rows of elephants, charging horsemen, and mythological birds and beasts leaves one spellbound. Beautifullysculpted images of gods, goddesses, and scenes from the epics, as well as the remarkably ornate ceilings in the pillared hall are very beautiful. At the temple, the outer walls are decorated with a series of star-shaped folds and the entire surface is covered with carved stone plaques. The walls above the plinth are also carved with exquisite figures of gods and goddesses, taken from the Hindu puranas, and meticulously arranged in vertical panels.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/somnathpur1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/somnathpur1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1200-1300",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2160,7 +2160,7 @@
           "title": "Karnataka  Monument- Shravanbelagola",
           "name": "Karnataka  Monument- Shravanbelagola",
           "desc": "Shravanbelagola, is one of the most important Jain pilgrim centres of India. The statue of the Jain saint Gommateshwara (981 CE) is one of the tallest and most graceful statues in the world. It is carved out of a mountain and is situated in the Indragiri hills. The colossal monolithic statue is fifty eight feet high, with twenty six ft wide shoulders, ten feet of its feet. Starkingly simple, the beautifully chiseled features of this statue embody serenity. Nearly 1,800 years old, the statue which is reached by climbing seven hundred steps carved in the steep granite slope, is symbolic of the renunciation of worldly possessions. It was sculpted by Aristanemi in 981 CE and Chamunda Raya, a general and minister of the Ganga King Rachamatta installed it in 983 CE. Lord Gomatesheshwara was the Jain prince Bahubali. During a war with his greedy elder brother, Bharata who wanted to grab the kingdom, Bahubali accepted defeat at the moment of his victory when he realized the futility of it all. He renounced the world and left to lead a life of penance and meditation, attaining nirvana. Thousands of devotees congregate here to perform the Mahamastakabhisheka. It is a spectacular ceremony which is the focus for Jain pilgrims across India, held once in twelve years. Hundreds of pots containing curds, milk, honey, vermilion, coconut water, turmeric paste and even gold and precious jewels are poured over the statue's head by priests. In addition to the statue, there are several Jain temples and monasteries in Shravanabelagola and it attained historical importance when Chandragupta Maurya, the greatest King of Mauryan Empire, settled on this hill in third century BCE, along with his Guru Bhagwan Bhadra Bahu Swami, after renouncing his kingdom.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/sbelagola1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/sbelagola1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 750-1200",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2169,7 +2169,7 @@
           "title": "Mysore Palace",
           "name": "Mysore Palace",
           "desc": "Although Dussera is celebrated all over India, it has special significance in Mysore. Dussera-ravana-kolam as it is called, is celebrated for ten days to symbolize the victory of goddess Chamundeswari (Durga) over the demon Mahishasura. i.e. the victory of the good over the evil. Mysore palace is fully illuminated for a whole month. On the last day, bands accompanied by a colourfully bedecked elephant carrying the statue of the goddess starts from the palace as a procession with palace chariots and units of the army through the city to Banni mandap (where Banni tree is worshipped. In the evening, there is a torchlight parade and a great display of fireworks. The magnificent ruined city of Hampi, which was once the capital of the Vijayanagar Empire was considered one of the greatest empires in the history of India. Vijayanagara became most powerful during the reigns of Krishnadevaraya and Achyutadevaraya when opulent palaces, marvellous temples, massive fortifications, baths, markets, aquaducts, pavilions, stables for royal elephants, and elegantly carved pillars were all built. This was a city whose merchants offered diamonds, pearls, fine silks, brocades and horses. This was also the period of greatest architectural activity, as both these rulers commissioned several buildings in the royal capital and in other parts of their vast empire. Most of the sites were early sixteenth century, during the twenty year reign of Krishna Deva Raya (1509 -1529). It has ruins of stone temples, elephant stables, barracks and palaces. Temples of this city are noted for their large dimensions, florid ornamentation, bold and delicate carvings, stately pillars, magnificent pavilions etc. Some of the beautiful monuments in Hampi are made out of a brilliant mix of the Hindu and Islamic style of architecture, popularly called as the Indo- Sarasenic architecture. This World Heritage Site has two important sites the royal Centre and the Sacred Centre. The Royal centre contains structures that seem to have been palaces, baths, pavilions, royal stables, and temples for ceremonial use. The Sacred Centre is situated on the northern edge of the city along the banks of the holy Tungabhadra river. The Queens bath which is a plain looking building from outside has an elaborate interior with a giant tank at the center. The corridor around has arches, domes and balconies which makes it look more like a palatial structure than a bath. The Kings Palace is the largest and includes two major structures an underground chamber which must have served as a treasury or private audience platform has densely carved bands of horses, soldiers and depiction of the various aspects of courtly life. Hazara Rama Temple was a royal temple. The entire temple is embellished with bas-reliefs depicting the scenes from the Ramayana. The walls are richly carved with friezes depicting processions of horses, elephants, dancing girls and soldiers attired in splendid weaponry. Vithala Temple in Hampi has a magnificent stone chariot standing in the temple courtyard with an equally impressive rangamandapa with fifxty six musical pillars that resound the musical chimes when struck.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/mysore-dasara.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/mysore-dasara.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
           "materials": ""
         }
@@ -2202,7 +2202,7 @@
             "title": "Bamboo Mat Painting",
             "name": "Bamboo Mat Painting",
             "desc": "Bamboo mat painting is one of the major handicrafts industries of Kerala. The local artists are skilled in this traditional art. Their work is first initiated by placing the correct size of mat which is utilized as a canvas for the painting. Then the basic coating is done on the mat which is then followed by the design on it. In these bamboo mat paintings the craftsmen depicts religious images, images of the birds and animals, human images as well as capture beauty of nature. These are highlighted with bright colours. The artists design all these paintings in different sizes which gives a very colorful look that is beautifully presented in appealing bamboo reed frames.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Kerala/bamboo-mat.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Kerala/bamboo-mat.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           }
@@ -2213,7 +2213,7 @@
             "title": "Martial arts",
             "name": "Martial arts",
             "desc": "Kerala is famous for its diverse martial arts such as. Kalaripayattu, Parisa Kali, Velakanni. Kalaripayattu is the oldest existing martial art form in the world which dates back more than 2000 years. It is believed to be the forerunner of popularly known Chinese martial arts, originally from India, theBuddhist monk Bodhidharma took this knowledge from India to China. Said to have originated from the Dhanur Vedic texts, the practice of Kalaripayattu encompasses all fighting arts and is described by the Vishnu Purana as one of the eighteen traditional branches of knowledge. Kalaris are the schools where training in this martial art form is imparted by masters. Kalaripayattu requires a unique harmony of mind and body along with energy, flexibility of the body and sharp focus of the mind. Kalaripayattu involves increasing the flexibility of the body. The student learns to twist, jump and flex his/her arms that will help when learning the next stage. The second stage involves learning how to handle wooden sticks and other wooden weapons. The third stage involves handling metal weapons. The final stage involves fighting with bare hands. The students are taught arm locks, wrestling and defense. The Parisa Kali of north Malabar and Velakanni of Travancore are two other martial arts forms of Kerala, which involve considerable physical training and knowledge of the use of arms. Velakanni represents the battle between Pandavas and Kauravas at Kurukshetra, and is played during the temple festival at Cherthala.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Kerala/kalaripayattu.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Kerala/kalaripayattu.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -2222,7 +2222,7 @@
             "title": "Kerala- Performing Art- Dance- Kathakali",
             "name": "Kerala- Performing Art- Dance- Kathakali",
             "desc": "Kathakali is the most popular dance Kathakali drama form of Kerala. The word Kathakali literally means story-play and it is known for its elaborate makeup and exquisite costumes have become the most acknowledged symbols for Kerala. The themes of Kathakali are religious and mythological and generally deal with the Mahabarata, the Ramayana and Puranas. Kathakali is usually performed by men and female characters are also portrayed by men dressed in women's costume. However, in recent years, women have also started becoming Kathakali dancers. Kathakalis tradition dates back to the seventheen century. It was given its present form by Mahakavi Vallathol Narayan Menon, who was the founder of the Kerala Kala Mandalam. The actors rely very heavily on hand gesture or mudra and eye movements to convey the story. Usually the large and heavy costumers of Kathakali are of different kinds meant for each character such as the hero, heroine or the villain who are instantly recognisable by their characteristic makeup and costume. The elaborate makeup, using different colors is more like a mask than makeup in the usual sense. The white is made fromrice flour, the red is made from vermilion (a red earth such as cinnabar). The black is made from soot. The colors are not merely decoration, but symbolize the characters. For instance, red on the feet is used to represent evil characters and evil meanings.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Kerala/kathakali.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Kerala/kathakali.webp",
             "historical_significance": "",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -2231,7 +2231,7 @@
             "title": "Kerala- Performing Art- Dance- Mohini Attam",
             "name": "Kerala- Performing Art- Dance- Mohini Attam",
             "desc": "Mohini Attam is a dance form of Kerala performed only by women. The typical costume for Mohini Attam is generally simple white, or off-white saree with a golden Zari or red border, worn in a typical style. One of the most characteristic signs of the Mohini Attam dancer is the bun of hair worn off-centre. The dance is based on the myth of Mohini. The story is about how Vishnu decides to foil the demons in their plans to steal the churned out of ocean. He takes the form of a beautiful apsara, and by way of her amorous charms distracts the demons from stealing the amrit or the celestial ambrosia that gives immortality and power. The dance is slow, and extremely lyrical in style.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Kerala/mohiniattam.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Kerala/mohiniattam.webp",
             "historical_significance": "",
             "materials": ""
           }
@@ -2242,7 +2242,7 @@
             "title": "Coconut shell, Fibre and Stem Craft",
             "name": "Coconut shell, Fibre and Stem Craft",
             "desc": "Coconut trees grow all over Kerala, and all pasts of the tree are use for making various prodence. Products like bowls, vases, tea pots and hookahs are crafted from coconut shell. Lamp stands encased in brass and smaller coconut shell articles are also made in Trivandrum, Attingal and Neyyatinkara, while larger items are made in Quilandy in Kozhikode district in north Kerala. Often hookahs and large vases are also made by combining coconut shells with brass bindings. Coconut fibre is cleaned, smoothened and made into various dolls and toys with beads and coloured threads to give it a decorative appearance.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Kerala/coconut-craft.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Kerala/coconut-craft.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -2251,7 +2251,7 @@
             "title": "Kerala Handicrafts - Aranmula Metal Mirror",
             "name": "Kerala Handicrafts - Aranmula Metal Mirror",
             "desc": "Aranmula, a small village in Kerala is renowned for making special type of metallic mirrors with bell metal, an alloy of copper and tin. It is believed that a divine visitation inspired a widow of this village to discover a mysterious blend of bronze which could be waxed bright into crystal clear mirror. These mirrors are known as Aranmula Kannadi. This is the only mirror of its type, which reflects from the front. It is believed that owning such a mirror is a lifetime possession which brings good luck and brings prosperity and keeps evil eyes away. A technique similar to lost wax method is used to make moulds of these mirrors with the local mud from the field. Once the casting is done, the surface is polished with oil and some metallic powder. The polish may continue for several days to get the required surface. Kannans, the artisans of this unique type of bell-metal are only a handful of familes in the area, who retain till date, this art of getting such reflection from metal.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Kerala/Aranmula_mirror1.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Kerala/Aranmula_mirror1.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -2260,7 +2260,7 @@
             "title": "Textiles",
             "name": "Textiles",
             "desc": "The plain white Karaikudi saris with a woven gold band on the borders and the pallu are very famous.The Balaramapuram sari woven in Thiruvananthapuram district have use of pure zari for border designs. These saris are processed on the traditional looms with the yarn count of 100 for the wrap and weft is also used.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Kerala/balaramapuram-sari.jpg",
