@@ -2272,7 +2272,7 @@
           "title": "Padmanabhaswami Temple",
           "name": "Padmanabhaswami Temple",
           "desc": "This temple, dedicated to Padmanabha (Vishnu), is located inside the fort area or the old city in Thiruvananthpuram having a blend of architectural styles of Tamilnadu and Kerala. The entrance has a Gopuram which is a common feature from Tamilnadu while the other entrances with tiled roofs on wooden structures are typical of Kerala. The circumambulatory has a typical Nadambalam which is basically an enclosure for the temple with a wooden roof, latticed walls and carved pillars. The carvings depict the deepalakshmis or women holding lamps. The sanctum known as srikovil is double storeyed and square in shape. The mandapa is made of stone which again depicts the Tamilnadu style of architecture.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Kerala/padmanabhaswamy.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Kerala/padmanabhaswamy.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2281,7 +2281,7 @@
           "title": "Kerala- Monument- The Paradesi Synagogue-Kochi (Cochin)",
           "name": "Kerala- Monument- The Paradesi Synagogue-Kochi (Cochin)",
           "desc": "The Paradesi Synagogue The grand and glorious synagogue known as Pardesi synagogue named after the foreigners who were the Jews from Spain and Portugal. This is one of the oldest synagogues built in the sixteenth century, being the only one in use even today out of the seven built in the Jew town. It was the king of Cochin, Paraja, who gave a piece of land to the Jewish community next to his palace to build the synagogue. It was reconstructed in 1662, after the Portuguese bombardment. Outside the synagogue, there are wrought-iron gates decorated with the Star of David. The rectangular building is white-walled and tile-roofed, with an inner courtyard lined with ancient Hebrew-inscribed grave stones. There is a Dutch-style square clock tower with four clocks featuring four different numerals: numerals facing the synagogue. Roman numerals, Malayalam numerals,and Arabic numerals facing the rest of thethree sides towards the public . At the bottom of the tower there is a well and inside the tower hangs the pendulum. The floor is paved with hundreds of old hand-painted porcelain tiles brought from China and no two of which are identical. The synagogue has a rich collection of gold and silver decorated Torah-scrolls crowned with solid gold and set with gems given as gifts by the kings, an oriental carpet and two brass columns symbolizing the pillars of the temple. In addition to this there is a collection of 10 paintings depicting the history of the Jews in the area.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Kerala/paradesi-synagogue.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Kerala/paradesi-synagogue.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Medieval 1600-1650",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2290,7 +2290,7 @@
           "title": "Mattancherry Palace",
           "name": "Mattancherry Palace",
           "desc": "The Mattancherry Palace is also known as the Dutch palace. It was built by the Portuguese in 1557 and presented to Raja Veera Kerala Varma of Kochi. The palace was renovated in 1663 by the Dutch and it took on its present popular name Dutch palace after 1663, when the Dutch carried out some extensions and renovations in the palace. The palace has two floors and a central courtyard which is in the traditional Kerala style of architecture known as nalukettus. The palace has a European character from the outside with scraped masonry walls and round-headed windows and doors but its sloping tiled roof and wooden balconies are local in features. The two arches at the entrance are typically Portuguese in character. Inside the inner court, there stands a small temple dedicated to Bhagavati, the protective goddess of the Kochi royal family. Two more temples are situated on either side of the Palace dedicated to Lord Krishna and Lord Siva. The interiors of the palace arepanelled with wood and has exhibits of the kings of Kochi like ceremonial robes, headdresses, weapons, palanquins, furniture but the main feature is the series of astonishing murals, depicting scenes from the Ramayana, the Mahabharata, and the Puranic legends connected with Shiva, Vishnu, Krishna, Kumara, and Durga painted on the walls as well as the portraits of the kings. The paintings cover a wide range of themes from the Puthra Kameshti Yagyam to Rama's return to Ayodhya after vanquishing Ravana. The paintings totally cover nearly 1000 sq.ft in area and date from as early as the 16th century through mid nineteenth century and are found in the chambers on the west side of the palace, in four chambers upstairs, and in two low ceiling rooms that are entered by a steep stairway. These beautiful and extensive paintings are fine examples of Kerala mural paintings best known for its unique style. The king's room is covered in about 48 paintings illustrating the Ramayana, from the beginning of the sacrifice of Dasaratha to Sita's return from captivity in Lanka. These paintings are the earliest in the palace, dating as early as 16th century. The last five scenes are from the Krishna Lila. Another important series of paintings in the Dutch palace is found in the upper rooms, notably that of the coronation hall that was made under Dutch patronage. Among the compositions are Lakshmi seated on the lotus, sleeping Vishnu (Ananthasayanamurti), Ardhanariswara and other goddesses, the coronation of Rama, Krishna lifting Mount Govardhana etc. The ladies chambers depict scenes from Kumarasambhavam and other works of the great Sanskrit poet Kalidasa. The dining hall has carved wooden ornate ceiling decorated with a series of brass cups. The palace also contains rare examples of traditional Kerala flooring, which looks like polished black marble but is actually a mixture of burned coconut shells, charcoal, lime, plant juices and egg whites.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Kerala/mattancherry.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Kerala/mattancherry.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Medieval 1550-1600",
           "materials": ""
         }
@@ -2301,7 +2301,7 @@
           "title": "Onam Festival",
           "name": "Onam Festival",
           "desc": "Onam is a time for sports, festivities, and ritual celebrations in Kerala. This festival is celebrated in memory of the golden era of King Mahabali whose spirit is said to visit the state at the time of Onam. Colorful water festivals are organized along the sacred river Pampa as part of the celebrations. The festival is held at the end of August or beginning of September depending local calendar. The celebrations last for ten days and the last day is called the Thiruonam which is the most important day of celebrations. People make a preparation of a floral carpet called Athappookkalam in front of houses, from the first day of Atham to the tenth day of Thiruvonam. All over the state celebrations, rituals, where everyone participae wearing new clothes, prepareing traditional cuisine, and dance, and music mark this harvest festival. Aranmulla is the place for the exciting snake boat races. Nearly 30 chundan vallams or snake boats participate in the festival with fishermen singing traditional boat songs as they move their boats.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Kerala/Onam.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Kerala/Onam.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
           "materials": ""
         }
@@ -2312,7 +2312,7 @@
           "title": "Napier Museum",
           "name": "Napier Museum",
           "desc": "The Napier museum is one of the earliest museum in India established by the Maharaja of Travancore in 1855. It is a ninteenth century structure, built in the Indo-Saracenic style and is a magnificent piece of architecture and is named after the former Governor General of Madras, Sir John Napier. Also called the Government Art Museum, this beautiful museum has an ornate interior and exterior. The building was designed by an English architect. The Sree Chitra Art Gallery inside the Napier museum features a rare collection of paintings by Raja Ravi Varma, Roerich and some works from the Rajput, Mughal and Tanjore schools of painting. Another attraction at the museum is the oriental collection comprising of paintings from Japan, China, Bali and Tibet. The museum has a vast collection of sculptures in bronze and stone, carvings of wood and ivory, lamps, textiles, Kathakali models, handicraft items, traditional musical instruments, a treasure of coin collections representing Chera, Chola, Pandya dynasties of Southern India. The bronze sculptures include fascinating images of Siva, Vishnu, Parvathi and Lakshmi. The Vishnu image of eight century collected from Ambalapuzha temple in central Travancore Pallava style is one of the oldest metal image from this region. The stone sculptures belong to eight to eighteenth century. All the wood carvings exhibited here shows high degree of excellence.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Kerala/NapierMuseum.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Kerala/NapierMuseum.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Modern 1850-1900",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2321,7 +2321,7 @@
           "title": "Ambalavayal Heritage Museum",
           "name": "Ambalavayal Heritage Museum",
           "desc": "The Ambalavayal Heritage Museum, the archaeological museum in Wayanad, has one of Kerala's largest collections of the bits and pieces of an era dating back to the second CE. The displayed pieces here support the fact that an advanced civilisation existed in the mountains of Wayanad. The articles on display are a fascination for historians, archaeologists and common men alike. The museum boasts of articles as varied as clay sculptures, ancient hunting equipments like bows and arrows, stone weapons and other curios.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Kerala/ambalavayal.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Kerala/ambalavayal.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2330,7 +2330,7 @@
           "title": "Archaeological Museum",
           "name": "Archaeological Museum",
           "desc": "The Archaeological Museum set in a majestic building is an architectural delight. It houses a gallery of murals from all over Kerala and preserves a rare treasure of Veerakallu, temple models, olagrandhangal (manuscripts on dry palm leaves), megaliths etc. Veerakallu translated as herostones are stone engravings and sculptures of figures and weapons belonging to the heroes of days gone by which were recovered from the forests of Wayanad and Thrissur. The megalith collection includes earthen pots, urns etc. The temple models cast in plaster of Paris are excellent representations of the ancient temples of Kerala.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Kerala/archaeological-museum-thrissur.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Kerala/archaeological-museum-thrissur.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Modern 1900-1947",
           "materials": ""
         }
@@ -2392,7 +2392,7 @@
             "title": "Wall paintings",
             "name": "Wall paintings",
             "desc": "The city of Orchha was founded on the banks of Betwa in the sixteenth century by the Bundela chief Rudra Pratap, who became the first ruler of Orchha. In the early seventeenth century, Raja Jujhar Singh rebelled against the Mughal emperor Shah Jahan, whose armies devastated the state and occupied Orchha from 1635 to 1641. The main historical monuments of Orchha are the Jehangir Mahal, Raj Mahal, Rai Praveen Mahal, Laxmi Narayan Temple and the Chaturbhuj Temple. The various palaces and temples built by the Bundela rulers retain much of their immaculate perfection. Of the succeeding rulers, the most notable was Raja Bir Singh who built the exquisite Jehangir Mahal, a tiered palace crowned with graceful Chhatries. Construction of the Raj Mahal was started by Rudra Pratap, and completed by one of his successors, Madhukar Shah. The Sheesh Mahal was built during the early 18th century, and was originally intended as an exclusive country retreat for the local king Udait Singh. It has two rectangular courtyards inside, the second, formerly used by the Bundela queens. Opulent royal quarters, raised balconies and interlocking walkways rise in symmetrical tiers on all four sides, crowned by domed pavilions and turrets. One can find the fragments of mirror-inlay and vibrant painting plastered over their walls and ceilings. Some of the friezes are still in remarkable condition. The Rai Praveen Mahal is a small, double-storeyed brick apartment built by Raja Indramani for his concubine in the mid seventeenth century. This building lies to the north of the Sheesh Mahal. The building, set amid the lawns of the Anand Mahal gardens, it has a main assembly hall on the ground floor, a private salon upstairs, and cool underground apartments. Entered through an ornate ceremonial gateway, the east-facing facade is covered with turquoise tiles. Two stone elephants flank the stairway, holding bells in their trunks to announce the arrival of the Raja. Three storeys of elegant hanging balconies, terraces, apartments and onion domes are piled around a central courtyard. This palace, however, is more airy and lighted since it has countless windows and pierced stone screens looking out over the skyline to the west, and a sea of treetops and ruined temples in the other direction.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/Orchha1.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/Orchha1.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1500-1550",
             "materials": ""
           },
