@@ -2479,7 +2479,7 @@
           "title": "Madhya Pradesh- Monument- Khajuraho",
           "name": "Madhya Pradesh- Monument- Khajuraho",
           "desc": "Known as the temple city of India, Khajuraho is known for its intricately carved, beautiful medieval temples. A symbol of India's glorious past, heritage and culture, the temples symbolize an immortal saga of Hindu art and culture in central India and are an outstanding image of the well developed Nagara style of temple architecture. Khajuraho was the historic capital of the Chandelas who built these temples from the ninth through the thirteenth centuries. Among the 85 original ones only 25 have survived the ravages of time. Around the temples are bands of fine work, portraying gods, goddesses, musicians, animals, etc. Erotic sculptures featuring apsaras and mithunas (couples) also dominate the themes portrayed here. shrine and four subsidiary shrines scattered around the landscape. The temples of Khajuraho are divided into three groups- the western,which is the largest group having temples dedicated to Shiva and Vishnu like Chitragupta Temple, Kandariya Mahadeo, Chausat Yogini Temple, Lakshmana Temple and Matangeswara Temple. The Eastern are the Jain temples including the temples of Pashvanath and the Adinatha which were built by the merchants and the Southern group, where the remains of the Vaidyanath Shiva temple has been exposed by the ASI (Archaeological survey of India). Khajuraho shows a highly evolved stage of Indian temple architecture. Most temples stand on a tall platform known as the Jagati. The elevation consists of three divisions: a pitha, the wall or jangha, and a roof or shikhara. Ornamentation is abundant on the basal storey in a series of mouldings. It depicts human activities, kirtimukhas and geometric designs. The wall section is divided into two or three sculptural sections including apsaras, mithunas and divine figures. As these figures rise upwards they add to the dramatic effect. The oldest of these temples, dating back to 900 CE is the Chaunsath Yogini temple, located southwest of the Shibsagar lake. Built of rough granite it is dedicated to the sixty four manifestations of the Mother Goddesses or the yoginis. This shrine is raised on a large platform in an open courtyard surrounded by miniature shrines for sixty four manifestations of the goddess. Kandariya Mahadeo temple is the largest in Khajuraho. It is considered to be the best representation of the nagara style of temples consisting of a garbhagriha or a sanctum, a circumambulatory path - pradakshinapatha, an antarala or the vestibule, ardhamandapa, mandapa and a mahamandapa. The sanctum enshrines a marble shivalingam. This mammoth structure stands 30 metres above the ground level. A rich variety of sculptures adorn the platform. They are sculptures of female figures in different poses engaged in writing letters, playing with a ball or applying makeup. The northern group has Vaishnava temples for the most part and the south eastern group consists of Jain temples. To the north of this temple is the Devi Jagadambi temple standing on the same platform as Kandariya Mahadeo. It is more delicately proportionate and the temple is decorated with some fine sculptures. Due to its smaller size, the sculptures are clearly visible. Further north is the sun temple, enshrining an image of the Sun God, on a chariot pulled by seven horses. To the extreme north of this, is the Vishwanath temple, built along the lines of the Kandariya Mahadeo temple. Inscriptions reveal that it was built by King Dhanga and that an emerald image of Shiva - Marakateswara was enshrined here. South west of this temple is the Parvati temple. The Lakshmana-Chaturbhuja temple stands on a platform which has a magnificent sculptural frieze of elephants and horse riders in a procession. There is also an embellishment of erotic sculptures. The Matangeswara temple enshrines Shiva, in the form of a highly polished gigantic Shivalingam. There is also a small Varaha temple with a gigantic monolithic image of a standing Varaha, the boar incarnation of Vishnu. The whole figure is carved in low relief. The main sanctum contains the image of Vaikunth Vishnu, bearing three heads representing his various incarnations. The eastern group has temples dedicated to Brahma. It is one of the smallest shrine with a single room having a shiv- linga protected by a huge pyramidal roof. Vamana and Javari are miniature versions of the western group of temples.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/chausath-yogini.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/chausath-yogini.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 750-1200",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2488,7 +2488,7 @@
           "title": "Adinath Temple, Parsvanath Temple, Ghantai Temple, Hanuman Temple and Brahma Temple",
           "name": "Adinath Temple, Parsvanath Temple, Ghantai Temple, Hanuman Temple and Brahma Temple",
           "desc": "Among the temples in the eastern zone, the ones worth mentioning are: Adinath Temple, Parsvanath Temple, Ghantai Temple, Hanuman Temple and Brahma Temple, which can be divided into two, the temples dedicated to the Jain pantheon and the temples dedicated to Hindu Gods and Goddesses. The Jain temples include the Parsavanath Temple dedicated to Parsavanath, the Jain Tirthankara. It is a well decorated and the largest Jain Temple. Adinath temple is dedicated to Adinath, a Jain Tirthankara. The sculpted figures of Yakshis are particularly famous here. Ghantai Temple is well known for the sculpture of a Jain goddess on a winged Garuda and for the sixteen dreams of Mahavira's mother. The temples in the southern zone has Dulah Deo temple dedicated to Lord Shiva and Chattarbhuj temple. The Dulah Deo Temple has been dedicated to Lord Shiva. It is supposed to have been constructed between 1100 CE and 1150. Inside the temple there is a vast Shivalingam. There is also an image of Lord Shiva at the very entrance of the temple.The architectural complex consists of the mandapa, where the shrine is placed, along with the mahamandapa and the ardhamandapa with no circumbulatory passage. There is also the cluster of the shikharas, both subsidiary and centralized. The Chaturbhuja temple consists of the shrine of Lord Vishnu, to whom this is dedicated. This temple has also been constructed in the 1100. The structure is more or less similar to the Dulah Dao Temple. The exotic sculptures add to the glamour and elegance of the place.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/Adinatha-Temple.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/Adinatha-Temple.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2506,7 +2506,7 @@
           "title": "Chanderi Fort",
           "name": "Chanderi Fort",
           "desc": "The Chanderi fort is located in the small historical town of Chanderi in the Ashoknagar District of Madhya Pradesh. The fort is the most striking monument in this ancient town and it dates back to the Mughal Period. The Muslim rulers, especially the Malwa Sultans took a lot of initiative to construct the fortification walls of the fort. The mahals within the fort were built by the Bundela rajputs revealing various phases of building styles. Located on top of a hill, the fortified walls enclose the citadel and protect it from outsiders. The Fort is approached through three gates. The first and main gate is well known as the Khooni Darwaza. It has been so named because criminals were thrown from the battlements above and their bodies crashed to pieces below. Hawa Paur is the third and highest gate of the Chanderi fort. The remnants of the Chanderi Fort include the Hawa Mahal and the Nau Khanda Mahal. Both these buildings were built by the Bundela chiefs. There is yetanother gateway on the south western side of the fort referred to as the Khatti Ghatti which is about twenty five metres in height forming part of the outer defenses of the town. There is a Purana Madarsa which was used as a school in the sixteenth century. It has latticed screens with geometric designs set in closed arches and the walls are embellished with lotus medallions. There is a massive structure known as the Koshak Mahal which houses four identical palace buildings which are situated at the corner of a square courtyard. The plain exterior contrasts well with the ornate bay windows. The battisi baoli is the biggest step-well in Chanderi. Thirty-two broad steps from each side descend down to a square pool.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/chanderifort-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/chanderifort-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1500-1550",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2515,7 +2515,7 @@
           "title": "Lal Baag Palace, Indore",
           "name": "Lal Baag Palace, Indore",
           "desc": "Lal Baag palace is located in Indore. It is surrounded by gardens and was built between 1886 and 1921. It is one of the grandest ancestral monuments built by the Holkar dynasty. The rooms have been restored and furnished and much of the furniture and ornamentation is in the early Georgian style. Its Italian marble columns, grand chandeliers, rich Persian carpets, flying nymphs on the ceiling, Belgian stained glass windows, Greek mythological reliefs, Italian style wall paintings speak of its past glory.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/lalbagh.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/lalbagh.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Modern 1850-1900",
           "materials": ""
