@@ -2569,7 +2569,7 @@
           "title": "Madhya Pradesh- Monument- Sanchi",
           "name": "Madhya Pradesh- Monument- Sanchi",
           "desc": "Sanchi, one of the most distinct archaeological sites in India is located right in the middle of the country. The world heritage site of Sanchi is being represented by various monuments including stupas, monolithic Asokan pillar, temples, monasteries and great sculptural wealth built over several centuries from the early Mauryan period to the twelfth century. It was Emperor Asoka who laid the foundations of a religious centre at Sanchi by erecting a monolithic pillar. He erected the stupa 1 here after redistribution of mortal remains of Lord Buddha for erecting several stupas all over the country in order to spread Buddhism. This stupa was originally a low structure of bricks, half the diameter of the present edifice hemispherical in shape with raised terraces at the base. It was enclosed by a wooden railing and a stone umbrella at the top. This great stupa later served as a nucleus to the large Buddhist establishment. Ashoka built a total of eight stupas on the hilltop of Sanchi including the great stupa. A number of stupas and other religious structures were added over the succeeding centuries. With the decline of Buddhism, the site decayed and was eventually completely forgotten. But, between1912 and 1919, the structures were rediscovered and carefully restored to their present condition. Originally stupas were mounds covering the relics of the Buddha or his followers. In its earliest stages Buddhist art didn't represent the Buddha directly. Instead, his presence was alluded to through symbols such as the bodhi tree, the wheel of law or his footprint. The stupa also became a symbol of the Buddha. More exactly, it became a symbol of his final release from the cycle of birth and rebirth - the Mahaparinirvana. During the Sunga period, several edifices were raised at Sanchi and its surrounding hills. The Ashoka stupa was enlarged and faced with stones and decorated with balustrades, staircases and a harmika on the top. In the Ist century BCE the Andhra-Satavahanas, whose boundaries covered the Malwa region, added the elaborately carved gateways to stupa 1 which is the most elaborate and well known of more than fifty magnificent Buddhist monuments that crown the hilltop of Sanchi. A nearly perfect hemispherical dome, the great stupa1 is topped by a triple parasol set within a square railing or harmika. A third of the way up from its base, a raised terrace, enclosed by a fence, is meant for ritual circumambulation of the monument. A second, stone-paved procession-path at ground-level is enclosed by an encircling stone balustrade. This path is accessed from the cardinal directions through four exquisitely carved gateways. The exquisite carvings of the doorway depict in detail the significant episodes and miracles from Lord Buddhas life and events depicted in the Buddhist jataka stories. The eastern gateway depicts the young prince, Gautama leaving his father's palace on his journey towards enlightenment and the dream his mother had before his birth. The western gateway depicts the seven incarnations of the Buddha. The northern gateway crowned by a wheel-of-law, this depicts the miracles associated with the Buddha as told in the Jatakas. The southern gateway shows the birth of Gautama revealed in a series of dramatically rich carvings. From the second to fourth century CE Sanchi and Vidisha came under the rule of Kushanas and Kshatrapas and subsequently passed on to the hands of the Guptas. During the Gupta period some temples were also built and sculptures were added displaying the classical grace and simplicity of the era. Further, statues of Lord Buddha seated in the canopies facing the four entrances of the great Stupa were also added. Sanchi also flourished during the seventh century when shrines and monasteries were continued to be added. Thus Sanchi displays harmonious co-existence of Hindu and Buddhist faiths.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/sanchi.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/sanchi.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Ancient 600-001 BCE",
           "materials": ""
         }
@@ -2594,7 +2594,7 @@
             "title": "Warli Paintings",
             "name": "Warli Paintings",
             "desc": "Warli painting derives its name from a tribe living in the remote regions of Maharashtra. Their imagination, beliefs and customs are freely and spontaneously expressed in their monochromatic paintings. The walls of the village of Warli have been adorned with these paintings for centuries and even today they form the primary decoration in most of the houses. Their way of life, trees, birds, men and women form composite paintings, and the most joyous celebration of their life. The moods of their life make for interesting themes, which is why Warli paintings are much more than designs on walls; they are authentic depictions of a way of life. The Warlis live in thatched mud-huts, which are constructed in such a way so that they all surround a central common area. They are primarily an agriculture-dependant tribe. Historians believe that the Warli tradition can be traced back to the neolithic period between 2500 BCE and 3000 BCE. During the harvest season, and weddings and births, their houses are adorned with a variety of patterns. The Warli way of life is beautifully captured in simple images in white against earthen background. Geometric designs dominate most paintings; dots and crooked lines are the units of these compositions. Spiral formations of men and women and concentric circular designs are symbolic of these paintings. The harmony and balance depicted in these paintings is supposed to signify the harmony and balance of the universe. Marriage is the most common theme of Warli paintings. Many paintings have a depiction of Palghat, the marriage God, accompanied by a horse and of course the bride and the groom. These paintings are sacred and without them the marriage cannot take place. Men and women in spirals dancing in celebration, is another theme typical to the Warli paintings. A musician playing a native instrument is usually found in the middle of such spirals. Flora and fauna are also depicted in these paintings.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/warli-1.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/warli-1.webp",
             "historical_significance": "",
             "materials": ""
           }
@@ -2605,7 +2605,7 @@
             "title": "Maharashtra- Performing Art- Dance- Tamasha",
             "name": "Maharashtra- Performing Art- Dance- Tamasha",
             "desc": "Tamasha is a form of theatre, which came into existence in the early sixteenth century in Maharashtra, which includes the love songs i.e. lavanis due to which, this folk art, was very popular among the common people. Lavani is a combination of traditional song and dance, performed by women. It is performed to the beats of dholak. The word lavani originates from lavanya, meaning beauty. Earlier, this art form dealt with different and varied subject matters such as society, religion, politics, romance, etc. It is said that Lavani was used as a form of entertainment and morale booster to the tired soldiers during the eighteenth and nineteenth century Maratha battle. Many famous Marathi poets like Honaji Bala, Ramjoshi, Prabhakar, have written for Lavani.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/lavani.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/lavani.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -2614,7 +2614,7 @@
             "title": "Maharashtra- Performing Art- Dance- Koli",
             "name": "Maharashtra- Performing Art- Dance- Koli",
             "desc": "Koli is one of the most popular folk dance forms of Maharashtra that derives its name from the fisher folk known as Kolis. It is a dance of joy, which is a celebration of valuable life, performed with sheer exuberance by the dancers. Both men and women in groups perform these dances, where all the performers have to make a single row or stand in pairs and portray many fishing actions in the dance form, by holding small oars in their hands and moving those to the beats of the songs. Generally, women participants wear green coloured sarees in their Koli style, whose length comes upto their knees; while the men wear a lungi having a triangular shape. The dance sequences include actions like rowing of a boat, or movement of waves, where swaying of oars in backward and forward direction creates a scene of a boat tossing on the high sea-waves. Ellora is the finest example of cave temples with almost thirty four caves with intricate interiors and ornamental facades. Carved during the 350 CE and 700 CE, the rock temples and monasteries represent three faiths - Hinduism, Buddhism and Jainism. Cave 10, a Chaitya-hall is popularly known as Visvakarma' with a seated Buddha in a stupa. The Ramesvara cave has figurines of river goddesses adorning its entrance. The Dumar Lena cave is dedicated to Lord Shiva. Among the viharas, Cave 5 is the largest. The most impressive vihara is the three - storeyed cave called 'Tin - Tala'. The Kailasa temple in cave 16 is a masterpiece in itself. The temple that took nearly hundred years to be completed is decorated with themes and incidents from the puranas. Consisting of exquisite carvings, the cave temple is the most striking one amongst the other 30 at the site. Though carved out of one single rock, the temple looks like a freestanding structural southern type temple. The exterior of the temple is richly carved with niches, plasters, windows as well as images of deities, mithunas (erotic male and female figures) and other figures. Most of the deities at the left of the entrance are Shaivaite (followers of Lord Shiva) while on the right hand side the deities are Vaishnavites (followers of Lord Vishnu). The whole temple consists of a shrine with a ling at the rear of the hall with a Dravidian shikhara, a flat-roofed mandapa supported by sixteen pillars, a separate porch for Nandi surrounded by an open-court entered through a low gopuram. There are two Dhvajastambhas Ajanta Photo Gallery (pillars with the flagstaff) in the courtyard. The grand sculpture of Ravana attempting to lift mount Kailasa, the abode of Lord Shiva, with his full might is a landmark in Indian art. All the carvings are done in more than one level. The lower storey consists of several large shrines while the gallery along the three sides at the rear contains huge sculpted panels. A two-storeyed gateway on the west side provides access to the main temple and the Nandi mandapa, both on the same level, and is approximately seven meters high. The lower storey of the main temple as well as the Nandi shrine is solid but unaccessible. Stone bridges carved from the living rock connect the Nandi pavilion and the entrance porch of the temple. The main temple has a southern style superstructure. The lower storey has almost life-size elephants carved as if they are holding up the temple. The Jaina Caves (30 to 34) are massive, well-proportioned, decorated and mark the last phase of the activity at Ellora.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/ellora1.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/ellora1.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Modern 1900-1947",
             "materials": ""
           }
@@ -2682,7 +2682,7 @@
           "title": "Mumbai- Monument- Chhatrapati Shivaji Terminus (formerly Victoria Terminus)",
           "name": "Mumbai- Monument- Chhatrapati Shivaji Terminus (formerly Victoria Terminus)",
           "desc": "The Chhatrapati Shivaji terminus, formerly known as Victoria Terminus in Mumbai was designed by Frederick William Stevens. It is an outstanding example of Victorian Gothic architecture in India, blended with themes deriving from Indian traditional architecture. The terminal was built over ten years starting in 1878 according to a design based on late medieval Italian models. Its remarkable stone dome, turrets, pointed arches, and interesting ground plan are close to traditional Indian palace architecture. The interiors are adorned with wood carving, tiles, ornamental iron and brass railings, grills for the ticket offices, and the grand staircase with balustrades.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/victoria-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/victoria-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Modern 1850-1900",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2691,7 +2691,7 @@
           "title": "Mumbai- Monument- Gateway of India",
           "name": "Mumbai- Monument- Gateway of India",
           "desc": "The Gateway of India, a massive archway on the Apollo Bunder, is one of the grand and magnificent landmarks of Mumbai, which was built to commemorate the visit of the British Monarch, King George V and Queen Mary. The Gateway of India is one of the hot spots of Mumbai city. Designed by the architect George Wittet it was formally opened in 1924 by the then Viceroy, Earl of Reading. The Gateway is a 26m high structure with four turrets and complicated lattice work engraved into the yellow basalt stone. The central dome of the Gateway of India is 48 feet in diameter. It is interesting to note that the whole harbour front was realligned in order to come in line with a planned walkway, sweeping down to the centre of the town. At the back of the Gateway arch, steps lead down to the sea front.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/gateway-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/gateway-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Modern 1900-1947",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2700,7 +2700,7 @@
           "title": "Mumbai- Monument- Elephanta",
           "name": "Mumbai- Monument- Elephanta",
           "desc": "The Elephanta Island is located ten km away from the Gateway of India at Mumbai. These caves house rock cut temples dating back to the fifth century CE. Elephanta Island was so named by the Portuguese, after the statue of an elephant near the landing area of the island. There are six caves dating back to sixth and seventh century. These rock cut temples dedicated to Lord Shiva are abundant in sculptural content. Created by carving out rocks, and creating columns, internal spaces and the images, the entire temple can be compared to a huge sculpture. Some of the rock surfaces are highly finished while some are untreated bare rock. The entire cave temple consists of a main chamber and two lateral ones, courtyards and several subsidiary shrines. Above the temple is the mass of natural rock. There are three entrances to this temple with the one on the east and the west marking the axis of the temple. A twenty pillared hall lines the axis, and on its western end is the cella in which is enshrined a Shiva linga. The pillars consist of fluted columns standing on square bases, and are crowned with fluted cushioned capitals. The most enigmatic image is of Trimurti or the Sadasiva manifestation of Shiva which is a collossal twenty feet high image of the three headed Shiva. Also on the southern wall are grand sculptured images of Kalyanasundara, Gangadhara, Ardhanariswara and Uma Maheswara. To the west of the northern entrance are sculptured images of Nataraja and Andhakaasuravadha urth, and to its east are images of Yogiswara and Ravanaanugrahamurthy in eight manifest forms. At Elephanta caves, Shiva is portrayed in the non-anthropomorphic Shivalinga form, as well as in the colossal human image. The secondary shrine is located to the east of the main temple in a courtyard. This temple contains six pillars at its entrance, four of which are free standing and two connected. The entrance leads to a hall decorated with sculptured panels depicting legends from the Shiva Purana.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/elephanta-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/elephanta-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Ancient 001-600 CE",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2709,7 +2709,7 @@
           "title": "Aurangabad- Monument- Bibi Ka Makbara",
           "name": "Aurangabad- Monument- Bibi Ka Makbara",
           "desc": "Bibi Ka Maqbara, the burial place of Aurangzeb's 1st wife, Rabia-ud-Durrani is an imitation of the Taj at Agra and is often referred to as the poor mans Taj Mahal due to its poor copy of the masterpiece. The mausoleum is made mostly of sandstone and has plastered walls and a marble dome.The tomb is adornedwith lattice work in marble and inlay decorations of flowers and creepers which are beautiful. A high wall with bastions runs around the monument and the recesses have little minarets. The entrance leads to an arch and from this point, a fine view of the structure ahead may be obtained. The pavement that leads to the mausoleum is flanked by oblong reservoirs.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/bibi-ka-makbara.jpg",
