@@ -2727,7 +2727,7 @@
           "title": "Mumbai- Monument- Kanheri Cave",
           "name": "Mumbai- Monument- Kanheri Cave",
           "desc": "Kanheri caves were the viharas for the Buddhist monks during the period between second century BCE and ninth century CE. These caves are simple and bare, quite different from that of Elephanta caves which are artistically embellished. Kanheri caves are mostly tiny cells that are cut into the edges of a hill, each having a stone plinth that might have served as a bed. There is also a congregation hall supported by huge stone pillars that contains the dagoba, a kind of Buddhist shrine. The largest is the Cave 13 which is adorned with sculptures in front of the stupa.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/Kanheri-Caves.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/Kanheri-Caves.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Ancient 001-600 CE",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2736,7 +2736,7 @@
           "title": "Lonavla- Monument- Bhaja Cave",
           "name": "Lonavla- Monument- Bhaja Cave",
           "desc": "The Bhaja caves near Lonavla are the perfect examples of the phase of early Buddhist architecture. Lord Buddha has been represented here through numerous symbols in the caves. However, the caves which were carved after the fourth century CE, also presents the Buddha in his physical forms. The pillars of these caves are carved with figures of flowers and other images. The cave 12, is a Chaitya hall, which is the largest of all has a fine vault. The last cave towards the south is a good example of fine architecture and sculpture. This cave includes the figures of a dancing couple, a prince seated on an elephant and The remaining 7 caves contain inscriptions about the donors. The chaitya caves do not have a wall in the front as they are open which might have been enclosed by a wooden door of some kind in olden days. The patterns of the ceilings are prototypes of wooden ceilings.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/bhaja-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/bhaja-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Ancient 600-001 BCE",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2745,7 +2745,7 @@
           "title": "Karle- Monument- Karle Cave",
           "name": "Karle- Monument- Karle Cave",
           "desc": "The Buddhist caves at Karle, built by the Buddhist monks, are the epitome of ancient rock-cut caves to be found in India. Dating back from the second to first century BCE, the Karle caves in Lonavla, were built during the Satvahahana period and hosted the monks in the monsoons, Built from top to bottom in the form of a ladder, the early Karle caves do not depict the Buddha in his physical forms but rather in symbols. However, from the seventh century CE when the Mahayana became more popular with their liberal thoughts, the later Karle caves do present the Buddha in his physical form such as the Buddha preaching while seated on a lion-supported throne, along withother magnificent carvings. The caves comprise mainly two types of structures: chaitya halls and viharas. The chaitya halls, which form the largest number of chaityas in the world (one being one hundred and forty eight feet long), were used for congregational worship whereas the viharas were the dwelling place for the monks and further included cells cut into the walls around three sides of a hall used for their residential and individual meditation. The pillars of the halls inside the caves have been carved with figures of men, women, flowers and elephants, though the pillars outside these halls have lions erected on them as their crowns, an emblem of Ashoka, the great Indian ruler.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/karle-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/karle-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Ancient 600-001 BCE",
           "materials": ""
         }
@@ -2756,7 +2756,7 @@
           "title": "Maharashtra- Fairs and Festivals - Ganesh Chaturthi",
           "name": "Maharashtra- Fairs and Festivals - Ganesh Chaturthi",
           "desc": "Also known as Vinayak Chaturthi, Ganesh Chaturthi is a festival celebrating the day on which Lord Ganesh, son of Shiva and Parvati appeared on earth for his devotees. According to the Hindu calendar, Ganesh Chaturthi is celebrated on the Shukla Chaturthi which is usually between 20 August and 15 September. The festival lasts for almost 10 days. The festival commences with the installation of the idol of Ganesh in beautifully decorated pandals. The pandals are decorated with paintings depicting the life of Lord Ganesha. The festival also includes a lot of cultural activities like music and dance. A popular sweet made during the Ganesh Chaturthi are modak and karanjis which are sweets made of coconut, jaggery, and other flavors.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/ganesh-chaturthi.jpg",
