           "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2765,7 +2765,7 @@
           "title": "Maharashtra- Fairs and Festivals- Parsi New Year",
           "name": "Maharashtra- Fairs and Festivals- Parsi New Year",
           "desc": "The Parsi New Year is also known as Navroze and is mainly celebrated by a particular sect of Parsis, the Faslis. It is also celebrated by the Muslims of Iran and Zoroastrians. Navroze is the first day of the first month according to the Zoroastrian calendar. Originally an agricultural festival, the Parsi New Year later transformed into a religious festival. The day is celebrated by wearing new and colorful clothes, decorating the house with colours, lighting incense sticks and sprinkling sandalwood powder on live coal. The day begins by going to the Fire Temple and offering Jashan or a thanksgiving prayer service and offering sandalwood to the holy fire. Food is a significant part of the Parsi New Year. It includes a wide selection of non-vegetarian dishes, fruits and nuts. Two important dishes in breakfast for the Parsi New Year are Ravo and fried vermicelli cooked in sugar syrup and decorated with raisins.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/ganesh-chaturthi-sweets.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/ganesh-chaturthi-sweets.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
           "materials": ""
         }
@@ -2776,7 +2776,7 @@
           "title": "Mumbai- Museum- Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Vastu Sangrahalaya (Prince of Wales Museum)",
           "name": "Mumbai- Museum- Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Vastu Sangrahalaya (Prince of Wales Museum)",
           "desc": "Some prominent citizens of Bombay decided to set up a Museum with the help of the government to commemorate the Prince of Wales, the Crown Prince who visited Bombay in 1905 and later became King George V, who laid the foundation stone of the museum. The exquisite basalt stone structure with its large central dome inspired by the Bijapur domes and beautiful gardens are set in a crescent shaped piece of land. Art, archaeology and natural history are three exclusive sections in the museum. It houses some of the finest collections, ranging from ancient Indus Valley relics to the artifacts from the Gupta and Maurya Periods. Here, one can see a few of the rarest and earliest exhibits of Indian history. There are scores of interesting artifacts from foreign countries like Tibet and Nepal. It has one complete gallery comprising the exquisite thangka scrolls of the Buddhists. Another gallery is devoted to bronze castings and stone sculptures and more than 2,000 miniature paintings from the different styles and phases of India. The collection presents the evolution of painting in India, ranging between eleventh and twelveth century illustrated palm leaf manuscripts to early nineteenth century Pahari paintings. Exquisite examples of all the main schools of miniature painting are on view, Rajput, Mughal, Pahari, and Deccani, each school with its characteristic style has its own delicacy of form and colour. The variety of art displayed in the museum is definitely a treat for any art-lover.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/POW-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/POW-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Modern 1900-1947",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -2785,7 +2785,7 @@
           "title": "The Raja Dinkar Kelkar Museum",
           "name": "The Raja Dinkar Kelkar Museum",
           "desc": "Dr. Dinkar Kelkar started collecting artifacts and objects around 1920s and by the sixties he had accumulated around 15000 exhibits. He was a great lover of Indian art. The Kelkar Museum is the result of his tireless efforts of about sixty years. It is a museum with a collection of everyday objects like pots, lamps, containers, nutcrackers, pen stands etc. Objects have been classified according to their materials and techniques of making them and their function. There are some unique collections in this museum. There is an assortment of oil lamps made of different materials right from clay to brass, each with their own shape and form and usage right from rituals, to practical lighting.Some beautiful, intricately carved wooden doors and windows adorn the museum. They are collected from Gujarat, Rajasthan, Karnataka and Kerala. Another kind of collection is of locks used for both trunks and doors. They are of interesting shapes of dogs, horses and even scorpions. One remarkable collection is the chitakathi scroll paintings which tell us the great tradition of the village storyteller and accompaniment of music and song.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/Kelkar-museum-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/Kelkar-museum-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Modern 1947-1980",
           "materials": ""
         }
