@@ -2828,7 +2828,7 @@
             "title": "Manipur- Handicrafts - Kauna (Water Reed) Crafts",
             "name": "Manipur- Handicrafts - Kauna (Water Reed) Crafts",
             "desc": "Imphal valley of Manipur is the centre for kauna crafts. This is often used for making mats, chairs, cushion, coasters, bags, moorah (low stools) etc. A unique craft of Manipur is the double-weave mat known as the kaunaphak reed mat. Known as phak in Meithei, a language of Manipur, the reed used to weave the mat is called kauna. The mat is made by the local people for their own use, as well as for commercial purposes. Phak is yellow in colour and is the succulent stem of a plant that grows in water. Reeds are carefully chosen for the mats. The plant is cut only when it has reached maturity. Once cut, the stems are dried and become soft and quite brittle. A bunch of cut stems of the appropriate length are then woven with bamboo placed at suitable distance to give the mat its desired length. The border has an interesting pattern and is about an inch wide. The kauna reed mats are not meant to be washed as the reed gets spoilt with moisture.",
