@@ -3118,7 +3118,7 @@
             "title": "Nagaland- Handicrafts- Woodcarving",
             "name": "Nagaland- Handicrafts- Woodcarving",
             "desc": "Woodcarving is another work of artistry by which Naga people are known. It can be explained under three heads namely headhunting, decoration of the Morungs, and funerary images. Though most of the tribes have skills in woodcarving, the Wanchos, Konyaks and Phom tribes are the finest wood-carvers in this genre.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Nagaland/woodhead1.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Nagaland/woodhead1.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -3127,7 +3127,7 @@
             "title": "Pottery",
             "name": "Pottery",
             "desc": "The pots are generally very simple and mainly functional. Some of the designs and the motifs found on these potteries have been inspired by the design of the textiles. The pots of villages of Tseminyu and Ungma are well known in the region. These pots are made on the pottery wheel, then the pot is heated and finally rice water is boiled to remove the taste of the baked clay and ensure durability.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Nagaland/bcft206b.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Nagaland/bcft206b.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -3136,7 +3136,7 @@
             "title": "Nagaland- Handicrafts- Metal Work",
             "name": "Nagaland- Handicrafts- Metal Work",
             "desc": "Some of the crafted items are spears, chisels, ornaments and items of utility. Nagas are attached to their weapons and it might be because their survival in the past depended on this skill. These weapons are different in shape and pattern from tribe to tribe. Among all the tribes, the Rengmas are considered the best blacksmiths.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Nagaland/metal-2-tools.jpg",
