@@ -3514,7 +3514,7 @@
             "title": "Punjab- Handicrafts- Embroidery- Phulkari",
             "name": "Punjab- Handicrafts- Embroidery- Phulkari",
             "desc": "Craft forms an integral part of the lifestyle of the people of Punjab. Phulkari, is an art of embroidering shawls, dupattas with floral motifs, dress materials etc. It is done on handspun, handwoven coarse dyed khadi with an ordinary needle and strands of untwisted silk. It is a skillful art of a single stitch that provides interesting patterns on the cloth. The smaller the stitch, finer is the quality of the embroidery. The silk threads in golden yellow, red, crimson, orange, green, blue, and pink are usually employed for the embroidery. The notable aspect of this technique is that single strand was used at a time, each part worked in one color and the varied color effect is obtained by clever use of horizontal, vertical or diagonal stitches. In Phulkari the design is worked very closely that even a square inch of the base cloth is not visible. It is then called Bagh. Besides floral motifs, birds, animals, human figures, vegetables, pots, buildings, rivers, the sun and the moon, scenes of village life, and other imagery are embroidered. Some of the designs include dhaniya bagh (coriander garden), motia bagh (jasmine garden), satranga bagh (garden of rainbow), leheria bagh (garden of waves) and many other depictions. It is made by female members of the house, and takes a long time to make; sometimes even a few years. Traditionally, a Phulkari shawl is gifted to the daughter at her wedding. Another kind of shawl is the Chope, It is very simple in comparison to the Phulkari and Bagh, and is presented to a new bride by her maternal grandmother. The darshan dwar shawl is gifted to a temple by a devotee whose wish has been fulfilled.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Punjab/PHULKARI.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Punjab/PHULKARI.webp",
             "historical_significance": "1600-1650",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -3532,7 +3532,7 @@
             "title": "Punjab- Handicrafts- Leather",
             "name": "Punjab- Handicrafts- Leather",
             "desc": "Leather jootis (shoes and slippers) of Punjab are extremely well known. Rich gold and multi-colored threads are used to decorate and impart a royal touch to a variety of jootis crafted from leather of different shades. In many parts of Punjab, entire families continue to devote themselves to making jootis.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Punjab/leather-jootis.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Punjab/leather-jootis.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           }
@@ -3544,7 +3544,7 @@
           "title": "Golden temple",
           "name": "Golden temple",
           "desc": "The Golden Temple or Darbar Sahib in Amritsar is the most sacred place for the Sikhs. It represents the magnificence of the Sikhs all over the world. Also known as Harminder Sahib, it is a great example of the spirit of tolerance and acceptance that the Sikh philosophy promotes. In the architecture of the Golden temple are included symbols associated with places of worship of other religions. Sri Harminder Sahib is built on a 67ft. square platform in the centre of a Sarovar (tank). The Golden temple has entrances from all the four sides. The temple is within a square pillared enclosure. Within the enclosed complex there is a massive square known as amritsar, or pool of nectar, from which the city derives its name. A marble ambulatory with white, black, and ochre stone tiles forming geometric designs surrounds the pool of water. The main square shrine stands on a platform in the centre of the pool. This shrine is approached through the Darshan Deorhi which is a richly inlaid marble archway on the northeast side. The doors of the archway are covered with silver and ivory, and the upper portions of the shrine are made of gold-plated copper sheets with embossed designs and sacred verses. A marble passage with gilded lamps along the sides, leads to a central hall with four silver doors in the cardinal directions and marble steps, called Har ki Pauri which descends into the water on the southern side. The Sheesh Mahal on the second floor is embellished mostly with floral designs made of coloured and mirrored glass, and semi precious stones inlaid in marble. It was in the nineteenth century, during the reign of Maharaja Ranjit Singh, that the people lavished their wealth on the Golden temple. The Guru Granth Sahib, the holy book of the Sikhs, was installed in the Harmandir Sahib in 1604.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Punjab/golden-temple-amritsar.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Punjab/golden-temple-amritsar.webp",
           "historical_significance": "1550-1600",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -3553,7 +3553,7 @@
           "title": "Punjab- Monument- Mausoleums of Ustad and Shagird, Jalandhar",
           "name": "Punjab- Monument- Mausoleums of Ustad and Shagird, Jalandhar",
           "desc": "1600-1650 Medieval Two beautiful seventeenth century tombs are situated in Nakodar belonging to Ustad (teacher) Mohammed Momin Hussaini alias Hafizah, a musician in the service of Khan-e-Khana- the chamberlain of Emperor Jahangir. The other tomb belongs to Haji Jamal, his disciple. The mausoleums are similar in architecture to Emperor Humayuns tomb in Delhi and stand out as buildings of great magnificence.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Punjab/Ustad-and-Shagird-jalandhar.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Punjab/Ustad-and-Shagird-jalandhar.webp",
           "historical_significance": "1600-1650 Medieval",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -3562,7 +3562,7 @@
           "title": "Punjab- Monument- Sarai Nurmahal, Jalandhar",
           "name": "Punjab- Monument- Sarai Nurmahal, Jalandhar",
           "desc": "Sarai Nurmahal, a small town around thirteen kilometer from Jalandhar is linked with Nurjahan, wife of Emperor Jahangir, Mughal Sarai alias Sarai Nurmahal, was constructed for Nurjahan, who was known for her beauty and style of living. It is a remarkable example of Islamic architecture. Its massive gate embellished with arches, filigree work and tiles of beautiful patterns is topped by 4 delicate minarets.",
