@@ -3676,7 +3676,7 @@
             "title": "Rajasthan- Handicrafts- Terracotta",
             "name": "Rajasthan- Handicrafts- Terracotta",
             "desc": "While villages and towns, all have a tradition of clay pottery, in Udaipur this is developed into a fine art. Here, potters use the clay at their command to create not just pots but also the object of art such as warriors mounted on horses, elephants and their mahouts and so on. They are",
-            "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/Terracotta.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/Terracotta.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -3685,7 +3685,7 @@
             "title": "Block Printed Textiles",
             "name": "Block Printed Textiles",
             "desc": "Sanganer is famous for its block printed textiles. A special feature of the Sanganer prints is the harmony of various colours and designs as well as the use of gold and silver in the prints. Stylised sunflowers, roses and geometric patterns form the design language of the blocks. Earlier vegetable colours were used but today both vegetable and chemical dyes are used to block print fabric yardage, saris, salwar kurtas. Ajrak is the hand block printing which is done on both sides. Only natural dyes are used in Ajrak block printing. A speciality of the Ajrak printing is the use of multicolours in individual motifs. Ancient centres such as Barmer have their unique Ajrak prints while Jaipur, Sanganer and Bagru celebrate stylised Mughal inspired blocks like paisley and floral motifs.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/sanganer-textiles.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/sanganer-textiles.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -3694,7 +3694,7 @@
             "title": "Blue Pottery of Jaipur",
             "name": "Blue Pottery of Jaipur",
             "desc": "The famous Jaipur Blue Pottery is perhaps the only pottery produced without the use of clay. The base is prepared out of the material from which the slip is made. All the materials that go into the composition -- quartz, raw glaze, sodium sulphate, fuller's earth locally known as multani clay, all require the same temperature, and the pottery needs to be fired only once unlike other pottery. The slip does not develop any crack and is more impervious and hence more hygenic for daily use. Only the neck and the lip are shaped on the wheel. For doing the decorative work, the pot is rotated and the ornamentation is done with brushes made of squirrel's hair. Some of this pottery is semi-transparent and mostly decorated with arabesque patterns, interspersed with animal and bird motifs.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/blue-pottery.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/blue-pottery.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -3703,7 +3703,7 @@
             "title": "Rajasthan- Handicrafts- Gesso Work",
             "name": "Rajasthan- Handicrafts- Gesso Work",
             "desc": "Rajasthan has fine samples of gesso work like the ones in the main hall of the Bikaner palace. The camel skin bottles of Bikaner are an example of the gesso painted objects of everyday use. In Tonk a special type of shield of thick hide is decorated with coloured gesso. This is finely varnished which gives durability apart from the finish. Gesso in mirror decoration is a fantastic form of architectural decoration. Jaipur is also noted for glass mosaics in which the design is cut out of plaster or metal and behind the opening pieces are formed by coloured glass neatly arranged. When another plaster piece is placed at the back a picture of splendour emerges and dazzles when the sun shines through.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/mosaic-work.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/mosaic-work.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -3712,7 +3712,7 @@
             "title": "Rajasthan- Handicrafts- Tie and Dye",
             "name": "Rajasthan- Handicrafts- Tie and Dye",
             "desc": "Principally, Rajasthan is known for its tie and dye technique in which fabrics are tied in small twists using thread. These are then dipped into vats with their array of bright colors to create a mosaic of patterns. Called Bandhani, these textiles, mostly used for sarees and odhnis, are done in different patterns.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/Tie-and-dye.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/Tie-and-dye.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -3721,7 +3721,7 @@
             "title": "Rajasthan- Handicrafts- Puppets",
             "name": "Rajasthan- Handicrafts- Puppets",
             "desc": "The art of Kathputli or the puppetary has a long tradition in Rajasthan, but the puppets themselves are fairly simple creations. Mostly, they consist of painted woodenheads draped with dresses made from old fabrics and sequined for charm. Simply stuffing rags or cotton into the sleeve of the dress and filling it out makes the hands. The quaintest aspect of these puppets is the way their expressions are painted; large expressive eyes with arched eyebrows and curling moustache for men or a nose ring for the women.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/kathputli.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/kathputli.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           }
@@ -3733,7 +3733,7 @@
           "title": "Rajasthan- Monument- Dilwara Temples, Mt. Abu",
           "name": "Rajasthan- Monument- Dilwara Temples, Mt. Abu",
           "desc": "India is the land where Jainism originated. Today, around 3.2 million Jain devotees live in India. Across the length and breadth of India lay the Jain temples and shrines and hold immense attraction for all those who desire to see the great Jain architectural splendor, and to know and understand the teachings of Lord Mahavira and the other Jain tirthankars. The essence of Jainism and its preaching is very well reflected in different Jain temples and shrines. Mount Abu, a hill station in southern Rajasthan was an important Vaishnava and Shaivite center up to the eleventh century. Today, it is recognized the world over for its magnificent Jain temples of Dilwara. Its sober exterior contrasts with the wealth of the interior ornamentation and wondrously carved in marble, the Dilwara temples enshrine various Jain tirthankaras and saints. Built between the 11th and thireenth centuries, the marble temples of Dilwara are one of the most sacred pilgrimages of the Jains. The marble temples have an opulent entranceway.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/dilwara-temple.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/dilwara-temple.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -3742,7 +3742,7 @@
           "title": "Dilwara Temples, Mt. Abu- Vimal Vasahi",
           "name": "Dilwara Temples, Mt. Abu- Vimal Vasahi",
           "desc": "Vimal Vasahi the oldest temple in this complex was built by Vimal Shah, Minister of Solanki ruler of Gujarat in 1031 CE. It is dedicated to Adinath, the first of the Jain tirthankaras. Constructed out of white marble from Arasoori Hill, near Ambaji, it is an outstanding example of Jain temple architecture. The central shrine has an image of Rishabhdev and large courtyard with fiftytwo small shrines, each housing a beautiful statue of tirthankaras with fortyeight elegantly carved pillars from the entrance to the courtyard. The Visalia Temple, dedicated to the twentysecond tirthankara. - Neminath was built in 1231 CE by two brothers - Vastupal and Tejpal, Minister of Raja Virdhavala, a ruler of Gujarat belonging to the Porwal Jain community. The door casings, friezes, architraves, pillars, the sculptures of porticos, are astonishing in their workmanship. Rewards in the form of silver and gold equivalent to the weight of marble covered, were offered to the laborer. A great deal of inspiration, devotion, love and encouragement was involved in the construction of this temple. The most striking feature of this Jain temple built in the Solanki style of architecture is the marble pendant in the dome of the porch. It drops from the ceiling like an enchanting cluster of half-open lotuses with cups so finely carved that they appear to be transparent.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/vimal-vasahi.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/vimal-vasahi.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 750-1200 CE",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -3760,7 +3760,7 @@
           "title": "Teja PalaTemple",
           "name": "Teja PalaTemple",
           "desc": "The Luna Vashi temple is dedicated to the twentysecond Tirthankara Shri Neminathji. It resembles the architectural plan of the Vimal Vashi temple although it was built two hundred years later. The two brothers, Vastu Pala and Tej Pala, both ministers of a local ruler, built this magnificent temple in 1230 CE. The temple is among the last few monuments built in the Solanki style, which came to an end towards the end of the thirteenth century with the Muslim occupation of Gujarat. The main hall or Rang mandap features a central dome standing on eight pillars from which hangs a big ornamental pendent featuring elaborate carving. It looks like a cluster of half open lotuses. Seventy two figures of Tirthankaras in sitting posture are arranged in a circular band. Just below this band another three hundred sixty small figures of Jain monks are in another circular band. The sanctum or the garbhagriha has a huge idol of Neminath. There are thirty nine cells each of which contains one or more images. The ceiling in front of the cell is profusely ornamented. The relief in the porticos of the cell portrays important scenes from the life of Neminath. The Hathishala or the elephant cell features ten beautiful marble elephants. They are neatly polished and realistically modeled earlier carried the idols representing the family of Vastu Pala. There are ten slabs behind the elephants bearing a figure of male and female on it. The Navchowki have some of the most magnificent and delicate marble stone cutting work. Each of the 9 ceilings is example of beauty and grace. The Gudh Mandap contains a black marble idol of Neminath. The Kirti stambha is a big black stone pillar on the left side of the temple. The pillar was constructed by Maharana Kumbha of Mewar.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/luna-vashi.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/luna-vashi.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1200-1300",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -3769,7 +3769,7 @@
           "title": "Rajasthan- Monument- Ranakpur",
           "name": "Rajasthan- Monument- Ranakpur",
           "desc": "Ranakpur, located in the mountain ranges of Pali district is renowned for some marvelous carved temples in Amber stone. Ranakpur is one of the five holy places of the Jain community. These temples were built during the reign of the liberal and gifted monarch Rana Kumbha in the fifteenth century. An enormous basement covers forty eight thousand sq. feet area. There are four subsidiary shrines, twenty four pillared halls and domes supported by over four hundred columns. The total number of columns is 1,444 all of which are intricately and diversely carved. The artistically carved nymphs playing the flute in various dance postures at a height of fortyfive feet are an engrossing sight. In the assembly hall, there are two big bells weighing hundred and eight kgs. whosesound echoes in the entire complex. The central Chaumukh or four faced temple is dedicated to the venerated tirthankara Rishabhnathji. Open on all four sides, it enshrines the four faces image of Adinath. Images of twenty four tirthankaras are carved on the mandaps or porticoes in a corridor around the shrine with each mandap having a shikhar or spire adorned with little bells on the top. Rising in three storeys, the temple has four small shrines with 80 spires supported by four hundred and twenty columns. Two temples, dedicated to saints- Parasvanath and Neminath, face the main temple. The temples have beautiful carvings similar to that of Khajuraho. Another temple worth visiting is the nearby eighth century Sun Temple. The temple has polygonal walls richly embellished with carvings of warriors, horses and solar deities riding splendid chariots.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/Ranakpur-Jain-Temple.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/Ranakpur-Jain-Temple.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1400-1450",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -3787,7 +3787,7 @@
           "title": "Rajasthan- Monument- Ajmer -Ajmer Sharif Dargah",
           "name": "Rajasthan- Monument- Ajmer -Ajmer Sharif Dargah",
           "desc": "Khwaja Moin-ud-din Chishti left for heavenly abode in 1256 CE after a six day prayer in seclusion. To commemorate those six days, the annual Urs, which is attended by innumerable pilgrims irrespective of their faith, is held at Ajmer every year. The shrine (Dargah Sharif) is considered to be a place where wishes of everyone are fulfilled. During the medieval period Akbar, the great Mughal emperor also visited the Dargah Sharif and sought blessings for his son. The Dargah Bazaar leads to the inner courtyard of the Dargah Sharif. The high gateway of the Dargah has beautifully carved silver doors. In the courtyard there are two huge cauldrons. The grave of Khwaja Moin-ud-din Chishti is surrounded by a silver railing and is partially covered with a marble screen. Emperor Akbar made an annual pilgrimage to Ajmer. Akbar and Shah Jahan have built mosques in the complex. The saint's tomb is in the centre of the second courtyard and the actual tomb inside is surrounded by a silver railing and partly by a marble screen. The tomb is of marble and dome is gold plated. The prayer room for women devotees at the Dargah was built by the daughter of Shah Jahan. In the premises of the Dargah Sharif there are tombs of Khwaja's daughter, Bibi Hafiz Jama and the tomb of Shah Jahan's daughter.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/ajmer-dargah-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/ajmer-dargah-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1200-1300",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -3796,7 +3796,7 @@
           "title": "Rajasthan- Monument- Ajmer- Adhai Din Ka Jhonpra",
           "name": "Rajasthan- Monument- Ajmer- Adhai Din Ka Jhonpra",
           "desc": "This is a masterpiece of Indo-Islamic architecture. As legend goes its was constructed in two and a-half days (Adhai-Din). It is a relic of an old mosque consisting of a quadrangle with a front screen wall of seven pointed arches. The distinct pillars and arched screen with its ruined minarets make it a splendid architectural masterpiece.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/adhai-din-ka-jhonpra.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/adhai-din-ka-jhonpra.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1200-1300",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -3814,7 +3814,7 @@
           "title": "Amber Fort",
           "name": "Amber Fort",
           "desc": "The Amber fort is set in a picturesque location near Jaipur, the capital of Rajasthan, representing its glory from the days when it was the capital of the ruling Kachhawahas before it was shifted to Jaipur in 1727.The Amber Fort presents a fascinating blend of Hindu and Muslim architecture. Raja Man Singh built the Amber fort in the sixteenth century. The Amber Fort is built in red sandstone and white marble. The palace complex of the Amber fort has very attractive apartments. This complex was built by Raja Man Singh, Mirza Raja Jai Singh and Sawai Jai Singh, over a period of about two centuries. The front courtyard of the fort complex is adorned with the splendid, pillared hall of the Diwan-i-Am, and the two-tiered painted gateway, Ganesh Pol. The palace of mirrors (the Sheesh Mahal) is also of special interest. The Sheesh Mahal has walls inlaid with exquisite mirrors. The Jas Mandir, hanging on the upper floor, is a superb blend of Mughal and Rajput style of architecture, as is evident from the elegantly carved Jali screens and fine mirror and stucco work. The entrance of the Amber fort is through the Dil-e-Aaram garden, which is laid out in the traditional Mughal style. An impressive flight of stairs leads to the Diwan-e-Am (hall of public audience) which has latticed galleries and double row of columns each having a capital in the shape of elephants on the top. To the right are steps that lead to the small Kali Temple. The Kali temple has massive doors made of silver. The walls of Amber fort are adorned with paintings of hunting and war along with precious stones and mirrors set into the plaster. The Maota Lake in the forefront of Amber fort gives spectacular and beautiful reflections of the magnificent Amber fort-palace. The fort also has a Kali Temple known as Shila Devi Temple, which is famous for its mysterious history, huge silver lions and silver doors with raised relief. On its doorway, is an image of Ganesha carved from a single piece of coral! Diwan-I-Aam or Hall of Public Audiences is a pavilion of double row of columns. Ganesh Pol leads to the residential apartments of the Maharaja. Jai Mandir or Hall of Victory has a dazzling mirror ceiling and an inlaid panel. Sukh Niwas or Residence of Pleasure has an ivory inlaid sandalwood door. Zenana or Palace of Women has rooms that are connected by a common corridor. It was cleverly designed to give privacy to each queen of the Maharaja.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/amber-fort.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/amber-fort.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Medieval 1550-1600",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -3823,7 +3823,7 @@
           "title": "Chittorgarh Fort",
           "name": "Chittorgarh Fort",
           "desc": "Chittorgarh holds a proud place in history and is regarded as the symbol of Rajput chivalry, resistance and bravery. The Chittorgarh fort remained the capital of Mewar from the eigth century to the sixteenth and was the stronghold of the Sisodia Rajputs. It was besieged three times by attackers, Alaud- Din Khalji (1303), Bahadur Shah of Gujarat (1534-35), and Akbar in 1567-68. It was after the capture of Chitor by Akbar that the capital was shifted to Udaipur. Chittorgarh covers an area seven mile covering 700 acres of land with its fortifications, palaces, temples and towers. The fortress of Chittorgarh epitomizes the romance and chivalry of the Rajput tradition. The fort is situated on a one hundred and eighty meter high hill that rises swiftly from the plains. The road leads through seven gates namely Padan Pol, Bhairon Pol, Hanuman Pol, Ganesh Pol, Jorla Pol, Lakshman Pol, to the main gate, Rampol. In between the second and the third gate there are two Chhatris or cenotaphs, built in honor of Jaimull and Patta, the heroes of 1568 siege by Emperor Akbar. The main gate of the fort is called the Surajpol - the Gate of Sun. Inside the Chittorgarh fort there are many palaces like the Rana Kumbha Palace, the Fateh Prakash Palace, the Tower of Victory and Rani Padmini's Palace. All these structures are significant for their Rajput architectural features. The Palace of Rana Kumbha is built of finely cut stone covered with coats of stucco and it is painted. The most imposing structures inside the Chittorgarh fort are the Kirti Stambh and the Vijay Stambh. The Kirti Stambh was built by Maharana Kumbha in 1440 to celebrate his victory over Mohammad Khalji. The pedestal of the tower is ten feet high and the tower stands at a height of one hundred and twenty two feet and is thirty feet wide at the base. The tower is nine storeyed and is adorned with sculptures of Hindu deities and stories from the Ramayana and the Mahabharata. The tower provides a breathtaking view of the city. There is a huge complex of Jain temples within the fort. The Vijay Stambh is an amalgamation of religious and secular architecture. The square ground floor plan has been converted into a form with many angles and niches with many images. It is also adorned with elaborate brackets and lattice windows.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/chittorgarh-fort.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/chittorgarh-fort.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 750-1200 1200-1300",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -3841,7 +3841,7 @@
           "title": "Jaipur-Palace",
           "name": "Jaipur-Palace",
           "desc": "Raja Sawai Jai Singh built the city palace complex in the heart of his newly founded capital which is one of the most imposing and magnificent examples of art and architecture to which the succeeding rulers made additions. Presenting a blend of Mughal and Rajasthani architecture, a part of this palace is still occupied by the royal family. As the name suggests Sawai Madho Singh built Mubarak Mahal or Palace of Reception in the nineteenth century to welcome his guests. It is now occupied by the costume gallery of Maharaja Sawai Man Singh II Museum displaying regal costumes such as exquisite and precious Pashmina Shawls, Benarasi silk saris, Sanganeri prints, embroidery and the voluminous clothes worn by the giant Maharaja Sawai Madho Singh I who ruled between 1750 and 68. Maharani's Palace houses, the weaponry section of the museum with weapons belonging to 15th century that are remarkably preserved. Diwan-i-am houses the art gallery showcasing rare ancient handwritten original manuscripts of Hindu scriptures, delicate miniature paintings in Rajasthani, Mughal and Persian schools and engrossing painted ceilings. Diwan-I-Khas has two of the world's largest sterling silver vessels that were made for Maharaja Sawai Madho Singh II to carry water from River Ganga to drink on his voyage to England.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/CityPalace-Jaipur.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/CityPalace-Jaipur.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -3850,7 +3850,7 @@
           "title": "Rajasthan- Monument- Jaipur- Hawa Mahal",
           "name": "Rajasthan- Monument- Jaipur- Hawa Mahal",
           "desc": "The best-known landmark and the signature monument of Jaipur, Hawa Mahal or palace of winds was built by the poet Raja Sawai Pratap Singh. It is the delicate façade that can be seen from outside the city palace.The five-storeyed building flaunts Rajput architecture and artistry to its best including its nine hundred and fifty three pink sandstone windows known as jharokhas that are built in such a manner that it resembles a giant honeycomb. Meant for the ladies of the royal household, the building was built to allow them to watch the colorful bazaars and processions going across the city while maintaining their tradition of being veiled and not to be seen from outside. Built in 1799, the chief architect of this exquisite example of architecture was Mr. Lal Chand Usta. The beautiful interplay of red and pink sand stone is outlined with white borders and motifs. The facade is a fifty-foot high thin shield, which is less than a foot in thickness and has small intimate chambers.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/hawa-mahal.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/hawa-mahal.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -3859,7 +3859,7 @@
           "title": "Rajasthan- Monument- Jaipur- Jantar Mantar",
           "name": "Rajasthan- Monument- Jaipur- Jantar Mantar",
           "desc": "The largest and best preserved, Jantar Mantar or the magical device of Jaipur is one of the five observatories in India built by Sawai Jai Singh, the leading astronomer of his times and the founder of Jaipur. The collection of giant masonry astronomical instruments continues to provide accurate information even today and is comparable with the most sophisticated instruments of today. The Brihat Samrat Yantra Sundial is said to be the most imposing of all instruments and is twenty seven meter high. It helps in calculating local time as its shadow moves up to four meter in an hour. It also helps in ascertaining the various attributes of the heavenly bodies and is definitely the highlight and center of attraction of the observatory. It was built between 1728 and 1734 based on the blueprints drafted by Maharaja Jai Singh.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/jantar-mantar-jaipur.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/jantar-mantar-jaipur.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Medieval 1700-1750",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -3868,7 +3868,7 @@
           "title": "Rajasthan- Monument- Osian",
           "name": "Rajasthan- Monument- Osian",
           "desc": "Osian, the ancient town of Thar desert was a great trading center between eighth and twelveth centuries. Today it is famous for its beautifully sculpted and designed Brahamanical and Jain temples. The main attractions of this otherwise an ancient city lying in ruins are its temples that have gracefully stood the test of time. They are beautifully designed and constructed and the main temples are Jain temples, Sachiya Mata temple, Surya temples, Harihara temples, Pippala Devi temple, Shiv temple, Vishnu temple. Jain temples belong to eighth and eleventh century. Surya (Sun) temple and the Sachiya Mata temple are famous for their beauty. Similarly, all the other temples are famous for one thing or the other. There are approximately fifteen Osian Jain temples near Jodhpur. The most fascinating one is the Mahavira temple dedicated to Mahavir, the twenty fourth tirthankara. The main temple was constructed around 775 CE. Till about fifteenth century, the other smaller shrines were being added to the main complex. Osian holds a great significance for the Jain community being the place of origin of Oswal Jains (a rich trading community). Most of the temples were constructed and maintained from the donations of Oswals. The entrance to the temple complex has carvings of beautiful maidens on it. The roof is also beautifully decorated. The terrace on which the temple stands is built up of sandstone. The main Mahavira temple has an inner sanctum and three balconied windows. The main porch of the temple has beautifully ornate pillars and the second porch, which was later added on, has more stylish construction. The other Jain temples are quite smaller and are uniform to an extent. Only little differentiation can be seen among them.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/osian-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/osian-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -3877,7 +3877,7 @@
           "title": "Osian-Sachiya Mata Temple",
           "name": "Osian-Sachiya Mata Temple",
           "desc": "The Osian Sachiya Mata temple was originally built in the 8th century. However, the temple complex that now stands dates back to the twelveth century. The Sachiya Mata Osian Temple in Rajasthan is dedicated to Sachi Mata. Sachi Mata, also known as Indrani, was the consort of the rain-god Indra. According to the legend, Indra married her for her voluptuousness. The main shrine, along with the other two temples - the Chandi Ka Mandir and the Amba Mata Mandir, was constructed around 1178. One can enter the Sachiya Mata temple complex, only through a series of magnificently sculpted arches. The temple complex was built in several phases having beautiful images and sculptures of Hindu deities in side. Located at the north-end of the complex is a sculpture of Varaha. To the east is an image of Vishnu with Lakshmi. Lying to the west is a stone slab full of sculptures coming out of the walls.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/sachiya-mata-osian.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/sachiya-mata-osian.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 750-1200",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -3886,7 +3886,7 @@
           "title": "Osian-Surya Temples",
           "name": "Osian-Surya Temples",
           "desc": "Dedicated to the Sun God, the Surya temple near Jodhpur was constructed in the tenth century. It is quite similar to the Sun temple at Ranakpur. The inner complex of the temple houses the Surya. Also within the temple complex are the sculptures of the Ganesh and Durga. There is also an open mandap (a platform for wedding and other ceremonial rituals) in the temple. The first Surya temple, dating back to the 8th century, has been incorporated into the Sachiya Mata temple complex. On its ceilings are images of serpents curled around lotus flowers and below this is portrayed the life-story of Lord Krishna. Quite opposite to this, the entrance has erotic images of couples clutching each other in amorous embraces. Inspite of all the beauty and splendor, the Osian Surya temples are ignored. The grace of the temples is slowly fading away due to neglect.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/surya-temple-osian.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/surya-temple-osian.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 750-1200",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -3895,7 +3895,7 @@
           "title": "Harihara Temples",
           "name": "Harihara Temples",
           "desc": "There are three Harihara temples. These temples are dedicated to Lord Harihara (A union of Lord Shiva and Lord Vishnu). The first two of the Harihara Osian temples were constructed in the eight century and the third one was added in ninth century. The temples are raised on huge plinths. Almost every space in the temples is sculpted to a great extent. the porch are especially quite complex. Another fact, a sad one, about these temples is that time has had its bad effect on them. For example, the first temple has witnessed much destruction of its ornamentation. It is now only left with its beautiful archway and the porch columns of the shrines.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/harihara-osian.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/harihara-osian.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 750-1200",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -3931,7 +3931,7 @@
           "title": "Rajasthan- Monument- Shekhawati",
           "name": "Rajasthan- Monument- Shekhawati",
           "desc": "Shekhawati takes its name after its ruler Rao Shekha. Shekhawati means the garden of Shekha. The town of Shekhawati is known for their amazing painted havelis. Such is the appeal of the havelis that this region is called the open art gallery of Rajasthan. The plethora of painted havelis in rich artistic tradition makes it commendable and fascinating. Most of the buildings are dated from 18th century to early 20th century. The Shekhawati landscape is dotted with so many havelis that tracking them is something like a treasure hunt. Various forms of fine art adorn the walls and the ceilings of these structures, complimenting the otherwise flat and barren land. The havelis are noted for their frescoes depicting mythological themes and that of huge animals. Some later frescos show the arrival of the British and highlighting steam locomotives and train doubtlessly for the benefit of those who did not travel to distant places by train or see this newly introduced mode of transport. There are also forts, minor castles, mosques, wells called baolis and chhattris to discover. The Rajputs mostly depicted the themes of historical events, personages' folk-heroes and the prominent war scenes, while the marwaris concentrated more on religious themes but with the passage of time the themes too began to divert towards the modern views. At the decline of Mughal Empire after the death of Mughal Emperor Aurangzeb in 1707 the descendants of Rao Shekha encroached upon areas west of Aravali Range also. The chieftains of the region retained nominal loyalty to Amber, who in turn honoured them with the title of tazmi sardars. It was probably this exposure to the courts of Jaipur that encouraged them to initiate their great succession of fresco. By the beginning of the nineteenth century British had established major ports at Calcutta (now Kolkata) and Bombay (now Mumbai) that were able to handle larger volumes of trade than ports in Gujarat. Moreover with Jaipur reducing its levies the Shekhawati route was almost abandoned by traders. However the Shekhawati merchants who were seasoned businessmen migrated en masse to the more lucrative centres of Calcutta and Bombay. Here they earned unparallel wealth and send home huge sums, which were used for building havelis, social welfare, wells, reservoirs, schools etc. Development of the murals in Shekhawati is inextricably linked with the history of the Marwaris, the influential business community. The Marwaris prospered till the beginning of the ninteenth century because of the caravan routes that crossed the area to reach the ports of Gujarat. But from 1820 onwards, many left their families behind and migrated to Calcutta, Bombay, Madras (now Chennai) which were gaining importance as main trade centers. There they had great financial success. The descendants of these rich merchants have permanently settled down in the towns where their ancestors immigrated to. Between 1830 and 1930, they erected buildings in their homeland, Shekhawati, as evidence of their success. As the ultimate symbol of their opulence, the Mawaris commissioned artists to paint those buildings. The architecture of the ninteenth and tweentieth centuries consists of the exaggerated display of Marwari wealth and completely overshadowed anything constructed before.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/Shekhawati-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/Shekhawati-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -3940,7 +3940,7 @@
           "title": "Rajasthan- Monument- Havelis - Personal Residences",
           "name": "Rajasthan- Monument- Havelis - Personal Residences",
           "desc": "The word haveli, means an enclosed place. The havelis were the symbol of the status of the Marwaris and a home for their extended family, providing security and comfort in a style. Secluded from the outside world the typical havelis in Rajasthan consist of two courtyards  an outer one for the men which serves as an extended threshold and the inner one, the domain of the women. The biggest havelis sometimes had three or four courtyards and were two to three stories high.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/shekhawati-haveli.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/shekhawati-haveli.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -3949,7 +3949,7 @@
           "title": "City Palace-Udaipur",
           "name": "City Palace-Udaipur",
           "desc": "The imposing city palace, towering over the lake Pichhola, is the largest palace complex in Rajasthan. Courtyards, pavilions, terraces, corridors, rooms and gardens adorn this palace. The palace museum contains a wonderful collection of old Rajput weaponry. The Mor Chowk is filled with beautiful mosaics of peacocks. The Manak Mahal has inlay glass and mirror work while Krishna Vilas has a remarkable collection of miniatures.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Rajasthan/CityPalaceudaipur-1.jpg",
