@@ -4231,7 +4231,7 @@
           "title": "Tamil Nadu- Monument- Mahabalipuram- Rathas",
           "name": "Tamil Nadu- Monument- Mahabalipuram- Rathas",
           "desc": "The eight rathas are monolithic temples fashioned as chariots. Of the eight rathas, five have been named after the Pandava brothers, the heroes of the Mahabharata, and their wife, Draupadi. They are amazing in style of architecture for each is apparently a faithful reproduction of a structure built of wood. In fact, even the grain of the timber beams and rafters has been simulated in stone. While the Dharmaraja, Arjuna and Draupadi rathas are square on plan, the Bhima and Ganesa rathas are rectangular and Sahadeva ratha apsidal. The Dharmaraja ratha is a tritala vimana having functional shrines at all the talas. It is the largest and it sets the tone for the others. Modelled on a Buddhist vihara or monastery, it has a square hall topped by a vaulted roof. The Draupadi ratha is the smallest and is a simple structure, fashioned as a thatched hut while the Arjuna ratha is a dvitala vimana with a mukhamandapa. The Bhima ratha is rectangular on plan with a wagon-vaulted roof. The Nakula-Sahadeva ratha with an apsidal plan and elevation indicate the experimental tendency of the architect. The fact that many of the temples and sculptures of Mamallapuram are unfinished, points to the sudden withdrawal of patronage from rock-cut temples when King Rajasimhavarman came to power.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/ratha-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/ratha-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Ancient 600-750 CE",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4240,7 +4240,7 @@
           "title": "Tamil Nadu- Monument- Mahabalipuram- Descent of the Ganges",
           "name": "Tamil Nadu- Monument- Mahabalipuram- Descent of the Ganges",
           "desc": "The world's largest bas-relief creation measures up to twenty seven meter in height and nine meter in width holds the pride of Mahabalipuram. The whale shaped rock contains figures of Gods and Demigods, beasts, birds and in fact represents the entire creation of the almighty. The rock reliefs in open air, illustrates a popular episode in the iconography of Siva, that of the Descent of the Ganges: the wise King Bhaghiratha having begged him to do so, Siva ordered the Ganges to descend to earth and to nourish the world. The sculptors used the natural fissure which divided the cliff to forcefully suggest this cosmic event to which a swarming crowd of gods, godesses, mythical beings (Kinnara, Gandharvas, Apsaras, Ganas, Naga and Nagin), savage and domestic animals bear witness.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/relief-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/relief-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4249,7 +4249,7 @@
           "title": "Tamil Nadu- Monument- Mahabalipuram- Mandapas",
           "name": "Tamil Nadu- Monument- Mahabalipuram- Mandapas",
           "desc": "Of the many excavated rock temples in Mahabalipuram called mandapas, two of the best are the Mahishamardhani and the Adivaraha. The former contains on walls facing each other two of the most superb sculptural reliefs known to Indian art. The Mahishasurmardhini temple has been excavated in a rock on the eastern side of the top of the hill. There are three shrines in the Mahishamardhani mandapa. A small mandapa projects forward from the central shrine. On the northern and southern walls of the ardhamandapa there are the great sculptural reliefs of Goddess Durga fighting the demon and of Lord Vishnu in His cosmic sleep. In the one relief the energy with which the young Goddess goes forth to war with the buffalo headed demon is in magnificent contrast with the cosmic sleep of Lord Vishnu on His serpent couch. The royal groups in the Adivaraha temple are, in one, of a seated king with two queens flanking him and, in the other, of a standing king with two consorts standing by him.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/mandapa-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/mandapa-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4258,7 +4258,7 @@
           "title": "Shore Temple, Mahabalipuram",
           "name": "Shore Temple, Mahabalipuram",
           "desc": "The shore temples were built in seventh century, during the reign of Rajasimha, and represent the final phase of Pallava art. These beautiful and romantic temples, ravaged by wind and sea, are so significant that they were included in the list of World Heritage site by UNESCO. The two spires of the temples, contain a shrine for Lord Vishnu, and for Lord Shiva. Designed to catch the first rays of the rising sun and to illuminate the waters after dark, the temple ended up with an unusual lay-out. As the main shrine faces the sea on the east, the gateway, the fore court and the assembly hall of the shore temple all lie behind the sanctum. Unusual, too, is the fact that the temple has shrine of Shiva and Vishnu both. The main sanctum and one of the two lesser ones on the west are dedicated to Shiva. The enclosing wall has a series of Nandi bulls on it.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/shore-temple-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/shore-temple-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Ancient 600-750 CE",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4267,7 +4267,7 @@
           "title": "Kailashnath Temple",
           "name": "Kailashnath Temple",
           "desc": "Kanchipuram is known as one of India's seven sacred cities. The historical capital of the Pallavas, it was under the Pallavas from sixth to eigth century and later became the citadel of Cholas, Vijayanagar Kings, the Muslim and the British. It has been a centre of Tamil learning, cultural and religious background for centuries. Dedicated to Lord Shiva, Kailashanathar (also known as Kailashnatha) is one of the earliest temples built by the Pallava ruler Rajasimha Pallava and was completed by his son, Mahendra Varma Pallava in the eight century. It is the oldest structure in Kanchipuram and the finest example of Pallava architecture in south India. It is built in sandstone hence the quality of the sculptures is best of its kind and is highly detailed. The architecture of this temple resembles the shore temple at Mamallapuram. This temple is unique in its architecture. It was constructed mostly of limestone. The walls and vimana of this temple are filled with great sculptures, and paintings. Fragments of the eigth Cen. murals are a visible reminder of how magnificent the original temple must have looked. There are total 80 shrines out of which 58 small shrines are dedicated to Shiva and Parvati and their sons Ganesh and Murugan. All shrines have pillars decorated with sculptures of lions and mythical creatures at their base.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/kanci-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/kanci-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4276,7 +4276,7 @@
           "title": "Vaikunth Perumal Temple",
           "name": "Vaikunth Perumal Temple",
           "desc": "This is a Vishnu temple built by Nandivarman the IInd of the Pallava dynasty. This beautiful temple is dedicated to Lord Vishnu and Goddess Vaikunthavalli Tayar (Goddess Lakshmi). It has the images of Lord Vishnu in standing, sitting and reclining postures. The vimana is similar to the Kailashnath temple, but the precincts are different. Instead of the small shrines, this one has a surrounding corridor with lion pillars very near the vimana which contain historical inscriptions related to the wars between the Pallavas and the Chalukyas. The reliefs on the",
-          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/vaikunth-perumal.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/vaikunth-perumal.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 750-1200",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4285,7 +4285,7 @@
           "title": "Brihadeshwara Temple",
           "name": "Brihadeshwara Temple",
           "desc": "This temple was built during the period of Raja Raja I, around the tenth century is the crowning glory of Chola temple architecture. Most of the Chola emperors were great patrons of art and architecture. During the period of Cholas, the Dravidian culture reached the height of its glory. The Brihadeshwara temple is built in Dravidian style. Brihadeshwara has a gigantic lingam cut in a monolithic rock and is about 8.7mtrs high. There is a huge Nandi facing the inner sanctum of the temple. It is one of the largest monolithic Nandi in the country. The temple is capped by a monolithic cupola made of a single granite block weighing 80 tons which was taken to the top by pulling it on 6 km. long inclined ramp a technique used by pyramid builders of Egypt. The stone carvings on the walls of the temple depict the influence of Shaivism, Buddhism and Vaishnavism. The temple has several pillared halls and shrines and 250 lingas enshrined along the outer walls. Interesting is that inscriptions record the names of dancers, musicians and poets, which reminds us about how developed the arts were.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/brihadeshwara-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/brihadeshwara-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 750-1200",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4294,7 +4294,7 @@
           "title": "Tamil Nadu- Monument- Rameshwaram- Ramalingeshwara Temple",
           "name": "Tamil Nadu- Monument- Rameshwaram- Ramalingeshwara Temple",
           "desc": "Rameshwaram is significant for the Hindus. The presiding deity is the linga of Sri Ranganatha, which happens to be one of the twelve jyotirlingas of India. Rama sanctified this place by worshipping Lord Siva after the war against Ravana. Therefore, it is held sacred by Saivites and Vaishnavites as well. Rameshwaram is also popularly referred to as the 'Benaras of the south'. In order to attain moksha it is believed that the visit to Rameshwaram is mandatory. In the principal sanctum there is the linga of Sri Ranganatha. This is the one, which Sita made and Sri Rama sanctified. There is much delicate artistry in many parts of the sanctum. The Vimana, of three storeys, contains images of Hanuman, the Gandhamadhana linga, and the Agastya linga. The linga of Visvanatha (also spelt as Vishvanatha), which Hanuman brought, is enshrined in another sanctum to the north. In yet another shrine there is an image of Visalakshi, the consort of Visvanatha, Ramanatha's consort, Parvathavardhani, is enshrined in a sanctum to the right. Usually, in Shiva temples, the Goddess is enshrined to the left of the Lord.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/rameshwaram1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/rameshwaram1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 750-1200",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4303,7 +4303,7 @@
           "title": "Meenakshi Temple",
           "name": "Meenakshi Temple",
           "desc": "One of the largest temple complexes in India Meenakshi temple has remained undiminished for the past two thousand years. The Meenakshi temple complex is dedicated to Shiva and his consort Parvati known here as Sundareswar and Meenakshi. This magnificent temple complex has twelve gopurams or gateways, ranging in height from 45 to 50 m, the tallest being the southern one. The temple complex is within a high-walled enclosure. There are two sanctums one for Meenakshi and the other for Sundareswarar, surrounded by a number of smaller shrines and thousand pillared halls. The temple has 12 impressive gopuras. The soaring towers of granite rise from solid granite bases and are covered with stucco figures of deities, mythical animals and monsters painted in vivid colors. The soaring and exquisitely carved towers that enclose the temple are dedicated to Meenakshi. The south gateway contains the twin temples of Shiva and Meenakshi and is about nine storeys high. The four tallest gopuras stand on the massive outer walls of the temple, one on each side. The southern gopuram is the tallest one and the only one that may be climbed. From atop this 60 ft high gopuram,one gets a complete view of the other gopuras as well as the two vimanas. Each of these gopuras are filled with countless stucco images portraying legends from the puranas. The southern gateway is beautifully proportioned and is nine storeys high. Like the other gopurams, it is also adorned with the images of gods, goddesses, gargoyles, and griffins. Like the statues of classical Greece, the people who follow the ancient Dravidian culture of south India also paint their gods in brilliant colors. Thus, one can experience a multitude of colors atop these gateways. Potramaraikulam or the Golden Lotus Tank is an ancient temple tank where the devotees take bath in the holy water. The area around this tank was the meeting place of the Tamil Sangam, the ancient academy of poets. A pillared corridor surrounds the tank. The thousand pillar mandapam is the 'Wonder of the Palace'. The actual number of pillars is 985 only. They are beautifully decorated and sculpted columns. The thousand pillared hall houses the temple art museum where one can see icons, photographs, drawings, etc., exhibiting the 1200 years old history. Just outside the hall, towards the west, are the musical pillars. Each pillar when struck produces a different musical note. The kalyana mandapam is to the south of the pillared hall. It is here the marriage of Shiva and Parvati is celebrated every year during the Chitirai festival in mid- April.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/meenakshi-temple-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/meenakshi-temple-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Medieval 1600-1650",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4312,7 +4312,7 @@
           "title": "Tamil Nadu- Monument- Chennai (Madras)",
           "name": "Tamil Nadu- Monument- Chennai (Madras)",
           "desc": "Situated on the east coast, Chennai, the fourth largest of the Indian cities is the capital of the state of Tamil Nadu. It boasts of a glorious past and has a rich cultural heritage. This is quite visible from the number of monuments in the city. Earlier known as Madras, the city has a plethora of monuments which significantly reveal the ancient history of the place. Chennai was the first British major settlement in India and was developed in 1639 by the British East India Company and called Madraspatnam. Though there are dominant British influences in the form of old Cathedrals, buildings in the Indo-Saracenic style of architecture and wide tree lined avenues, Chennai retains a charm characteristic of the typical south Indian heritage and way of living.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/chennai.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/chennai.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Medieval 1600-1650",
           "materials": ""
         }
@@ -4323,7 +4323,7 @@
           "title": "Pongal Festival",
           "name": "Pongal Festival",
           "desc": "Pongal is an important festival of Tamil Nadu, which is celebrated to mark the withdrawal of the south east monsoons as well as the reaping of the harvest. The festival is celebrated for four days and the celebrations on the first day of the Tamil month Thai and continues for next three days. It is the biggest harvest festival, spread over four days. In fact, the name of the festival is derived from Pongal, a rice pudding made from freshly harvested rice, milk and jaggery. The first day, Bhogi Pongal, is a day for the family. Surya Pongal, the second day, is dedicated to the worship of sun. The third day of Pongal, Mattu Pongal, is to worship the cattle. Cattle are bathed, their horns polished and painted in bright colours, and garlands of flowers placed around their necks. Pongal is associated with cleaning and burning of rubbish, symbolizing the destruction of evil. All the four days of Pongal have their own individual significance. On the first day, delicious preparations are made and homes are washed and decorated. Doorways are painted with vermilion and sandalwood paste with colourful garlands of leaves and flowers decorating the outside of almost every home. On this day 'Bhogi' or the Rain God is worshipped. According to a legend, the third day of Pongal is celebrated because Lord Shiva once asked Nandi, his bull, to go to earth and deliver his message to the people - to have an oil bath every day and food once a month. But Nandi got it all mixed up when he delivered the message, and told the people that Shiva asked them to have an oil bath once a month and eat every day. Shiva was displeased, and told Nandi that since the people would now need to grow more grain, Nandi would have to remain on earth and help them plough the fields. Sweet rice, known as Pongal, is cooked in a new earthenware pot at the same place where puja is to be performed. Fresh turmeric and ginger are tied around this pot. Then a delicious concoction of rice, moong dal, jaggery and milk are boiled in the pot on an open fire. This Pongal, according to ritual, is allowed to boil and spill out of the pot. Pongal, once ready, is offered to God first, on a new banana leaf along with other traditional delicacies like vadas, payasam, etc. Besides this, sugarcane, grain, sweet potatoes, etc. are also offered to the Sun God.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/pongal-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/pongal-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4332,7 +4332,7 @@
           "title": "Tamil Nadu- Fairs & Festivals- Meenakshi Kalyanam- Madurai",
           "name": "Tamil Nadu- Fairs & Festivals- Meenakshi Kalyanam- Madurai",
           "desc": "The annual solemnization of the marriage of Meenakshi (Parvati) with Lord Sundareshwar (Shiva) is one of the most spectacular temple festivals at Madurai's famous Meenakshi temple in Tamil Nadu. Processions of the goddess and the god are some of the colourful features of this festival. Meenaskhi Kalyanam, the wedding festival of Goddess Meenakshi and Lord Sundareshwar is celebrated for twelve days from the second day of the lunar month (i.e. two days after the new moon). This is a spectacular festival celebrated in the month of Chaitra (April-May). The festival is characterized with royal decorated umbrellas, fans and traditional instrumental music. Scenes from mythology are enacted and the deities of Lord Shiva, Goddess Shakti and Goddess Meenakshi are taken out in a colourful procession. Thousands of devotees from all over the country gather in the city of Madurai on this occasion. Mahabalipuram, was the second capital of the Pallava kings of Kanchipuram and a well established sea port during the seventh and tenth centuries. It was during their reign that great poets, dramatists, artists, artisans, scholars and saints emerged. Infact, the Pallavas are the pioneers and forerunners of new styles both in art and architecture with Mahabalipuram being the best example. Known for its rock carvings and monolithic sculptures it has the famous shore temple, the only one to have survived the ravages of nature. Also known as the Seven Pagodas (temples), six now lie submerged in the sea. Mahabalipuram temples whose architecture was inspired by the Pallavas were built during the period 630 - 1100 CE. The proper name of the site is Mamallapuram, after Mamalla, a title of the Pallava king, Narasimha Varman I (630-668), who created the earliest of its monuments. But it is popularly called Mahabalipuram, or the city of Bali, whom Lord Vishnu chastised for his pride and of whom there is a relief in one of the excavated temples here. Mahabalipuram contains nearly forty monuments of different types but mainly the structures are divided into four categories open air bas reliefs, structured temples, man-made caves or mandapas and rathas ('chariots' carved from single boulders, to resemble temples or chariots used in temple processions).",
-          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/meenakshi-kalyanam.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Tamil_Nadu/meenakshi-kalyanam.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
           "materials": ""
         }
@@ -4355,7 +4355,7 @@
             "title": "Tripura Dance",
             "name": "Tripura Dance",
             "desc": "The culture of dance in Tripura is vibrant and associated with the ethnic tribes that are both indigenous and native. The Tripuri, the Reang, the Chakma, Halam (Malsum), and the Lushai community and Garo tribes are some of the groups that have exotic dress code and dance forms. The main folk dances are Hozagiri dance of Reang, Garia, Jhum, Maimita, Masak Sumani and Lebang boomani dances of Tripuri, Bizu dance of Chakma, Cheraw and welcome dances of Lusai, Hai-Hak dance of Malsum, Wangala dance of Garo, Sangraiaka, Chimithang, Padisha and abhangma dances of Mog Garia dances of Kalai and Jamatia, Gajan, Dhamail, Sari and Rabindra dances of Bengali and Basanta Rash and Pung chalam dances of Manipuri community. Each of them have their own traditional musical instruments. The important musical instruments are Khamb( Drum), Bamboo flute, Lebang, Sarinda, Do- Tara, and Khengrong, etc.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Tripura/Hojagiridance.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Tripura/Hojagiridance.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -4364,7 +4364,7 @@
             "title": "Garia Dance",
             "name": "Garia Dance",
             "desc": "The life and culture of Tripuris revolve around Jhum (shifting) cultivation. When the sowing of seeds at a plot of land selected for Jhum is over by middle of April, they pray to the God Garia for a happy harvest. The celebrations attached to the Garia Pujacontinue for",
-            "img": "/state-images/Tripura/goriadance.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Tripura/goriadance.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           }
@@ -4375,7 +4375,7 @@
             "title": "Tripura - Handicrafts - Cane and Bamboo Work",
             "name": "Tripura - Handicrafts - Cane and Bamboo Work",
             "desc": "Tripura is famous for its cane and bamboo handicrafts. They include table mats, floor mats, room dividers, decorated wall panels, and attractive furniture of cane. The Tripura mudah is a low stool made of bamboo and split cane. Bamboo is used in the body and rim structure; while split-cane is used in binding all elements and in the seat weave. Very intricate and exquisite bamboo and cane ornaments are also produced in Tripura. In Agartala, a few craftsmen prepare small woven animal forms from fine splints of bamboo. They are intended both as toys and as decorations. The forms are evolved by either weaving or braiding uniformally fine splints.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Tripura/basket-tripura.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Tripura/basket-tripura.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           },
@@ -4402,7 +4402,7 @@
             "title": "Tripura- Handicrafts- Handlooms",
             "name": "Tripura- Handicrafts- Handlooms",
             "desc": "The people of Tripura look to weaving as an inseparable part of life as it is the oldest industry in Tripura. Passed on from generation to generation the people of the state produce their own clothes with elegant designs and lasting texture using unique color combinations. Risa and Riha are two traditional handloom items of the state that are used during rituals. In Tripura, for a prospective birde, good knowledge of the craft of weaving is essential.",
-            "img": "/state-images/Tripura/weaving-tripura.jpg",
+            "img": "/state-images/Tripura/weaving-tripura.webp",
             "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
             "materials": ""
           }
@@ -4414,7 +4414,7 @@
           "title": "Ujjayanta Palace",
           "name": "Ujjayanta Palace",
           "desc": "is the royal house, which stands in the Capital city of Agartala. Built by Maharaja Radha Kishore Manikya during 1899-1901, this two storied royal house is beautifully carved and sculpted in Indo-Greek style. High domes, well laid out gardens and parks, fountains and tanks make it an impressive structure. The palace is set with huge Mughal style gardens, beautified by pools and gardens and tiled floors. Particularly notable are the magnificently tiled floors, the ceiling of the Chinese Room which was crafted by artisans brought from China, and the carved front doors. The two-storied palace has three large domes, the largest of which is 86 ft (26 m) high from the ground and which rests a top a four-storied central tower.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Tripura/btr0100004.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Tripura/btr0100004.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4423,7 +4423,7 @@
           "title": "Tripura- Monument- Rock Cut Carving in Tripura",
           "name": "Tripura- Monument- Rock Cut Carving in Tripura",
           "desc": "Tripura has beautiful rock cut carvings and stone images at Unakoti, Debtamura and Pilak. Most of these carvings are huge in size and made on vertical walls exposed in the open atmosphere.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Tripura/Pilak.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Tripura/Pilak.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4432,7 +4432,7 @@
           "title": "Unakoti Palace",
           "name": "Unakoti Palace",
           "desc": "Unakoti dating back to the eight or ninth century probably has the biggest bas-relief sculpture in India. The Legend- According to Hindu mythology, when Lord Shiva was proceeding to Kashi along with a crore of gods and goddesses he made a night halt at Unakoti. Shiva asked all the gods and goddesses to wake up before sun rise and proceed to Kashi. In the morning no one except Lord Shiva woke up, forcing him to set out for Kashi by himself and cursing the others to become stone images. Lord Shiva's curse fell upon them and as a result there are one less than a crore stone image carvings at Unakoti. The images found at Unakoti are of two types namely rock-carved figures and stone images. The most imposing sculpture is the immense 30 feet-high image of Lord Shiva called Unokotiswara Kal Bhairava carved in a vertical rock face. The colossal image in grey stone has an elaborate head. On each side of the central Shivas head-dress are two full size female figures - one of Goddess Durga standing on a lion and another female figure on the other side. In addition to this, three enormous images of Nandi the bull vahana of Lord Shiva are found half buried in the ground. A small, rock strewn stream flows near the central Shiva. Near the huge Shiva image, among scattered rocks, are the three imposing images of Lord Ganesha. There is a huge figure of the four - armed seated Ganesha and on its side two standing figures of Sarabhuja Ganesha with three tusks and the Asthabhuja Ganesha with four tusks- a rare sight to behold. Among the rock cut carvings, the central Shiva and gigantic Ganesha are certainly master pieces. There are various others stone as well as rock cut images at Unakoti.Every year a big fair popularly known as Ashokastami Mela is held here in the month of April which is visited by thousands of pilgrims.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Tripura/unakoti.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Tripura/unakoti.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Early Medieval (750-1200)",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4441,7 +4441,7 @@
           "title": "Tripura- Monument- Palaces- Debatamura",
           "name": "Tripura- Monument- Palaces- Debatamura",
           "desc": "Debatamura is famous for its panels of rock carvings on the steep mountain wall on the bank of Gomati. There are huge images carved of Shiva, Vishnu, Kartika, Mahisasur Mardini, Durga and other gods and goddesses. This is also called Chabimura. These images dating back to fifteenth-sixteenth centuries are carved with a lot of dexterity on the rocky faces of Debtamura which is as steep as 90 degrees.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Tripura/btr0160001.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Tripura/btr0160001.webp",
