@@ -4585,7 +4585,7 @@
           "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Bara Imambara, Lucknow",
           "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Bara Imambara, Lucknow",
           "desc": "Asaf-ud-Daulah, an enthusiastic builder of monuments shifted his capital to Lucknow from Faizabad. Driven with an ambition to testify his architectural zeal, the Bara Imambara was built by this generous Nawab, who employed more than 22,000 people, including nobles. He adopted a dual policy of construction. During the day, the Imambara was built, and at night, the workers destroyed parts of what had been built. This was to ensure that no one remained unemployed, and the edifice was not completed too soon. This is indeed a monumental feat considering the fact that it was once the largest vaulted hall in the world, with a 50 feet high roof, spanning an area of 162 feet. The galleries and corridors within the great Imambara form a complicated and intricate labyrinth (bhool-bhuliaya) through which at times it is difficult to find ones way.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/bada-imambara.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/bada-imambara.webp",
           "historical_significance": "1750-1800",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4594,7 +4594,7 @@
           "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Rumi Darwaza",
           "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Rumi Darwaza",
           "desc": "Towards the west of the Imambara is the Rumi Darwaza or the Turkish Gate built. The 60 feet high gateway stands as an equally grand entrance to the great hall. During the Nawabi era, it is believed that a huge lantern placed atop the Rumi Darwaza would light up the pathway, while jets of water gushed from the numerous fountains created on the rim of the gateway.",
