@@ -4612,7 +4612,7 @@
           "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Residency",
           "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Residency",
           "desc": "Asaf-ud-Daulas brother Sadat Ali proved to be a great builder who introduced a large number of architectural styles. One of his best-known monuments is the Residency, which was built for the British Residents. The ruins of the Residency stand desolate as a mute witness to the Mutiny of 1857 when it was almost completely destroyed. The redbrick ruins and the shattered walls are still scarred by cannon shots. The cemetery at the nearby ruined church has the graves of 2000 men, women and children. The 1857 Memorial Museum at the Residency has been established and designed to present a visual accounts of the freedom struggle of 1857. Located in a portion which was annex of the main Residency building, original photographs and lithographs of many sites, connected with the uprising, and paintings depicting significant events of the uprising have been displayed along with portraits of local heroes. Period objects such as guns, swords, shields, cannons, rank badges, medals and other items also form part of the display.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/residency.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/residency.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4621,7 +4621,7 @@
           "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Chhota Imambara",
           "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Chhota Imambara",
           "desc": "The British crowned the third king of Awadh, Muhammad Ali in 1837 at a ripe old age of 63. He was a just and popular ruler under whom Lucknow once again regained its splendor for a brief spell. Interested in building activities, he built his own Imambara as well as the Juma Masjid. The Chhota Imambara as it had been called later was left incomplete by Muhammad Ali, was later completed by Begum Mallika Jehan of the Royal family. It has a golden dome, exquisite chandeliers, huge mirrors, silver minbar, colourful interiors and delicate calligraphy on its arched entrance.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/Chota-Imambara.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/Chota-Imambara.webp",
           "historical_significance": "1800-1850",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4630,7 +4630,7 @@
           "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Tomb of Itmad-ud-Daula",
           "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Tomb of Itmad-ud-Daula",
           "desc": "Tomb of Itmad-ud-Daula is one of the most attractive structures in Agra. Designed by Nur Jahan the favorite queen of Jahangir, for her father, the structure is renowned for its pietra dura decoration i.e. the inlaid stonework and the carvings. It is the first Mughal building to be constructed of white inlaid marble, a type of architecture which becomes characteristic of Mughal buildings in the seventeenth century. Believed to have inspired the design of the Taj Mahal, the tomb stands on a low platform and is rectangular with four minars or domed towers growing out of its four corners. The roof is flat, and at the centre is a small roofed pavilion. The marble façade is inlaid with patterns in multi-coloured stones and colourful intarsia representations of vases with flowers, wine cups, cypresses and fruit trees, while much of the second storey is composed of exquisitely carved pierced screens.The structure is set within a formal charbagh and is approached by a monumental gate.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/Itmad-Ud-Daulah-Tomb.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/Itmad-Ud-Daulah-Tomb.webp",
           "historical_significance": "1600-1650",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4639,7 +4639,7 @@
           "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Agra- Monument- Taj Mahal",
           "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Agra- Monument- Taj Mahal",
           "desc": "Overlooking the River Yamuna, the Taj Mahal was built by Emperor Shah Jahan to enshrine the body of his favourite wife, Arjumand Banu Begum, better known as Mumtaz Mahal who died shortly after giving birth to her fourteenth child, in 1631. The layout follows a distinctly Islamic theme representing Paradise. The walled complex is approached from the south through a massive arched gateway topped with delicate domes and adorned with Koranic verses, directly aligned with the Taj. Ustad Isa of Turkey is credited to have been the main architect along with Ismail Afandi, who built the domes. Chiranji Lal was called from Delhi to pattern the mosaic while Amanat Khan was the master calligrapher from Persia. Stone cutters came all the way from Baluchistan. Square in shape, the Taj Mahal known for its symmetry, sits on a raised platform surrounded by four minarets. The fully developed four minarets mark an important stage in Mughal architecture. The most remarkable feature of these minarets is their harmony with the tomb. The Taj surmounts a square marble platform marked at each corner by a high minaret. It is topped with a huge central dome , a bulging white double - dome with a broad padma-kosa (lotus - petals) and beautiful Kalasa-finial mounted on the tomb, capped by a crowning brass spire. It is embellished with extraordinarily fine detail of relief carving, highlighted by floral patterns of precious stones. Carved vases of flowers including roses, tulips and narcissi, rise subtly out of the marble base, a pattern repeated more colourfully and inlaid with precious stones around the four great arched recesses on each side. Chhatris flank the dome on all the four angles which appear to be attached to the dome when viewed from all sides. At the Taj the builder calculated the distance between the two features for the precise relationship they were intended to present together. The plan, which is also known as panch-ratna gives the Taj Mahal such a remarkable, impressive and unparalleled grace. The marble mausoleum is square in plan with chamfered corners. Each facade of the tomb is composed of a grand iwan framed by bands of calligraphy. The doorways inside these iwans are also adorned with calligraphy and is flanked on both sides by small double arches one over the other. Each section in the facade is crowned by beautiful pinnacles with lotus buds and finials. The 99 names of Allah adorn the top of Mumtaz's tomb, and set into Shah Jahan's is a pen box, the hallmark of a male ruler. These cenotaphs, in accordance with Moghul tradition, are only representations of the real coffins, which lie in the same positions in an unadorned and humid crypt below. The pietra dura or inlay has been very well combined with the jali-work. The inlay work on the spandrels of the arches in the interior and exterior add magnificence to the overall aesthetic effect. Bouquets of flowers, zigzag motifs and series of small arches are some of the most commonly used decorative elements. Slender twigs, twisting leaves and bold flowers combine to give the monument a beautiful look. Contemporary texts specify the stones used in decoration and inlay work they are- Lapis lazuli, various types of jasper, agate, carnelian, jade, amethyst, turquoise, onyx, coral etc. The Makrana marble with its pearly clarity has contributed most to the beauty of the Taj Mahal. A number of marks are engraved on red stone slabs, on the pathways, stairs, plinths and pavements of the Taj Mahal. Some of them are- symbolic motifs swastika, chakra, aako, a (Hexagon), Paucako,a (Pentagon), aakh (conch-shell), animated motifs - fish, bird, geometrical motifs - triangle, square, rectangle, floral motifs- leaves and petals of the flowers chiefly lotus. The Taj stands at the end of superb gardens designed in the charbagh style so popular among the Persian architects. Divided into four quadrants by waterways, they evoke the Islamic image of the Gardens of Paradise, as according to the holy Koran, a garden is symbolic of paradise where rivers flow with water, milk, wine and honey. The \"rivers\" converge at a marble tank in the centre that corresponds to al-Kawthar, the celestial pool of abundance mentioned in the Koran. Another genius of the architect is reflected in the layout of the fountains and somber rows of cypress trees, which are present only on the north-south water canal, lest the attention of the onlooker would be diverted to the sides.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/taj_mahal_01.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/taj_mahal_01.webp",
           "historical_significance": "1650-1700",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4648,7 +4648,7 @@
           "title": "Agra Fort",
           "name": "Agra Fort",
           "desc": "Built by the Mughal emperor Akbar, the Agra fort contains numerous impressive structures like the Jahangir Mahal, Khas Mahal, Diwan-i-Khass, Diwan-i-Am, Machchhi Bhawan and Moti Masjid. Imprisoned in the Agra fort by his son Aurangzeb, Shah Jahan spent his last days in the Musamman Burj of the Agra fort. The Musamman Burj is located on the left of the Khaas Mahal. It is a beautiful octagonal tower with an open pavilion. The Jahangiri Mahal was built by Akbar and is one of the few structures that survives from his reign. It is a stately building with rows of ornate pillars with elaborate hanging brackets set around an open courtyard. One of the most beautiful complexes in the fort is the Khaas Mahal constructed by Shahjahan. The central area has a marble courtyard, a rectangular pool with frilled edges and a fountain. The Diwan-e-Khas boasts of Shahjahans evolved and mature style, with cusped arches and rich lattice marble screens and veneers.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/agra-fort.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/agra-fort.webp",
           "historical_significance": "1600-1750",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4666,7 +4666,7 @@
           "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Fatehpur Sikri",
           "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Fatehpur Sikri",
           "desc": "Birbal's house stands near the northwest corner of JodhaBai's palace. It is one of the marvelous buildings of Imperial Harem. It consists of four square rooms, each are interconnected through open doorways and two oblong entrance porches. While all four rooms, have flat ceilings, porches have triangular ceiling with pyramidal roofs. Shafts of these pilasters have been divided into three zones, which contain three different kind of decoration like single border designs, arabesque geometrical designs and stylized floral designs. An extension of northern suite of Jodha Bai's palace, Hawa Mahal is composed of beautiful jali screens. Therefore, it is named as Hawa Mahal or Palace of Breeze. This kind of arrangement of screens keeps the interior cool and at the same time ensured purdah for ladies. The purpose of Viaduct passage was to allow the ladies of the imperial Harem to go on outings in purdah. This has been provided from west of the Hawa Mahal to the plain below. It was originally closed with beautiful stone screens and extended upto Hathi Pol and Sangin Burj, but now only a part of it is survived. Several Hindu motifs like hans (swan), parrot, elephants, lotus, srivatsa mark, ghantmala etc. are decorated in the interior The colonnaded dalan contains curvilinear square pillars with beautiful moldings and extremely graceful brackets. The western suite, which serves the purpose of temple, contains beautiful curvilinear pillars with brackets. This suite contains vedikas and the super structure of the building comprises of chhatris, semi-circular domes and triangular tiled roofs which bear traces of arabesque designs in glazed tiles. Panch Mahal, a five-floored pillared pavilion is an extraordinary building was called badgir or wind tower, which was constructed to alleviate the heat and is a very popular architectural mechanism of Persian architecture. The first floor has 84 pillars with each row of eight pillars arranged in north to south direction and six pillars arranged in east to west direction. The second floor has 56 pillars beautiful carved pillars with unique designs with row of six pillars in north to south direction and row of four pillars in east to west direction and also. Capitals of these pillars also contain beautiful carved designs like floral vase, arch or rosette pattern.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/buland-darwaza-f-sikri.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/buland-darwaza-f-sikri.webp",
           "historical_significance": "1550-1600",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4675,7 +4675,7 @@
           "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Ghats of Varanasi",
           "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Ghats of Varanasi",
           "desc": "With more than 100 ghats along the Ganga some of the prominent and popular Ghats at Varanasi are the Dasaswamedh Ghat, Manikarnika Ghat, Harischandra Ghat, Kabir Ghat and Assi Ghat. Dasaswamedh Ghat is one of the most important Ghats of Varanasi. Dasaswamedh literally means the river front of ten sacrificed horses where ten times the Ashwamedha Yagya has been performed by different kings. Manikarnika Ghat is the main cremation ghat of Varanasi. Manikarnika Ghat is one of the oldest and most sacred ghats in Benaras. Hindus believe that who ever is being cremated here after the death becomes free from the cycle of births. Tulsi Ghat is named after the great Hindu poet of the sixteenth century, Tulsidas, who composed the, Ramcharitmanas sitting at this ghat in Varanasi. Perhaps, to commemorate this, a temple of Lord Rama was built on the Tulsi Ghat. Many of the relics of Tulsisas are preserved at the Tulsi Ghat. The house in which Tulsidas died has been preserved and his samadhi, wooden clogs, pillow and the idol of Hanuman, which Tulsidas worshipped, are all still intact here.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/tulsidas.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/tulsidas.webp",
           "historical_significance": "1947-1080",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4684,7 +4684,7 @@
           "title": "UttarPradesh- Monument- Sarnath",
           "name": "UttarPradesh- Monument- Sarnath",
           "desc": "About 10 km from the holy city of Varanasi, is the Sarnath where Buddha delivered his first sermon after gaining enlightenment. The mantra, Buddham Sharanam Gachhami, owes its origin to Sarnath. On the day before his death Buddha included Sarnath along with Lumbini, Bodh Gaya and Kushinagar as the four places he thought to be sacred to his followers. It makes Sarnath one of the most venerated Buddhist places. Besides Buddhism, Sarnath is also connected with Jainism. Some of the important Buddhist monuments at Sarnath are the Dhamekha stupa, the Chaukhandi stupa and monasteries and temples of different schools of Buddhism from Japan, China, Thailand, Burma etc. The Indian Buddhist society called Mahabodhi Society maintains a deer park around the Buddha temple. The Mahabodhi Temple within the park has a tooth relic of the Buddha. The capital of Ashoka pillar of Sarnath is the national emblem of India. Chaukhandi Stupa is one of the important Buddhist Stupas at Sarnath. It is a lofty mound of brick, whose square edifice is surrounded by an octagonal tower. It was built to mark the place where Lord Buddha met his firstdisciples as he traveled from Bodh Gaya to Sarnath. The Dhamekh Stupa is the most remarkable and massive structure at Sarnath. Built partly in stone and partly in brick, the stone facing the lower part is decorated with delicate floral carvings of Gupta period. Dhamekh Stupa at Sarnath as it signifies the place where Buddha delivered his first sermon.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/sarnath.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/sarnath.webp",
           "historical_significance": "100-600 CE",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4693,7 +4693,7 @@
           "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Kapilvastu",
           "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Kapilvastu",
           "desc": "Piprahwa 22 km from Siddharthnagar is identified with ancient Kapilvastu where Lord Buddha spent the early years of his life. Kapilvastu was the capital of the Shakya clan whose ruler was the father of Lord Buddha, and therefore Lord Buddha is also called as Shakyamuni. Prince Gautam, as the Buddha was then known, left his palace in Kapilvastu at the age of 29, and only to come back after 12 years, after attaining enlightenment. Today, Kapilvastu comprises of several villages, mainly Piprahwa and Ganvaria. A large Stupa stands at the ancient site which is said to have housed the bone relics of Lord Buddha. The presence of these relics is testified by an ancient Brahmi inscription found at Piprahwa. The ruins of the royal palace of King Shuddhodhana, the father of Prince Gautam are spread over a large area It was at this place, where the Shakya prince Siddhartha Gautama had spent 29 years of his life before leaving home to become the Buddha.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/kapilavastu.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/kapilavastu.webp",
           "historical_significance": "",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4711,7 +4711,7 @@
           "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Kushinagar",
           "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Kushinagar",
           "desc": "Kushinagar, one of the principal centres of the Buddhist pilgrimage, is the place where Lord Buddha attained Maharparinivana  gained freedom from rebirth. The Chinese travelers Fa Hien and Hieun Tsang have also mentioned Kushinagar in their travelogues. The credit for bringing this ancient site to light goes to General A. Cunningham and A.C.L. Carlleyle who, after excavating the site in 1861, established its antiquity for the first time. Later, more",
