@@ -4785,7 +4785,7 @@
           "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Fairs & Festivals- Holi/ Janamashtami",
           "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Fairs & Festivals- Holi/ Janamashtami",
           "desc": "Holi and Janamashtami are two festivals celebrated with great fervour in Mathura. Both these festivals are associated with Lord Krishna and in Holi, festivities start by offering colours to the Lord at the temples before it is celebrated on the streets. Artists from various parts of the country gather here to perform raas leela, or divine dance recitals of Krishnas by devotees. Gayan mandalis, or singing troupes that render folk songs particularly associated with Holi, flock to this place. Janamashtami too is celebrated on the day when Krishna was born. Idols of Krishna and Radha are decorated beautifully and then worshipped.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/janamashtami.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/janamashtami.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4794,7 +4794,7 @@
           "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Fairs & Festivals- Ganga Dusshera",
           "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Fairs & Festivals- Ganga Dusshera",
           "desc": "Ganga has been considered by Hindus as a holy river for time immemorable and being worshipped. Various pilgrimages are situated along this river, which are the hubs of fairs, festivities and worship by millions of Hindus every year. Ten days in the month of Jyeshtha (June) Ganga Dussehra is celebrated at Rishikesh, Haridwar, Garhmukteshwar, Prayag and Varanasi. Devotees take a holy dip in the Ganga, worship it, collect the clay from the banks/ ghats and take it home. Thousands of lamps are floated into the Ganga after aarti at the dusk. Lucknow, the capital of Uttar Pradesh situated along the banks of the river Gomti, rose to prominence as the centre of the Nawabs of Avadh. Historically reputed as a city of culture, it is famous for its gharana of music, Kathak dance and chikankari work (shadow work embroidery) and the huge mausoleums of thenawabs, and the ruins of the Residency stand witness to one of the most remarkable episodes in the Revolt of 1857. During Akbars reign (1556-1605), Oudh, as Lucknow was then called, flourished under imperial patronage. In early eighteenth century, the Mughal Emperor Muhammad Shah, appointed Nawab Sadat Khan Burhan-ul-Mulk as the Governor of Oudh beginning the reign of the Nawabs over Oudh, which continued till the close of the eighteenth Century. It was under Asaf-ud-Daulah, the fourth Nawab that Lucknow prospered and grew into a sophisticated and picturesque city with palaces, gardens and imposing architectural monuments. The Nawabs patronage of music and arts attracted poets, artists, and musicians to Lucknow in large numbers. During these eventful years, Lucknow became one of the most celebrated centers of opulence, dance, poetry and scholarship.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/ganga-dusshehra.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/ganga-dusshehra.webp",
           "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
           "materials": ""
         }
@@ -4805,7 +4805,7 @@
           "title": "Mathura Museum",
           "name": "Mathura Museum",
           "desc": "The Mathura school of sculpture known for the Mathura style flourished here for almost 1200 Years. Starting from the Kushan period around 100 BCE and reaching its peak during the Gupta period (AD 325 to 600). The sculptures made in red sandstone were marked by sharp and beautiful features, graceful and slim body, with many folds of transparent drapery and a new style of coiffure. The collection of Mathura sculptures and terracottas are displayed in the Government Museum at Mathura. Established in 1874, the Mathura Museum is of great importance among the museums in India. Exhibiting a rich collection of sculptures belonging to the Mathura School including sculptures dating back to the second century BCE, graceful statues of Buddha and exquisitely carved female figures in addition to images of deities, excellent collection of Buddhist, Brahminical and Jain sculptures in ancient red sandstone it has earned worldwide acclaim. Perhaps the most impressive work of art displayed here is the headless life-size statue of Kanishka. The Mathura school of art adopted the traditions of the early Indian art of Bharhut and Sanchi . Starting with the earliest terracotta figures of the mother goddess, the free use of elaborate motifs in hairstyles and drapes, and the fusion of the old Indian -cults as the worship of yakshas are all important features of this school of art.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/mathura-museum-1.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/mathura-museum-1.webp",
           "historical_significance": "1850-1900",
           "materials": ""
         },
@@ -4823,7 +4823,7 @@
           "title": "Sarnath Museum Buddha",
           "name": "Sarnath Museum Buddha",
           "desc": "The Ashoka Lion capital or the Sarnath lion capital is the national symbol of India. The Sarnath pillar bears one of the Edicts of Ashoka, an inscription against division within the Buddhist community, which reads, \"No one shall cause division in the order of monks\". The Sarnath pillar is a column surmounted by a capital, a canopy representing an inverted bell-shaped lotus flower, which consists of a short cylindrical abacus with four 24-spoked Dharma wheels with four animals (an elephant, a bull, a horse, a lion). The four animals in the Sarnath capital are believed to symbolize different steps of Lord Buddha's life. The Elephant represents the Buddha's idea in reference to the dream of Queen Maya of a white elephant entering her womb. The Bull represents desire during the life of the Buddha as a prince. The Horse represents Buddha's departure from palatial life. The Lion represents the accomplishment of Buddha hood. Besides the religious interpretations, there are some non-religious interpretations also about the symbolism of the Ashoka capital pillar at Sarnath. According to them, the four lions symbolize Ashoka's rule over the four directions, the wheels as symbols of his enlightened rule (Chakravartin) and the four animals as symbols of four adjoining territories of India.",
-          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/buddha_in_sarnath_museum.jpg",
+          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/buddha_in_sarnath_museum.webp",
           "historical_significance": "600-100 BCE",
           "materials": ""
         }
