@@ -5099,7 +5099,7 @@
           "title": "Terracotta Temples, Bishnupur",
           "name": "Terracotta Temples, Bishnupur",
           "desc": "Bishnupur boasts of thirty temples built between seventeenth and eighteenth century. It is a treasure-trove of terracotta sculptures. The temples are built mostly of brick and at times, of laterite. The architecture is modeled on the pattern of Bengali huts built of bamboo and mud and thatched roofed. For almost a thousand years it was the capital of the Malla kings of Mallabhum, of which Bankura was a part. The temples are dedicated to Radha and Krishna. Here, royal architects and craftsmen of Bengal have poured all their talents into the red laterite soil found here, and the result was one of the most beautiful cities in the world of its time built of terracotta. Apart from the temples, terracotta pottery, artifacts and even jewellery from Vishnupur is famous. The potters here derive their inspiration from the glorious history of kings, soldiers and wars. Rasmancha is the oldest brick temple having a unique style with a cubic shrine, pillared corridors on three sides and a pyramidal roof. The temples built after this Pancharatna Temple , the Radha Shyama Temple and the Keshta Raya Temple are the best examples of terracotta architecture. The temple built of bricks has an exterior and interior clad with high quality, densely sculpted, terracotta panels. It is a Pancharatna with a main tower and four small towers in the four corners. The temple has a square plan with porches on all four sides and a three-arch entrance, on each of the four sides. The ceiling is is in the form of a dome with a terracotta finish having Islamic influence with a terracotta finish that is not seen in any other temple. Most of the sculptures are of Krishna. There are Rasa mandalas with Krishna, Radha and gopis. Jorebangla Temple has two traditional Bengali huts placed together and the sloping split roof called Bangaldar roof. Madanmohan temple built in 1694 CE in the Ekaratna style, a square flat-roofed building with carved cornices, surmounted by a pinnacle. Impressive carvings on the walls depict scenes from the Ramayana, Mahabharata and the Puranas. The temples at Bishnupur are mostly of Ekaratna type with a single tower upon a sloping roof and a square cell (Garbhagriha) flanked by a porch on each side with three cusped arches.",
